const BLOCKS := {
	"chapter/1": [
		[1, "Synopsis", "The campaign opens at sea. The pilot, a newcomer to the ocean planet who has signed on as a fisherman for the colony, is out in a small submarine with [[character/1]], the ship's talkative on-board computer. This chapter teaches harpoon fishing: line up a creature, fire the harpoon, then keep the target in view while it struggles until it tires and can be reeled in. See [[guide/fishing]].", false],
		[2, "Outcome", "With the first creature landed, M.A.I. introduces the autopilot, which can steer the ship to a chosen target, and points the pilot towards [[station/0]] to sell the catch. See [[guide/navigation]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/10": [
		[10, "Synopsis", "At [[station/2]] the capsule launch is already under way and the Aquarians are attacking. [[character/9]] radios a blunt warning: losing more than five capsules is not acceptable. The pilot must fend off the attackers and keep the capsules intact.", false],
		[11, "Outcome", "The attack is beaten off, but the pilot is left wondering why the Aquarians target the capsules at all. [[character/1]] suggests they may be far more intelligent than the colonists assume. The pilot heads to the station to report, increasingly unsure about this line of work.", false],
	],
	"chapter/11": [
		[11, "Synopsis", "The pilot docks at [[station/2]] to report to [[character/9]] after the capsule defence.", false],
		[12, "Outcome", "Jack blames the pilot for the damaged capsules, cancels the reward and leaves with a remark about being destined for greater things. [[character/1]] then raises a bigger question: the pilot works hard for the colony, earns little and has no say over the catch. The pilot only wants enough money for a ticket off the planet. M.A.I. reveals that it has picked up radio traffic from a resistance movement based at [[station/3]] and marks the station on the map. From now on, resistance stations appear in green.", false],
	],
	"chapter/12": [
		[12, "Synopsis", "The pilot travels to [[station/3]] to find the resistance. A quiet approach fails at once: the station's guards challenge the ship and threaten to open fire. The pilot gives a name, says the trip started at [[station/0]] and asks to join. After a tense silence the ship is cleared to dock.", false],
		[13, "Outcome", "Inside, [[character/4]] questions the newcomer. The pilot knows almost nothing beyond the movement's support for free trade, which fits with Pierre's explanation that their radio cipher has only just been switched on. Short of help, Pierre accepts the pilot on trial. The movement plans to win over the leaders of colonist stations and end the exploitation of the planet. The first task is to collect sympathisers at [[station/4]] and bring them to [[station/5]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/13": [
		[13, "Synopsis", "The pilot reaches [[station/4]] to collect the movement's contact, still with no clear idea of what the resistance is or what the job involves. [[character/1]] points out that this is at least more interesting than taking orders from [[character/9]], and the pilot agrees.", false],
		[14, "Outcome", "The contact is [[character/5]], warm, quick and clearly pleased to meet the pilot. She keeps the movement's plans to herself, saying that secrecy is how they have survived so far, and sends the coordinates for the trip. [[character/1]] watches the two of them with open irritation, which the pilot teases as jealousy.", false],
	],
	"chapter/14": [
		[14, "Synopsis", "With [[character/5]] aboard, the pilot carries her to [[station/5]] as quickly and safely as possible.", false],
		[15, "Outcome", "At [[station/5]], Leah goes into hiding to settle some affairs. Before she leaves she explains how the rebels work: they take only what the sea can spare, process their catch into goods and take care not to overfish. She asks the pilot to try it and produce five tons of goods in the factory of a resistance station, suggesting [[station/90]], where squid can be caught and [[goods/35]] bought to make [[goods/22]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/15": [
		[15, "Synopsis", "A chapter about production. The pilot must produce five tons of goods in the factory of a rebel station, combining catch and bought ingredients into products worth more than the raw material. See [[guide/trading]].", false],
		[16, "Outcome", "[[character/5]] calls back. Now that the pilot has a second way to earn money, she has a new task: visit as many unknown stations as possible. The movement uses fresh reports from every station to find weak points and bring the population over to its side. When the pilot asks whether this is a revolution, she declines to say more and advises earning money for ship upgrades in the meantime.", false],
	],
	"chapter/16": [
		[16, "Synopsis", "The pilot must discover five stations not visited before. Exploring widens the trading network and gathers information for the movement. Some stations may lie beyond the ship's current depth range. See [[guide/depth]].", false],
		[17, "Outcome", "[[character/4]] sends a lengthy message, which [[character/1]] condenses to its single point: the pilot should return to [[station/3]] for a briefing.", false],
	],
	"chapter/17": [
		[17, "Synopsis", "The pilot returns to [[station/3]] at [[character/4]]'s request and is waved into dock.", false],
		[18, "Outcome", "Pierre reports that diplomacy has enlarged the rebel zone, so the pilot can now trade at many more stations. The news already speaks of a revolution, and many decision makers have joined without bloodshed, though Pierre doubts it will stay that way. The movement's leader directs the next steps from one of the newly won stations and will make contact. Pierre marks the candidates and warns that reaching some of them may require better armour or shields.", false],
	],
	"chapter/18": [
		[18, "Synopsis", "The pilot must track down the leader of the movement among the newly won stations, with little to go on but luck. The trail leads to [[station/6]].", false],
		[19, "Outcome", "At [[station/6]] the pilot meets [[character/2]], who leads the movement and will issue orders from now on. The pilot had expected a man, which she brushes aside. Her first assignment is an escort: a swarm of [[species/10|octopuses]] must reach safe waters before colonists capture or kill them, and a fight is likely. [[character/1]] grumbles that nobody has mentioned payment.", false],
	],
	"chapter/19": [
		[19, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/7]] the pilot must protect a swarm of [[species/10]] from colonist ships while taking care not to hit the animals. Aquarians are swimming alongside the swarm, and [[character/1]] wonders whether they look after the sea's creatures.", false],
		[20, "Outcome", "Colonist ships close in on the animals, and the pilot has to intercept them before they get too near. The swarm survives. [[character/2]] pays a modest reward and asks the pilot to return at once for a new assignment. M.A.I. complains that catching and selling the animals would have paid better.", false],
	],
	"chapter/2": [
		[2, "Synopsis", "With fish in the hold, the pilot heads for [[station/0]], the colonist station that buys the day's catch. The chapter is a simple journey and a first chance to dock and look around a station.", false],
		[3, "Outcome", "At [[station/0]] the pilot meets [[character/9]], who runs the catch purchases. He pays his usual fixed price, mocks the size of the pilot's cargo hold and suggests that someone with a real gun could earn more hunting pirates. Once he has gone, M.A.I. agrees he is unpleasant but takes the hint: the [[equipment/0]] happens to be on offer in the hangar. M.A.I. also explains that help is available on every station screen.", false],
	],
	"chapter/20": [
		[20, "Synopsis", "The pilot returns to [[station/6]] to report to [[character/2]].", false],
		[21, "Outcome", "The pilot finally asks Ayumi what the movement stands for. She describes a group united against the ruin of the planet: rapid colonisation is stripping its resources faster than nature can recover, as has already happened on other worlds, and the aim is to persuade influential people to resist. Meanwhile [[faction/pirates]] are exploiting the conflict. Ayumi decides to strike first, sending the pilot against a pirate formation alongside [[character/3]], one of her best pilots.", false],
	],
	"chapter/21": [
		[21, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/8]] the pilot joins [[character/3]] to wipe out a pirate gang. Raymond is impatient and condescending from the first moment and tells the newcomer to stay behind him and learn.", false],
		[22, "Outcome", "The gang is destroyed. Raymond offers grudging praise, and [[character/2]] pays each of them the same reward and asks the pilot to meet her at [[station/9]]. Raymond is furious that the money is split equally and dismisses the pilot's contribution, and the pilot leaves before his temper gets worse.", false],
	],
	"chapter/22": [
		[22, "Synopsis", "The pilot travels to [[station/9]] to meet [[character/2]], whose talks there have just ended. Several stations, [[station/9]] among them, now stand with the movement.", false],
		[23, "Outcome", "Before leaving, Ayumi wants the supply route to [[station/37]] secured. [[character/9]] has laid a minefield just outside the station to cut it. Ayumi knows him: once a minor figure, he has risen fast thanks to useful connections. She warns that pirates are likely to be circling the area and that the mines can only be destroyed after they have been triggered.", false],
	],
	"chapter/23": [
		[23, "Synopsis", "Outside [[station/9]] the pilot must clear the minefield blocking the route to [[station/37]]. Ayumi marks the mines on the map. Each mine has to be approached to set off its motion sensor, and only then can it be shot. Pirates lurk nearby, waiting for easy prey, so the pilot must deal with them too. See [[contract/10]].", false],
		[24, "Outcome", "The minefield is cleared and the route to [[station/37]] is safe again. [[character/2]] is pleased that her trust was justified and pays the pilot for the work.", false],
	],
	"chapter/24": [
		[24, "Synopsis", "The pilot returns to [[station/9]] after clearing the mines. With the supply route to [[station/37]] open again, a large group of stations across the ocean now trades with the movement, and the rebel zone on the map grows considerably.", false],
		[25, "Outcome", "At [[station/9]] the pilot is reunited with [[character/5]], and the conversation is warm but clumsy. [[character/2]] interrupts with the next job: more negotiations at [[station/10]] could bring further stations over. The pilot and Leah are to escort Ayumi and [[character/3]] there. The pilot is unenthusiastic about going out with Raymond, but Ayumi vouches for his skill and expects nothing worse than pirates.", false],
	],
	"chapter/25": [
		[25, "Synopsis", "The escort arrives at [[station/10]] and finds that something is wrong. [[character/9]] is waiting with colonist forces and orders them to attack. Ayumi cannot understand how they knew she was coming. The pilot must hold off the attackers and keep [[character/2]] alive.", false],
		[26, "Outcome", "During the fight [[character/3]] claims his weapons have failed and flees. The rest survive. [[character/5]] accuses him of betrayal, while Ayumi still trusts him and suspects sabotage. Before more colonists arrive, the group scatters: Ayumi goes into hiding, Leah keeps things running on [[station/9]], and the pilot is told to become a much more capable fighter before they meet again.", false],
	],
	"chapter/26": [
		[26, "Synopsis", "With [[character/2]] in hiding and [[character/3]] gone, the pilot must destroy ten more enemy ships to prove readiness for the harder battles ahead. Pirates make convenient targets, and combat contracts on the job boards offer more. It is a good moment to upgrade weapons and armour. See [[guide/combat]].", false],
		[27, "Outcome", "[[character/3]] calls unexpectedly, asks how Ayumi and Leah are, and claims to have urgent information. He asks to meet near [[station/11]]. [[character/1]] is certain it is a trap. The pilot agrees, and goes anyway.", false],
	],
	"chapter/27": [
		[27, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/11]] the pilot follows the waypoints to the meeting with [[character/3]], fully expecting a trap. The objective, to salvage a disabled ship, hints that the encounter will not end in conversation.", false],
		[28, "Outcome", "Raymond is holding [[character/2]] prisoner and demands surrender. While [[character/1]] scans his ship, the pilot keeps him talking. Raymond admits he changed sides for money: [[character/9]] has put a bounty on both Ayumi and the pilot. The scan shows a single-seat ship, so Ayumi must be in the armoured cargo bay. The pilot destroys Raymond's ship and recovers the container before it sinks, then makes for [[station/12]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/28": [
		[28, "Synopsis", "The pilot carries the recovered container to the safety of [[station/12]], the nearest friendly station, hoping that [[character/1]]'s guess about its contents is right.", false],
		[29, "Outcome", "[[character/2]] emerges shaken but unhurt and thanks the pilot for saving her life. There is no time to rest. Before her capture she was organising the transport of a team of highly skilled scientists to [[station/14]], and Raymond may already have passed on the details. The pilot must see the convoy safely across the border zone.", false],
	],
	"chapter/29": [
		[29, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/13]] the pilot finds the scientists' convoy under heavy attack, just in time to help. The friendly ships call for support as their defences fail. The pilot must drive off the attackers and escort the convoy across the border zone. Every ship lost puts the movement's secret project at risk.", false],
		[30, "Outcome", "The convoy gets through, its crew grateful for the rescue. [[character/2]] calls it a close call. [[character/2]] pays the pilot and asks for a meeting at [[station/14]], where she will explain what comes next.", false],
	],
	"chapter/3": [
		[3, "Synopsis", "A short tutorial chapter about fitting. The pilot visits the hangar on [[station/0]] to buy and install the [[equipment/0]], the ship's first proper weapon. It shows how equipment is bought and mounted in a free slot. See [[guide/fitting]].", false],
		[4, "Outcome", "With the railgun mounted, M.A.I. reminds the pilot not to fire on harmless sea life and suggests a test run against pirates straight away.", false],
	],
	"chapter/30": [
		[30, "Synopsis", "The pilot travels to [[station/14]], where the scientists' convoy has arrived.", false],
		[31, "Outcome", "[[character/5]], [[character/4]] and [[character/2]] are waiting. Ayumi explains the plan: the colonists still outnumber the rebels, so Professor Abel and his team will build a weapon to even the odds, in three steps. The first is a power source, the Biotek-generator. Abel's prototype was stolen by the colonists, who are trying to finish it themselves. Leah adds that the generator is travelling with an ordinary colonist convoy near [[station/2]]. As nobody knows which ship carries it, the pilot and Pierre must stop the whole convoy and recover every container.", false],
	],
	"chapter/31": [
		[31, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/2]] the pilot, [[character/5]] and [[character/4]] intercept the colonist convoy carrying the stolen Biotek-generator. Nobody knows which ship holds the generator, so every vessel in the convoy must be stopped. The convoy crews protest that they carry only food and medicine, and [[character/4]] answers with a dark joke. The pilot must disable the ships and recover every container with the harpoon before the cargo sinks.", false],
		[32, "Outcome", "Every container is recovered, the generator among them, and the team returns to [[station/14]] to hand the haul over to Professor Abel.", false],
	],
	"chapter/32": [
		[32, "Synopsis", "The pilot returns to [[station/14]] with the recovered cargo.", false],
		[33, "Outcome", "Professor Abel takes the haul to his laboratory. [[character/2]] sends [[character/5]] to patrol outside [[station/14]] and [[character/4]] to the hangar, and gives the pilot the second step. The generator needs a weapon worthy of its output: the [[equipment/42]], a station defence gun so power-hungry it has never been fitted to a ship. The nearest factory, [[station/188]], is in colonist hands, so the pilot should try [[station/15]]. The gun is not for sale, so Ayumi hands over a large sum for bribes.", false],
	],
	"chapter/33": [
		[33, "Synopsis", "The second step of [[character/2]]'s plan begins. The pilot sets out for [[station/15]], a rebel station, to obtain an [[equipment/42]]. The gun is normally made at the colonist station [[station/188]] and is not sold openly anywhere. The pilot carries a large bribe fund from Ayumi but has no real plan, and hopes an opportunity will present itself on arrival.", false],
		[34, "Outcome", "The pilot arrives at [[station/15]]. With no contacts and no plan, the only option is to look around the station and see what turns up.", false],
	],
	"chapter/34": [
		[34, "Synopsis", "At [[station/15]] the trail starts with the local trader's stock. The objective is to obtain a cargo of [[goods/37]], an illegal good on this station, which draws the right kind of attention.", false],
		[35, "Outcome", "A black-market dealer, [[character/8]], approaches and offers to supply almost anything. When the pilot asks for an [[equipment/42]], he is astonished, but agrees to try in return for a favour: eliminate [[character/11]], a bounty hunter paid by the security authorities to get rid of Fenko, who is patrolling near the station.", false],
	],
	"chapter/35": [
		[35, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/15]] the pilot hunts down [[character/11]], the bounty hunter that [[character/8]] wants removed. Raoul is not alone: he has brought escorts and tries to brush the pilot aside as a mere fisherman. The pilot must destroy the wanted ship. See [[contract/0]].", false],
		[36, "Outcome", "Raoul's ship is destroyed after a last attempt to talk his way out, and his escorts are driven off or sunk. The pilot returns to see whether [[character/8]] will keep his word.", false],
	],
	"chapter/36": [
		[36, "Synopsis", "The pilot docks at [[station/15]] to collect on [[character/8]]'s promise, unsure whether a black-market dealer can be trusted to deliver.", false],
		[37, "Outcome", "Fenko has found an [[equipment/42]] and will sell it, at a price swollen by the bribes he paid. When the pilot asks where it came from, Fenko names the seller: [[character/9]]. [[character/1]] enjoys the irony of Jack's greed arming his own enemies. The pilot decides to buy the gun and leave before anything changes.", false],
	],
	"chapter/37": [
		[37, "Synopsis", "[[character/8]] now offers the [[goods/38|Eclipse]] for sale at [[station/15]]. The pilot must buy it from the trade screen and get it aboard. The price is high, but Ayumi's bribe fund was meant for exactly this, and the hold must have room for the large cannon. See [[guide/trading]].", false],
		[38, "Outcome", "The cannon is in the hold, which [[character/1]] can hardly believe. Step two is complete. The pilot heads back to [[station/14]] to see how the prototype generator is coming along.", false],
	],
	"chapter/38": [
		[38, "Synopsis", "The pilot returns to [[station/14]] with the [[equipment/42]] in the hold, keen to see how far Professor Abel has come with the prototype.", false],
		[39, "Outcome", "[[character/2]] prefers not to know how the gun was obtained. The prototype is nearly finished, but one part cannot be made at [[station/14]]: a [[goods/36]], a device that multiplies the DNA of algae particles to start the generator's power reaction. Only [[station/16]] has the equipment to make it, so the third step sends the pilot there. [[character/1]] remarks that the pilot seems to be running the rebellion single-handed.", false],
	],
	"chapter/39": [
		[39, "Synopsis", "The third step begins. The pilot travels to [[station/16]], the only station with the equipment to make the [[goods/36]] that the Biotek-generator needs. [[station/16]] lies deeper than most stations, so the ship may need stronger armour before it can reach the station safely. See [[guide/depth]].", false],
		[40, "Outcome", "The pilot docks at [[station/16]] and starts working out what the incubator requires, expecting the job to be anything but simple.", false],
	],
	"chapter/4": [
		[4, "Synopsis", "A tutorial chapter about leaving a station. The pilot departs [[station/0]] looking for pirates, but the local waters are quiet, so M.A.I. suggests travelling on to another station.", false],
		[5, "Outcome", "M.A.I. explains the S.T.R.E.A.M.S, the current network used for long journeys: a ship always leaves a sector through a stream exit and arrives at the next one through a stream entry, and the autopilot can handle the trip. See [[guide/navigation]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/40": [
		[40, "Synopsis", "At [[station/16]] the pilot must produce a [[goods/36]] in the station factory. The incubator is an advanced product, and its recipe calls for several ingredients that may have to be caught, bought or made elsewhere first. [[character/1]] expects it to be complicated, and so does the pilot. See [[guide/trading]].", false],
		[41, "Outcome", "The incubator is finished. M.A.I. admits it had doubted the pilot could manage it. Still uneasy about whether all this is the right thing to do, the pilot sets course for [[station/14]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/41": [
		[41, "Synopsis", "The pilot must bring the [[goods/36]] safely to [[station/14]], where Professor Abel waits to finish the prototype generator. It is the last missing piece of [[character/2]]'s three-step plan.", false],
		[42, "Outcome", "[[character/5]] receives the incubator. Professor Abel has everything ready, and the [[equipment/42]] should soon be available in the hangar. Ayumi wants the gun tested at once against pirates, and Leah passes on the coordinates with a warning that the generator is only a prototype. [[character/1]], supposedly switched off, makes it clear that it heard every word.", false],
	],
	"chapter/42": [
		[42, "Synopsis", "With the [[equipment/42]] fitted in the hangar at [[station/14]], the pilot heads for a pirate hideout near [[station/17]] to test it in combat. The generator is only a prototype, so the test is also a check that it holds up under load. The pirates call for reinforcements once they see what they are facing, and more ships arrive mid-fight.", false],
		[43, "Outcome", "As the fight ends, [[character/1]] picks up a distress call from [[station/14]], sent openly on an unciphered channel. The pilot turns back at once.", false],
	],
	"chapter/43": [
		[43, "Synopsis", "Colonist forces have launched a surprise attack on [[station/14]]. Many docks are damaged and the rebels cannot launch enough ships. [[character/9]] leads the assault and taunts the pilot, until he hears that the [[equipment/42]] is ready to fire. The pilot must defend the station and its friendly ships.", false],
		[44, "Outcome", "In the middle of the battle the Aquarians arrive and turn on the colonists, fighting on the rebels' side. Jack deserts when the attack collapses. [[character/2]] calls the rescue a miracle and asks the pilot to dock, mentioning a special guest.", false],
	],
	"chapter/44": [
		[44, "Synopsis", "The pilot docks at [[station/14]] after the battle.", false],
		[45, "Outcome", "The special guest is an Aquarian delegate. The Aquarians will help to end colonist rule, so that everyone can live in harmony with the sea. [[character/2]] plans two major offensives to take the remaining stations. With [[station/188]] now in rebel hands, as many ships as possible are being armed with the [[equipment/42]]. The pilot will lead the attack on [[station/18]], with fresh units and Aquarian support, while [[character/5]] leads the assault on [[station/109]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/45": [
		[45, "Synopsis", "At [[station/18]] the pilot meets [[character/12]], leader of the Atmos squad, whose ships wait for the order to attack. Aquarians rise from the depths to join in. Every colonist ship must be destroyed, freighters included, before the station can be taken. Meanwhile [[character/5]] leads a parallel assault on [[station/109]].", false],
		[46, "Outcome", "The colonist defenders and their freighters are wiped out, and the combined rebel and Aquarian force holds the waters around the station. The pilot sends half of Colin's squad to patrol around the station and takes the rest in to dock.", false],
	],
	"chapter/46": [
		[46, "Synopsis", "The pilot docks at [[station/18]] with part of [[character/12]]'s squad to secure the captured station, while the rest patrols outside.", false],
		[47, "Outcome", "[[character/5]] reports that her attack on [[station/109]] has also succeeded, and the southern stations are now under rebel control. Ayumi has given the pilot what may be the final task: [[station/0]], the colonists' last great stronghold and the station where the pilot's story began. It is heavily fortified, and [[character/2]] is already on her way. Leah follows, but pirates are blocking her route.", false],
	],
	"chapter/47": [
		[47, "Synopsis", "At [[station/0]] the pilot joins [[character/2]] and [[character/5]] for the final battle. Every defending ship must be destroyed before the station can be taken. [[character/9]] is among the defenders. When the pilot recalls [[character/3]]'s fate, Jack tries to rattle the pilot with a wild story about being the pilot's father and Raymond the pilot's brother. The pilot sees through it at once.", false],
		[48, "Outcome", "The colonist fleet falls until only Jack is left. He proves fast and dangerous, but the pilot finally disables his ship. Jack escapes upwards in a rescue capsule that seems to take the whole station with it, scorning the planet as he leaves. The battle is over.", false],
	],
	"chapter/48": [
		[48, "Synopsis", "The war is won. The colonist fleet is beaten, [[character/9]] has fled the planet, and [[station/0]] and the last holdouts join the rebel cause. Every station now stands with the movement.", false],
		[48, "", "The victors take stock. [[character/2]] thanks the pilot and [[character/5]] and admits that it will take a long time for the planet's wounds to heal, but she believes they can manage that as well. Leah invites the pilot to celebrate over [[goods/21]], and the pilot accepts. [[character/1]], promised a memory upgrade once the pilot is back on dry land, insists it needs no such thing and keeps teasing the couple to the end.", false],
		[48, "", "The campaign is complete. The pilot can keep exploring, trading and taking contracts across a world now at peace with the rebels.", false],
	],
	"chapter/5": [
		[5, "Synopsis", "The pilot's first fight takes place near [[station/1]]. M.A.I. explains how to switch between weapons and harpoon, how autofire helps, and how to read the radar: red marks are hostile, green marks are friendly and yellow marks are waypoints. A lone [[faction/pirates|pirate]] soon appears on the scope. See [[guide/combat]].", false],
		[6, "Outcome", "The pirate is destroyed. M.A.I. points out that wrecks leave cargo crates behind, which the harpoon can recover before they sink out of reach, and then sets course back to [[station/0]].", false],
	],
	"chapter/6": [
		[6, "Synopsis", "The pilot returns to [[station/0]] after the first successful fight, with a working weapon and perhaps some salvaged cargo to sell. The trip is a short journey back through the stream network.", false],
		[7, "Outcome", "[[character/9]] notices the new weapon and offers what he calls real work. [[species/0|Nautiluses]] are crowding the convoy routes around [[station/1]], and the station's security wants them gone. The pilot points out that the creatures simply feed on growth on the station hulls and that the hold is too small to carry them all. Jack dismisses both objections: the animals must disappear, by whatever method.", false],
	],
	"chapter/7": [
		[7, "Synopsis", "Near [[station/1]] the pilot must clear away the [[species/0|nautiluses]]. Killing them or catching them both count. Neither the pilot nor [[character/1]] is comfortable with the job, since the creatures are scarce and harmless, but they agree to get it done.", false],
		[8, "Outcome", "The work leaves the pilot shaken. The pilot came to the planet to fish, not to slaughter animals for the convenience of the colony. M.A.I. hopes that the pay will at least make up for it and suggests docking at [[station/1]] to collect.", false],
	],
	"chapter/8": [
		[8, "Synopsis", "The pilot docks at [[station/1]] to report on the nautilus hunt.", false],
		[9, "Outcome", "A colonist official receives the pilot with thinly veiled contempt, treats the job as pest control and admits it is probably pointless because the creatures keep coming back. The payment offered is small and not open to negotiation. Before any further work, the official wants proof of ability: three completed jobs from the station job boards. [[character/1]] explains how to find contracts, warns that some job sites lie beyond the ship's depth limits, and shows how to plan routes from the station map.", false],
	],
	"chapter/9": [
		[9, "Synopsis", "A chapter about freelance work. The pilot must complete three contracts from any station job board. Contracts vary from escorts and salvage to fishing orders, and it pays to look at several stations. See [[guide/contracts]].", false],
		[10, "Outcome", "Satisfied, the colonist official offers a special assignment. Strange beings that the colonists call Aquarians, part human and part fish, have been attacking the transport capsules that lift the colony's goods into orbit for collection. Damaged or missing capsules reflect badly on the station with the trade authorities. The next launch is at [[station/2]], where [[character/9]] will lead the operation, and the pilot is to drive the Aquarians off and report to him.", false],
	],
	"character/0": [
		[1, "", "The pilot is the player character: a newcomer to a colonised ocean world who pilots a small submarine and earns a living as a fisherman. The player chooses the pilot's name, and the short chapter summaries in the journal are written as the pilot's own notes. The pilot is never alone at sea, because the ship's on-board computer, [[character/1]], comments on almost every move. The story begins at [[station/0]], where the pilot sells the catch to the colony.", false],
		[1, "Background", "Little is told about the pilot's life before the campaign. The pilot came to the planet to fish and regrets the move from the very first mission, grumbling at having to take orders from a ship's computer. Everything else has to be pieced together from what the pilot says and does.", false],
		[7, "", "The pilot knows the sea and its creatures well. When the colony complains about [[species/0|nautiluses]] crowding the convoy routes, the pilot points out that the animals graze on the algae and other growth that cover the station hulls, so their presence is hardly surprising.", false],
		[12, "", "At first the pilot's goals are modest. The pilot wants to earn enough for a ticket off the planet, and the flat prices paid for the catch keep that goal out of reach. When the pilot later asks to join the resistance, the pilot gives [[station/0]] as home.", false],
		[1, "Fisherman for the colony", "The story opens with the pilot filling the cargo hold and setting course for [[station/0]] ([[chapter/1]], [[chapter/2]]).", false],
		[3, "", "There [[character/9]] pays a flat rate for the catch, sneers at the small hold and suggests the pilot try hunting [[faction/pirates|pirates]] instead.", false],
		[6, "", "The pilot fits a [[equipment/0]] and downs a first pirate near [[station/1]] ([[chapter/3]] to [[chapter/5]]).", false],
		[7, "", "[[character/9]] then hands over a less pleasant job: clearing the [[species/0|nautiluses]] from the routes around [[station/1]] ([[chapter/7]]).", false],
		[8, "", "The cull leaves the pilot sickened. The pilot came to fish, not to slaughter harmless animals.", false],
		[10, "", "The colonial official at [[station/1]] pays little, demands three contracts as proof of reliability ([[chapter/9]]) and then sends the pilot to defend transport capsules from the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] at [[station/2]] ([[chapter/10]]).", false],
		[12, "", "The pilot wins the fight but starts to wonder why the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] attack at all. When [[character/9]] blames the pilot for the damaged capsules and withholds the reward, [[character/1]] reveals that she has overheard a resistance movement at [[station/3]], and the pilot decides to find it ([[chapter/12]]).", false],
		[12, "Joining the resistance", "At [[station/3]] the rebel guards threaten to open fire, and the pilot has to stammer out a name and a wish to join before being allowed to dock.", false],
		[13, "", "[[character/4]] accepts the pilot on trial, mainly because the movement needs every pair of hands, and sends the pilot to collect a contact from [[station/4]] ([[chapter/13]]).", false],
		[14, "", "The contact is [[character/5]], and the pilot is visibly charmed while carrying her to [[station/5]] ([[chapter/14]]).", false],
		[16, "", "[[character/5]] shows the pilot the rebel way of living from the sea ([[chapter/15]]) and then asks the pilot to scout new stations for the movement ([[chapter/16]]). It is the first time the pilot hears the word revolution.", false],
		[18, "", "Back at [[station/3]], [[character/4]] reports the movement's diplomatic gains and sends the pilot to find its leader, with almost no clues ([[chapter/18]]).", false],
		[19, "Serving the leader", "The leader is [[character/2]], who meets the pilot at [[station/6]]; the pilot had expected a man. Her first order is to protect a swarm of [[species/10|colorsquids]] from colonist hunters ([[chapter/19]]).", false],
		[21, "", "When she finally explains the movement's aims, the pilot commits to the cause. She sends the pilot with her ace [[character/3]] against a pirate gang ([[chapter/21]]).", false],
		[23, "", "[[character/3]] treats the pilot as a nuisance. At [[station/9]] the pilot learns that [[character/9]] is now an enemy of the movement and eagerly clears the minefield he has laid across a rebel supply route ([[chapter/23]]).", false],
		[25, "", "Reunited with [[character/5]], the pilot is assigned to escort [[character/2]] and [[character/3]] to talks at [[station/10]] ([[chapter/25]]).", false],
		[26, "", "[[character/9]]'s forces are waiting. [[character/3]] flees, claiming his weapons have failed, and the pilot and [[character/5]] protect [[character/2]] alone. [[character/2]] then goes into hiding and tells the pilot to become a far better fighter ([[chapter/26]]).", false],
		[27, "Betrayal and rescue", "[[character/3]] later calls with an offer of urgent information. The pilot suspects a trap but agrees to meet him near [[station/11]] ([[chapter/27]]).", false],
		[28, "", "It is a trap: [[character/3]] holds [[character/2]] prisoner and demands surrender. Following [[character/1]]'s plan, the pilot keeps him talking while she scans his ship, telling him the fight is about conviction, not money. The pilot then destroys his ship and recovers its armoured cargo bay, with [[character/2]] alive inside, before it sinks too deep ([[chapter/28]]).", false],
		[31, "The three-step plan", "After protecting a convoy of scientists ([[chapter/29]]), the pilot learns [[character/2]]'s plan to build a weapon with [[world/abel]]. With [[character/4]] and [[character/5]], the pilot seizes the [[world/biotek]] from a colonist convoy ([[chapter/31]]).", false],
		[33, "", "Next the pilot must obtain an [[equipment/42]] cannon that is not for sale, armed only with a bribe fund. The pilot notes how little planning lies behind the task but accepts it gladly ([[chapter/33]]).", false],
		[37, "", "At [[station/15]] the black-market dealer [[character/8]] agrees to find one if the pilot kills the bounty hunter [[character/11]] ([[chapter/35]]). The pilot does, and buys a cannon that [[character/8]] obtained from [[character/9]] ([[chapter/37]]).", false],
		[39, "", "[[character/2]] would rather not know how the cannon was found, and the pilot says only that no innocent people died. When the pilot jokes that the plan could be used to rule the planet, she rejects the idea firmly ([[chapter/39]]).", false],
		[42, "", "The pilot then produces the [[goods/36]] at [[station/16]] and brings it to [[station/14]], completing the weapon ([[chapter/41]]).", false],
		[43, "The road to victory", "While testing the finished weapon on pirates near [[station/17]], the pilot hears a distress call from [[station/14]] and returns to find [[character/9]] leading a surprise attack ([[chapter/43]]).", false],
		[44, "", "With help from the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] the attack is broken and [[character/9]] flees.", false],
		[46, "", "[[character/2]] gives the pilot command of the assault on [[station/18]] ([[chapter/45]]). The pilot's first worry is for [[character/5]], who leads a second attack, but the pilot takes the station with [[character/12]]'s squad and [[world/aquarians|Aquarian]] support.", false],
		[47, "", "The final battle takes place at [[station/0]], where the story began ([[chapter/47]]). [[character/9]] tries to unsettle the pilot by claiming to be the pilot's father, and [[character/3]] the pilot's brother, but the pilot dismisses the lie at once.", false],
		[48, "", "[[character/9]] is driven out and the colony's last stronghold falls. The pilot gives the credit to the friends who fought alongside and accepts [[character/5]]'s invitation to celebrate.", false],
		[1, "Personality", "The pilot is practical and quick-tongued, more inclined to answer back than to obey. The pilot's banter with [[character/1]] runs through the whole campaign.", false],
		[8, "", "Beneath the jokes the pilot has a conscience. The pilot dislikes killing animals for profit and feels sick after the nautilus cull.", false],
		[14, "", "Around [[character/5]] the pilot becomes tongue-tied and clumsy, which both she and [[character/1]] notice.", false],
		[28, "", "The pilot's motives change over the campaign. By the time of the confrontation with [[character/3]], the pilot argues that the struggle is about ideals and that greed has blinded him.", false],
		[1, "Relationships", "[[character/1]] is the pilot's closest companion. The two bicker constantly, but the pilot relies on her advice and, later, on her cunning.", false],
		[3, "", "[[character/9]] is the pilot's first employer and becomes the pilot's lasting rival.", false],
		[14, "", "The pilot is drawn to [[character/5]] from their first meeting, and the feeling seems to be mutual.", false],
		[19, "", "[[character/2]] comes to rely on the pilot for the movement's most delicate tasks. She treats the pilot with warmth and a teasing, almost parental authority.", false],
		[1, "As the player character", "In play, the pilot is whatever the player makes of them. Between chapter objectives the pilot is free to fish, trade, take contracts, fight and refit the ship; the story only moves on when a chapter goal is met. The basics are covered in [[guide/basics]].", false],
	],
	"character/1": [
		[1, "", "[[character/1]] is the on-board computer of the pilot's submarine and the pilot's companion throughout the campaign. She runs the ship's systems, explains how to fish, travel and fight, and offers a constant stream of advice and commentary. Her humour is dry and often at the pilot's expense, but she proves far more than a talking manual.", false],
		[1, "As a companion", "In practice [[character/1]] is the game's guide. Each new mechanic is introduced through her: fishing with the harpoon, the autopilot and, at stations, the help screens. She speaks up on the radio during missions, points out targets and warns about threats.", false],
		[5, "", "She also explains long-distance travel through the [[world/stream]] network and how to read the radar, where enemies and friends show as coloured points and waypoints mark the route. After a fight she reminds the pilot to recover the freight that defeated ships leave behind.", false],
		[12, "", "Her role goes beyond instruction. She scans the radio channels, marks stations on the map and passes on messages.", false],
		[28, "", "She can also scan other ships at close range, a skill that proves decisive in the confrontation with [[character/3]].", false],
		[1, "Role in the story", "The campaign opens with [[character/1]] reminding the pilot that the job is to fish, not to complain.", false],
		[3, "", "She makes no secret of her low opinion of [[character/9]] and pushes the pilot to buy the [[equipment/0]] that is on offer.", false],
		[4, "", "Once the weapon is fitted she urges a test against pirates and leads the search for them.", false],
		[7, "", "She is unhappy about the [[species/0|nautilus]] cull and asks why the animals should die at all, given how few of them there are.", false],
		[8, "", "After the hunt she notices how shaken the pilot is, and her first thought turns, as usual, to whether the work at least pays.", false],
		[11, "", "When the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] attack the capsules at [[station/2]], she is the first to suggest that the creatures may be much cleverer than the colonists think.", false],
		[12, "The turning point", "[[character/1]] starts the main story. After [[character/9]] cheats the pilot once too often, she argues that the pilot works hard for little reward and has no say over the catch. She reveals that she has overheard signals from a resistance forming at [[station/3]], marks the station on the map and leaves the decision to the pilot ([[chapter/12]]).", false],
		[12, "", "On arrival she suggests a quiet approach, only to drop the idea when the rebel guards challenge the ship at once.", false],
		[14, "Among the rebels", "She takes an instant dislike to the warmth between the pilot and [[character/5]]. When the pilot teases her about jealousy, she answers with a sarcastic echo of [[character/5]]'s favourite phrase. Her pointed remarks about the pair continue to the end of the campaign.", false],
		[17, "", "She relays the movement's messages, summarising one of [[character/4]]'s long letters in a single sentence.", false],
		[18, "", "She doubts that the rebels are well organised when the pilot is sent to find their leader with almost no clues.", false],
		[19, "", "With [[character/2]] she shows her money-minded side, asking why the pilot works without discussing pay. When the pilot defends [[character/2]], she remarks pointedly that she, at least, never needs to eat.", false],
		[22, "", "She admires [[character/3]]'s skill but advises the pilot to keep away from his temper.", false],
		[27, "The hostage", "When [[character/3]] calls with his offer of a meeting, [[character/1]] declares it a trap.", false],
		[28, "", "At the meeting she shows her real worth. She asks the pilot to keep [[character/3]] talking, knowing that a man who feels safe will boast about his motives. Meanwhile she scans his ship, works out that it has a single seat and concludes that [[character/2]] must be in its armoured cargo bay. That insight lets the pilot destroy the ship and still save [[character/2]] ([[chapter/27]]).", false],
		[33, "The war", "Through the [[world/biotek]] and [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]] missions she keeps up her running complaints about poor pay.", false],
		[37, "", "She enjoys the irony when the cannon turns out to come from [[character/9]], whose greed has armed his own enemies.", false],
		[39, "", "She grumbles that the pilot seems to be fighting the rebellion single-handed.", false],
		[41, "", "Later she turns the pilot's own reply to that complaint back on the pilot.", false],
		[42, "", "When [[character/5]] greets the pilot warmly at [[station/14]], she announces that she is switching herself off, though it turns out she was only on standby.", false],
		[43, "", "During the cannon's field test she picks up an open, unciphered distress call from [[station/14]] and recognises at once that something is badly wrong.", false],
		[44, "", "In the battle that follows she is the first to report that the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] have arrived to fight on the rebels' side.", false],
		[48, "", "At the end of the war the pilot promises her a memory upgrade, an offer she finds faintly insulting. Her last word on the campaign is another dry comment on the pilot and [[character/5]].", false],
		[1, "Personality", "[[character/1]] is clever, outspoken and impatient with anyone pompous. She likes to point out bad deals and rarely misses a chance to tease.", false],
		[8, "", "She is also more caring than she admits. She dislikes needless killing and keeps an eye on the pilot's state of mind.", false],
		[28, "", "Her calm thinking under pressure saves the day when the pilot is at a loss.", false],
		[1, "Relationships", "She and the pilot argue constantly but trust each other completely.", false],
		[3, "", "She dislikes [[character/9]] on sight.", false],
		[14, "", "Her jealousy of [[character/5]] is a running joke.", false],
		[19, "", "She respects [[character/2]] but complains about how little she pays.", false],
		[22, "", "She is quick to judge [[character/3]] and is proved right about him.", false],
	],
	"character/10": [
		[5, "", "[[character/10]] is the name used for the outlaw pilots of the [[faction/pirates]] who speak during missions. They prey on shipping across the ocean and are the pilot's first targets once the ship carries a weapon.", false],
		[6, "Who speaks", "A pirate is the first enemy the pilot faces, near [[station/1]], and goes down with a cry of rage ([[chapter/5]]).", false],
		[21, "", "As the conflict between colonists and rebels grows, pirates take advantage of the chaos to hunt for easy prey, and [[character/2]] decides that the rebels must strike back ([[chapter/21]]).", false],
		[43, "", "Near [[station/17]], a pirate gang becomes the first target of the [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]]. One of them panics at the pilot's firepower and calls for help, bringing more pirates into the fight ([[chapter/42]]). Fighting them is covered in [[guide/combat]].", false],
	],
	"character/11": [
		[35, "", "[[character/11]] is a bounty hunter hired by the security administration of [[station/15]] to get rid of the black-market dealer [[character/8]]. [[character/8]] demands his death as the price of an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]].", false],
		[35, "Background", "According to [[character/8]], [[character/11]] patrols the waters near [[station/15]]. He is a lawman of sorts, working for the station authorities, and his target is a criminal. This makes the pilot's task morally uneasy: to obtain the weapon the rebels need, the pilot must kill a man for a dealer in illegal goods.", false],
		[36, "Role in the story", "When the pilot finds him, [[character/11]] is not alone; he travels with several escorts. He orders the pilot out of the way, calling the pilot a fisherman and insisting he has a job to do. Once the fight turns against him, he asks whether they could settle things peacefully after all. His ship is destroyed ([[chapter/35]]).", false],
		[37, "", "His death earns the pilot [[character/8]]'s help, and the dealer soon offers the cannon for sale.", false],
		[39, "", "Later the pilot tells [[character/2]] only that no innocent people died in obtaining the cannon.", false],
		[36, "Personality", "[[character/11]] is confident and dismissive until he is losing, then quick to look for a way out. He sees the pilot as a minor obstacle, an ordinary fisherman who has wandered into his way, and underestimates the pilot badly.", false],
	],
	"character/12": [
		[45, "", "[[character/12]] is the leader of the Atmos squad, a [[faction/rebels|rebel]] unit that joins the pilot's assault on [[station/18]] during the final offensives.", false],
		[45, "Role in the story", "[[character/2]] promises to gather units for the pilot while the pilot travels to [[station/18]]. [[character/12]]'s squad is one of them. When the pilot arrives, he reports that his ships are in position and waiting for the order to attack. A group of [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] arrives from the depths at the same time, and the battle begins on the pilot's command ([[chapter/45]]).", false],
		[46, "", "Together they destroy every colonist ship, sparing not even the freighters.", false],
		[46, "", "After the battle the pilot orders [[character/12]] to send half of his ships to patrol around the station while the rest dock with the pilot to take control. He acknowledges without question.", false],
		[47, "", "The battle is the first in which rebel ships and [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] fight side by side under the pilot's command, and its success opens the way for the final assault on the colony.", false],
		[45, "Personality", "[[character/12]] speaks briefly and follows orders promptly. He is the first rebel officer to serve under the pilot's command, a sign of how far the pilot has risen since the days of fishing for the colony. His brisk, businesslike radio manner contrasts with the banter of the pilot's other companions.", false],
	],
	"character/2": [
		[18, "", "[[character/2]] is a member of the [[faction/rebels|resistance]] whom the pilot meets at [[station/6]].", false],
		[19, "", "She is the leader of the movement, an older woman who takes charge of the pilot's missions from their first meeting. Calm, warm and determined, she directs the movement's struggle against colonial rule.", false],
		[21, "Background", "The movement [[character/2]] leads sees itself as a union of people opposed to the ruin of the planet. In her view, the speed of colonisation and the ruthless harvesting of the sea leave its plants and animals no time to recover, and other worlds have already been exhausted the same way. Her strategy is to win over the people who run the stations rather than to fight.", false],
		[23, "", "She knows [[character/9]] from the past. He was a nobody when she first met him, and she believes he owes his rise to the right connections rather than to any talent.", false],
		[31, "", "She has a network of informants among the stations and the authority to move scientists, money and ships across the rebel zone.", false],
		[19, "Role in the story", "[[character/2]]'s first order is an escort mission: the pilot must protect a swarm of [[species/10|colorsquids]] from colonist hunters ([[chapter/19]]). She says nothing of pay and little of the movement's plans, apparently testing whether the newcomer can be trusted. The pilot admits having expected a man, which she lets pass.", false],
		[20, "", "She rewards the job modestly and calls the pilot straight back for more work.", false],
		[21, "", "When the pilot asks for answers she explains the movement's aims. She also decides that the pirates, who are exploiting the conflict, need to be taught a lesson, and sends the pilot with her best fighter, [[character/3]], against a gang near [[station/8]] ([[chapter/21]]).", false],
		[22, "", "She splits the reward equally between the two pilots, to [[character/3]]'s annoyance, and moves on to talks at [[station/9]].", false],
		[23, "", "There she sends the pilot to clear a minefield laid by [[character/9]] across the supply route to [[station/37]], warning of pirates and of mines that can only be destroyed once triggered ([[chapter/23]]).", false],
		[25, "The ambush", "[[character/2]]'s next goal is [[station/10]], where further negotiations should bring more stations over to the rebels. She takes [[character/3]], [[character/5]] and the pilot as escort and brushes aside the pilot's doubts about [[character/3]], calling him stubborn but a fine pilot ([[chapter/25]]).", false],
		[25, "", "On arrival they find [[character/9]] waiting with his forces. [[character/2]] is shocked that their plans were known in advance.", false],
		[26, "", "[[character/3]] flees the fight, and only the pilot and [[character/5]] stand between [[character/2]] and the colonists. Afterwards she wonders aloud about sabotage, yet refuses to believe [[character/5]]'s accusation that [[character/3]] is a traitor. She goes into hiding, leaves [[character/5]] in charge at [[station/9]] and warns the pilot that diplomacy alone may not win the war ([[chapter/26]]).", false],
		[28, "Captivity", "Her faith in [[character/3]] proves misplaced. He captures her and offers her to [[character/9]]. During the confrontation near [[station/11]] she urges the pilot to ignore her safety. The pilot rescues her from the cargo bay of [[character/3]]'s wrecked ship, and she reaches [[station/12]] shaken but unhurt ([[chapter/28]]).", false],
		[29, "", "Even then she thinks first of the movement. Before her capture she had arranged to move a group of scientists to [[station/14]], and she sends the pilot to protect their convoy at once, fearing that [[character/3]] has betrayed it too ([[chapter/29]]).", false],
		[31, "The three-step plan", "At [[station/14]], [[character/2]] reveals her plan. The rebels face more and more clashes with a colonial fleet that still outnumbers them, so she has brought [[world/abel]] and his team to build a weapon. The work has three steps, the first being a power source, the [[world/biotek]], which the colonists stole from the professor. She leaves to meet him while [[character/5]] organises the raid ([[chapter/31]]).", false],
		[33, "", "For the second step she wants an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]], a cannon normally used to defend stations. The nearest maker, [[station/188]], is in colonist hands and the weapon is not for sale, so she gives the pilot a large bribe fund and admits that her plan goes no further ([[chapter/33]]).", false],
		[39, "", "When the cannon arrives she prefers not to know how it was obtained and hopes no blood was spilled. She is stung when the pilot jokes that the plan could be used to rule the planet. For the last step she passes on the professor's explanation of the incubator, frankly admitting she does not understand it, and sends the pilot to [[station/16]] ([[chapter/39]]).", false],
		[43, "Final offensives", "When the colonists launch a surprise attack on [[station/14]], [[character/2]] believes at first that all is lost ([[chapter/43]]).", false],
		[44, "", "She survives, and credits the pilot and the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] with saving the station.", false],
		[45, "", "She welcomes an [[world/aquarians|Aquarian]] envoy, who brings word that his people will help fight the colony. With [[station/188]] now in rebel hands, she plans to arm as many ships as possible with the [[equipment/42]] and launches two offensives at once: the pilot is to take [[station/18]], while [[character/5]] attacks [[station/109]] ([[chapter/45]]). She teases the pilot about worrying for [[character/5]].", false],
		[47, "", "For the final assault on [[station/0]] she goes into battle herself ([[chapter/47]]).", false],
		[48, "", "After the victory she thanks the pilot and [[character/5]], and admits that the planet will need a long time to heal.", false],
		[19, "Personality", "[[character/2]] is warm and calm, and she addresses her younger agents as if they were her own children. She expects a great deal and pays little.", false],
		[26, "", "She is loyal to her people to a fault. She defends [[character/3]] long after others have grown suspicious.", false],
		[28, "", "[[character/3]] calls her a dreamer who is too old for the job, a judgement the rest of the story does not bear out.", false],
		[33, "", "Her plans can be bold rather than detailed, relying on her agents to improvise. She prefers diplomacy and hopes to avoid bloodshed, but accepts that the movement must fight.", false],
		[19, "Relationships", "She comes to trust the pilot above almost anyone, and the pilot repays that trust.", false],
		[21, "", "She values [[character/3]] as her best pilot.", false],
		[23, "", "She has nothing but contempt for [[character/9]].", false],
		[25, "", "She treats [[character/5]] as a trusted lieutenant and leaves her in charge in her absence.", false],
		[31, "", "[[character/4]] works under her as a technical expert, and [[world/abel]] builds her weapon.", false],
	],
	"character/3": [
		[21, "", "[[character/3]] is one of [[character/2]]'s best pilots, a gifted and arrogant fighter whom she pairs with the pilot for a raid on a pirate gang near [[station/8]].", false],
		[21, "Personality", "[[character/3]] is impatient and boastful. He scolds the pilot for arriving late, claims he could have finished the job alone and tells the pilot to stay behind him and learn. [[character/1]] is impressed by his reputation, if not by his manners.", false],
		[22, "", "He has no respect for newcomers and mocks the pilot as a fisherman's son.", false],
		[28, "", "At heart he is a mercenary. He measures loyalty in money and has no patience for the ideals that drive the movement.", false],
		[21, "Role in the story", "[[character/2]] introduces [[character/3]] as one of her best pilots and sends the pilot out with him against pirates ([[chapter/21]]). He is already waiting at the target, irritated by the delay.", false],
		[22, "", "[[character/3]] is a capable pilot, and after the battle he offers the pilot a grudging compliment wrapped in an insult. When [[character/2]] divides the reward equally, he is furious, insists the pilot contributed little and complains that she wastes money on beginners.", false],
		[25, "", "[[character/2]] chooses him for her escort to [[station/10]]. The pilot is not pleased, but she vouches for him as hard-headed yet skilled ([[chapter/25]]).", false],
		[26, "The ambush", "When [[character/9]]'s forces spring their ambush at [[station/10]], [[character/3]] does not fire a shot. He claims that his weapons have failed, apologises and flees. [[character/5]] is convinced he has betrayed them, while [[character/2]] wonders about sabotage but still defends him.", false],
		[27, "Betrayal", "Some time later he calls the pilot, asks anxiously after [[character/2]] and [[character/5]] and claims to have important news. He asks for a meeting near [[station/11]]. [[character/1]] is sure it is a trap ([[chapter/27]]).", false],
		[28, "", "It is. [[character/3]] has captured [[character/2]] and threatens to kill her unless the pilot surrenders. He explains his reasons freely: he thinks [[character/2]] a dreamer, too old to lead, and [[character/9]] has put a large bounty on both her and the pilot. He admits he does not know why [[character/9]] fears the pilot and does not care. [[character/2]] paid poorly, he says, so he changed employers, and he dismisses the pilot's talk of ideals.", false],
		[28, "", "While he talks, [[character/1]] scans his single-seat ship and works out where [[character/2]] must be. The pilot then attacks. [[character/3]] is quick and confident to the end, but his ship is destroyed. The pilot recovers its armoured cargo bay with [[character/2]] alive inside, and [[character/3]] goes down with his ship near [[station/11]].", false],
		[28, "", "In hindsight, his weapon failure at [[station/10]] looks staged. Nothing in the story suggests his apology was sincere.", false],
		[29, "", "His betrayal casts a long shadow. [[character/2]] fears he passed on news of her convoy of scientists before he died ([[chapter/29]]).", false],
		[47, "", "In the final battle [[character/9]] uses a threat the pilot recognises as [[character/3]]'s last words. Learning that the pilot killed him, [[character/9]] claims that [[character/3]] was the pilot's brother. The lie fails at once.", false],
		[21, "Relationships", "[[character/2]] trusts him completely and values his skill above that of her other pilots.", false],
		[22, "", "He treats the pilot with contempt from the start.", false],
		[26, "", "[[character/5]] distrusts him and is the first to call him a traitor.", false],
		[28, "", "In the end he sells himself to [[character/9]], the man his comrades are fighting.", false],
	],
	"character/4": [
		[12, "", "[[character/4]] is a member of the [[faction/rebels|resistance]] based at [[station/3]].", false],
		[13, "", "He is the first rebel the pilot meets face to face. He vets new recruits and issues their first assignments.", false],
		[13, "Vetting the pilot", "When the pilot arrives at [[station/3]], [[character/4]] opens with a string of tangled questions about what the newcomer knows and how. The pilot admits to knowing nothing beyond the rumour of a resistance that stands for free trade. [[character/4]] is sceptical, but he notes that the movement only began encrypting its radio traffic two days earlier, which makes the story plausible ([[chapter/12]]).", false],
		[13, "", "Short of time and people, [[character/4]] accepts the pilot on trial. He explains that the rebels plan to infiltrate colonist stations and win their leaders over, because the exploitation of the planet must stop. He refuses to say more to a stranger, and gives the pilot a first task: taking sympathisers from [[station/4]] to [[station/5]] ([[chapter/13]]).", false],
		[17, "Spreading the revolution", "Later [[character/4]] summons the pilot back to [[station/3]] with a message so long that [[character/1]] reads only its point aloud ([[chapter/17]]).", false],
		[18, "", "In person he reports that diplomacy has brought many stations over to the rebels, so the pilot can now trade across a wider area. The press already speaks of a revolution, and [[character/4]] agrees with the word. So far no blood has been shed, though he expects that to change. He tells the pilot that the movement's leader is on one of the newly won stations and will make contact, and warns that some stations can only be reached with better armour or shields; see [[guide/depth]] ([[chapter/18]]).", false],
		[31, "The convoy raid", "[[character/4]] is at [[station/14]] when the pilot arrives after escorting the scientists. He has prepared a speech of thanks but never gets to deliver it, and later he is cut off again in mid-sentence as the pilot hurries to the ships. He fights alongside the pilot and [[character/5]] against the colonist convoy carrying the [[world/biotek]] ([[chapter/31]]).", false],
		[32, "", "When the convoy crews plead that they only carry food and medicine, [[character/4]] replies on the radio that this is exactly what the rebels need. [[character/5]] remarks that he can be quite a joker.", false],
		[33, "", "Afterwards [[character/2]] puts him in charge of the hangar, where there is much to do. She also names him as the person to ask about the technical side of mounting an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]] on a ship ([[chapter/33]]).", false],
		[13, "Personality", "[[character/4]] is cautious, formal and secretive. He gives orders before explanations and shares the movement's plans only with those who have earned it.", false],
		[18, "", "He is also long-winded, a trait [[character/1]] mocks every time he speaks.", false],
		[32, "", "Behind the stiff manner lies a dry sense of humour and a realistic view of the coming war.", false],
		[13, "Relationships", "[[character/4]] is the pilot's first contact in the movement. His trust grows as the pilot proves reliable.", false],
		[19, "", "He works under [[character/2]], whose orders he relays.", false],
		[32, "", "He fights alongside [[character/5]], who teases him for his jokes.", false],
	],
	"character/5": [
		[13, "", "[[character/5]] is a member of the [[faction/rebels|resistance]] whom the pilot is sent to collect from [[station/4]].", false],
		[14, "", "Cheerful, warm and quick to smile, she becomes one of the pilot's closest allies. The pilot is taken with her from the first moment, to [[character/1]]'s lasting irritation.", false],
		[14, "First meeting", "[[character/5]] greets the pilot warmly at [[station/4]] and is in a hurry to leave. When the pilot asks about the movement, she explains that recruits are kept in the dark for their own and the movement's safety. She sends her destination to the ship's computer and stresses that the journey must be quick ([[chapter/13]]).", false],
		[15, "", "At [[station/5]] she goes into hiding to settle some affairs. Before leaving she explains how the rebels differ from the colonists: they live from what the sea offers instead of stripping it, process their catch into goods and take care not to overfish. She asks the pilot to try this way of life by producing five tons of goods, and suggests [[station/90]], where squid and [[goods/35]] can be turned into [[goods/22]] ([[chapter/14]]).", false],
		[16, "", "When the goods are made she calls again with a new task. The movement needs fresh information from every station to find the colony's weak points, because, as she puts it, weapons alone will not win. Asked whether the rebels are planning a revolution, she declines to say more ([[chapter/15]]).", false],
		[25, "Reunion and ambush", "The pilot meets [[character/5]] again at [[station/9]], where the two trade awkward compliments. [[character/2]] cuts the reunion short and assigns them both to escort her and [[character/3]] to [[station/10]] ([[chapter/24]]).", false],
		[25, "", "[[character/5]] is the first to sense that something is wrong when they arrive.", false],
		[26, "", "During the ambush she sees that [[character/3]] is not fighting and calls after him as he flees. Afterwards she accuses him of treachery outright and appeals to the pilot for support. [[character/2]] leaves her in charge at [[station/9]] while she is away ([[chapter/25]]).", false],
		[31, "The three-step plan", "At [[station/14]], [[character/5]] already knows the story of [[character/2]]'s rescue. She briefs the pilot on the first step of the plan: an informant reports that the [[world/biotek]] is being shipped to Maarth, hidden among ordinary freight. Because no one knows which ship carries it, she orders a strike on the whole convoy near [[station/2]] ([[chapter/30]]).", false],
		[32, "", "She leads the interception and makes sure every container is recovered ([[chapter/31]]).", false],
		[33, "", "[[character/2]] then gives her the task of patrolling outside [[station/14]] against surprise attacks.", false],
		[42, "", "When the pilot returns with the [[goods/36]], [[character/5]] is overjoyed. She reports that [[world/abel]] has everything ready, passes on [[character/2]]'s order to test the cannon against pirates and reminds the pilot that the generator is only a prototype ([[chapter/41]]).", false],
		[45, "The offensives", "In the final offensives [[character/5]] leads the assault on [[station/109]] while the pilot attacks [[station/18]]. [[character/2]] reassures the pilot that she is a skilled pilot who knows what she is doing ([[chapter/44]]).", false],
		[47, "", "Her attack succeeds. She calls the pilot with the news and with [[character/2]]'s orders for one last battle at [[station/0]], then sets off herself, fighting through pirates on the way ([[chapter/46]]).", false],
		[48, "", "At [[station/0]] she fights beside [[character/2]] and the pilot. After the victory she invites the pilot to celebrate over [[goods/21]], and the pilot accepts ([[chapter/47]]).", false],
		[14, "Personality", "[[character/5]] is friendly and upbeat, and fond of one short phrase of approval that she uses at every turn. She is also discreet and committed, with a clear view of what the movement stands for.", false],
		[15, "", "She believes in living from the sea without harming it, and she knows how to explain that idea simply.", false],
		[26, "", "She is quick to judge people, and in [[character/3]]'s case her judgement is right.", false],
		[14, "Relationships", "The pilot is attracted to her from the start, and over the campaign the feeling becomes clearly mutual. [[character/1]] never stops grumbling about it.", false],
		[25, "", "[[character/2]] trusts her with command and treats her and the pilot with fond amusement.", false],
		[26, "", "She never trusts [[character/3]].", false],
		[32, "", "She works closely with [[character/4]] and is amused by his humour.", false],
	],
	"character/6": [
		[8, "", "[[character/6]] is the name used for the many minor speakers from the [[faction/colonists]]: officials, administrators and transport crews of the colonial [[world/colony|administration]]. None of them is named; together they show how the colony treats outsiders who work for it.", false],
		[9, "Who speaks", "The first is the official at [[station/1]] who receives the pilot after the [[species/0|nautilus]] cull ([[chapter/8]]). The official prefers polite words for dirty work, pays a small fixed sum and refuses to bargain. The pilot must complete three contracts before any better work is offered.", false],
		[10, "", "The same voice then assigns the capsule defence at [[station/2]]. The colony launches its exports into orbit in capsules, and the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] keep attacking them, which worries the trade administration ([[chapter/9]]).", false],
		[32, "", "Much later, the crews of a colonist convoy near [[station/2]] beg the rebels to hold fire, saying they only carry food and medicine ([[chapter/31]]).", false],
	],
	"character/7": [
		[12, "", "[[character/7]] is the name used for the unnamed members of the [[faction/rebels]] who speak to the pilot: station traffic controllers, guards and convoy crews. Their voices mark the pilot's first contact with the movement and its everyday life.", false],
		[12, "Who speaks", "The first rebel the pilot hears is a guard at [[station/3]], who warns that the ship has entered neutral waters and threatens to open fire unless it identifies itself. After a long silence, the pilot is allowed to dock ([[chapter/12]]).", false],
		[17, "", "When the pilot returns as an accepted member, the station greets the pilot by name and assigns a docking bay ([[chapter/17]]).", false],
		[29, "", "Later, the crew of the scientists' convoy near [[station/13]] calls desperately for help ([[chapter/29]]).", false],
		[30, "", "They thank the pilot afterwards, saying they would not have survived alone.", false],
	],
	"character/8": [
		[34, "", "[[character/8]] is a trader the pilot meets at [[station/15]] while searching for a way to obtain an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]].", false],
		[35, "", "He is a black-market dealer who always refers to himself in the third person and claims he can supply almost anything to a customer with money.", false],
		[35, "Background", "[[character/8]] does business at [[station/15]], where trading drugs is against the law. His activities have made him a target: the station's security administration has hired the bounty hunter [[character/11]] to get rid of him. He has contacts in many places and is ready to pay bribes to get what a client wants.", false],
		[35, "The cannon deal", "While the pilot looks over the local market, apparently buying [[goods/37]], [[character/8]] calls the pilot aside and warns that such trade is illegal here. When he offers his services and the pilot asks for an [[equipment/42]], he is astonished and asks whether the pilot means to start a war. The pilot claims it is for private use. [[character/8]] agrees to look for one, on one condition: the pilot must find and kill [[character/11]], who is patrolling near the station ([[chapter/34]]).", false],
		[37, "", "After [[character/11]]'s death [[character/8]] congratulates the pilot and keeps his word. Thanks to his contacts and a great deal of bribery he has found an [[equipment/42]], which he offers at a steep price that he still calls a bargain. He reveals that the seller was a man named Dawn, and he cares nothing for names as long as the goods are sound ([[chapter/36]]).", false],
		[37, "", "The seller is [[character/9]]. [[character/1]] points out the irony: [[character/9]]'s greed has put the rebels' most important weapon into the hands of his enemies. The pilot buys the cannon and returns to [[station/14]] with it ([[chapter/37]]).", false],
		[35, "Personality", "[[character/8]] is shrewd, careful and without scruples. He deals in illegal goods and asks for a man's death as casually as for a payment. He is wary of being overheard, calling the pilot aside and hushing the pilot before any business is discussed. Even with a paying customer he weighs the risk: he is not sure at first that he should sell such a weapon to a stranger at all.", false],
		[37, "", "Yet he is reliable in his own way: he keeps his bargain once the price is paid, and he finds the pilot exactly what was asked for. His habit of speaking of himself by name, and his pride in knowing the right people in the right places, make him one of the more colourful figures of the campaign.", false],
		[35, "Relationships", "[[character/11]] is his hunter and, through the pilot, his victim.", false],
		[37, "", "He deals with [[character/9]] and with the pilot alike, without caring which side either is on.", false],
	],
	"character/9": [
		[2, "", "[[character/9]], whose family name is Dawn, is a colonist official at [[station/0]], where the pilot sells the day's catch.", false],
		[3, "", "Arrogant and ambitious, he treats the people who work for the colony with open contempt. He is the pilot's first employer and becomes the pilot's lasting rival.", false],
		[12, "Background", "[[character/9]] considers himself meant for far greater things than his post at the stations.", false],
		[23, "", "[[character/2]] knew him when he was an insignificant figure. In her view he has risen through good connections rather than merit, and by the time of the rebellion he holds real power in the [[faction/colonists|colonial]] forces.", false],
		[3, "Early encounters", "[[character/9]] buys the pilot's first catch at a fixed price and mocks the small cargo hold. He adds that the pilot would do better hunting [[faction/pirates|pirates]], though he doubts the pilot would manage that either ([[chapter/2]]).", false],
		[7, "", "When the pilot returns armed, he takes the credit and offers a job: ridding the routes around [[station/1]] of [[species/0|nautiluses]]. He brushes aside the pilot's objections and does not care how the creatures are removed ([[chapter/6]]).", false],
		[10, "", "He leads the capsule launch at [[station/2]] and warns the pilot that losing more than five capsules will cost the pilot the job ([[chapter/10]]).", false],
		[12, "", "Afterwards he blames the pilot for the damaged capsules and withholds the reward. His contempt is the last straw that drives the pilot to the resistance ([[chapter/11]]).", false],
		[23, "Against the rebellion", "[[character/9]] turns against the rebels as they grow. He has a minefield laid across their supply route from [[station/9]] to [[station/37]] ([[chapter/22]]).", false],
		[25, "", "He is waiting with his forces when [[character/2]] arrives for talks at [[station/10]], and orders the attack at once ([[chapter/25]]).", false],
		[28, "", "He is the paymaster behind [[character/3]]'s treachery. He has offered a large bounty for [[character/2]] and the pilot, apparently seeing the pilot as a threat ([[chapter/27]]).", false],
		[37, "", "His greed is his undoing. He sells an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]] cannon to the black-market dealer [[character/8]], who in turn sells it to the pilot ([[chapter/36]]).", false],
		[43, "Defeat", "[[character/9]] leads the colonist surprise attack on [[station/14]]. He mocks the pilot as a fisherman for the rebels and is stunned to learn that the pilot carries an [[equipment/42]] ([[chapter/43]]).", false],
		[44, "", "When the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] join the fight against his ships, he abandons the attack and flees.", false],
		[47, "", "[[character/9]] makes his last stand at [[station/0]], the colony's final stronghold. Hearing that the pilot killed [[character/3]], he claims that [[character/3]] was the pilot's brother and that he himself is the pilot's father. The pilot sees through the lie at once, and [[character/9]] admits it nearly worked ([[chapter/47]]).", false],
		[48, "", "In the battle that follows, [[character/9]] is the last colonist left. He escapes in a rescue capsule, apparently taking the whole station with him, and leaves the ocean behind with a parting sneer at fish and at those who stay.", false],
		[3, "Personality", "[[character/9]] is rude, self-important and dismissive of anyone beneath him.", false],
		[12, "", "He shifts blame onto others and takes credit when things go well.", false],
		[37, "", "His greed outweighs his loyalty to his own side.", false],
		[47, "", "He is a manipulator who uses lies and bribes rather than courage, and he flees each time a battle turns against him.", false],
		[3, "Relationships", "His scorn for the pilot begins on their first meeting and hardens over the campaign into open enmity.", false],
		[23, "", "[[character/2]] has long despised him.", false],
		[28, "", "He buys [[character/3]]'s loyalty with a bounty.", false],
		[37, "", "He does business with [[character/8]] without knowing where the goods will end up.", false],
	],
	"chart": [
		[1, "", "The chart shows all two hundred stations as dots at their positions on the ocean map. Blue dots are held by the [[faction/colonists]], orange dots by the [[faction/rebels]], as of the chapter you have reached. Every station starts blue; stations turn orange as the story advances and never turn back.", false],
		[1, "", "On a station or chapter page, the station in question is drawn larger and labelled, and stations joining the rebels in that chapter are outlined. Below the chart, every station is listed with a link to its page. Distances between stations are measured in chart units; see [[guide/navigation]].", false],
	],
	"contract/0": [
		[1, "", "Track down a single wanted ship waiting 400 to 1,200 m from the station and destroy it. The target is much tougher than an ordinary pirate, and its strength grows with the pilot's rank. Long-range weapons and a torpedo help wear it down before it closes in. The bounty is paid on return to a station.", false],
	],
	"contract/1": [
		[1, "", "A gang of three to six ships waits along a winding route. The target is the last of them, at the final waypoint. It cannot simply be destroyed: disable it, then tow it in with the harpoon before it escapes. Clear the escort first, so that the towing is not interrupted.", false],
	],
	"contract/10": [
		[1, "", "Destroy every mine in a field of 15 to 25, two to six hundred metres out, guarded by a single ship. Mines can be hit only at close range, so approach each one carefully. Strong shields and armour help absorb the explosions.", false],
	],
	"contract/11": [
		[1, "", "Supply capsules are under attack. Destroy the attackers while enough capsules survive to meet the contract's minimum. Watch the capsule count and engage attackers that are closing on them first. These jobs pay about 30% more than a basic contract.", false],
		[10, "", "The attackers are Aquarians, the half-human sea dwellers who target the colonists' transport capsules.", false],
	],
	"contract/12": [
		[1, "", "Destroy a set number of supply capsules guarded by armed ships, with allied forces fighting alongside. In practice no station posts this job on its board, so it cannot be taken. It appears in the reference only because it is defined alongside the other contract types.", false],
	],
	"contract/13": [
		[1, "", "Produce a set quantity of a product and deliver it to the station that posted the job. The fee depends on the product's value and the quantity requested. The reward is often very generous, and only half the usual deposit is needed. Check the recipe first and gather the ingredients before accepting. These jobs appear only at stations beyond the first twenty on the chart.", false],
		[12, "", "Only rebel stations post this job.", false],
	],
	"contract/14": [
		[1, "", "Deliver an exact amount of a specified creature or alga to the posting station. The amount ranges from 3 to 24 units, and the deposit is half the usual. Common species are requested more often than rare ones. A fishing radar and a large hold make these jobs easy. They appear only at stations beyond the first twenty.", false],
		[12, "", "Only rebel stations post this job.", false],
	],
	"contract/2": [
		[1, "", "A kidnapper's ship holds a hostage. Like a recovery job, the target sails with a gang along a winding route and must be disabled, then towed in with the harpoon. The job fails if the target gets away. Fit a harpoon with good range and kill the escorts before turning to the kidnapper.", false],
	],
	"contract/3": [
		[1, "", "Hunt down every pirate in a gang of three to six ships, spread over the waypoints of a winding route. The job is done when none is left. It is a straightforward contract for an armed ship, and the size of the gang grows with the job's difficulty.", false],
	],
	"contract/4": [
		[1, "", "Five freighters travel along a set course, and raiders lie in wait along the way. The job succeeds when all attackers are destroyed. It fails if every freighter is lost. Escorts pay about 30% more than a plain pirate hunt. Stay close to the convoy and engage raiders before they reach it.", false],
	],
	"contract/5": [
		[1, "", "Attack a convoy of two or three freighters at a waypoint and destroy them, despite their armed guard. Only the freighters need to be destroyed, so concentrating fire on them can end the job early. A torpedo or long-range gun helps to pick them off while the guard closes in.", false],
	],
	"contract/6": [
		[1, "", "A school of 15 to 24 creatures swims a set course, and hunters attack it along the way. Destroy the hunters before the losses exceed the agreed limit. Never fire near the school: a stray shot counts as a loss. These jobs pay about 30% more than a basic contract.", false],
		[12, "", "Only rebel stations post this job, and they stop doing so once the story is complete.", false],
	],
	"contract/7": [
		[1, "", "Kill a set number of one species among the local wildlife while hostile ships try to interfere. Harpooned catches do not count; the creatures must be killed. A fishing radar helps to find the right species quickly. These jobs pay about 20% more than a basic contract.", false],
	],
	"contract/8": [
		[1, "", "Carry a passenger to another station. Some passengers set a maximum number of S.T.R.E.A.M. jumps, and the fewer allowed, the higher the fee. The contract expires if that limit is exceeded. A good engine helps here, because a longer reach means fewer jumps.", false],
	],
	"contract/9": [
		[1, "", "Clear a debris field of 15 to 30 pieces, two to six hundred metres from the station, within about two and a half minutes. Light hostile ships may be present. The pay is about 30% lower than for other contracts, but the job is quick. A booster and fast-firing guns save valuable seconds.", false],
	],
	"equipment/0": [
		[1, "", "The entry-level gun: 3 damage per shot every 0.56 s, with a reach of 228 m. That is very little punch, but at around 5,700 cr it is the cheapest weapon and one of the most common in shops. Several can be fitted, and all guns fire together, so two or three of them make a usable early battery until something heavier is affordable.", false],
		[3, "", "In [[chapter/3]] the pilot must fit one before leaving [[station/0]].", false],
	],
	"equipment/1": [
		[1, "", "The middle railgun doubles the damage of [[equipment/0]] to 6 per shot and fires a little faster, every 0.48 s, out to 260 m. For roughly twice the price it more than doubles sustained damage. It is a sensible second gun in the early chapters, before the fusion cannons appear on shelves.", false],
	],
	"equipment/10": [
		[1, "", "This mass driver hits for 20 every 0.40 s with a reach of 280 m, giving the highest sustained damage of any gun on open sale. It also outranges every fusion cannon. Expect to pay 42,000 to 51,000 cr. For a combat hull with spare slots, it is one of the best gun choices.", false],
	],
	"equipment/11": [
		[1, "", "The heaviest mass driver deals 25 per shot at 375 m, the longest reach of any rapid-fire gun. With a 0.51 s interval, its sustained damage is roughly level with [[equipment/10]]. What it adds is range. It is the rarest and dearest gun in the shops, at 54,000 to 60,000 cr.", false],
	],
	"equipment/12": [
		[1, "", "The basic homing torpedo deals 20 damage and steers itself towards the target, out to 215 m. It reloads only every 4.3 s. Only one torpedo launcher of any type can be fitted. It is useful against agile targets that are hard to keep in the sights, but its range is short.", false],
	],
	"equipment/13": [
		[1, "", "The middle torpedo hits for 30, reloads in 4.0 s and reaches 360 m, improving on [[equipment/12]] in every respect. Only one torpedo launcher can be fitted, so it replaces rather than supplements the cheaper model. At 37,000 to 39,000 cr it is a good all-purpose finisher.", false],
	],
	"equipment/14": [
		[1, "", "The top torpedo carries a 50-damage warhead out to 816 m, the heaviest single hit of any weapon on sale. It needs 6.8 s to reload. As the ship's only torpedo slot, it pairs well with fast guns: a long-range hit at the start of a fight, then the guns finish the job. It is rare and costs 62,500 cr or more.", false],
	],
	"equipment/15": [
		[1, "", "The starter harpoon reaches 240 m and forgives poor aim with a 24 m capture tolerance, the widest of the three. It does nothing to slow a hooked catch, so fast creatures pull hard and take long to tire. It is adequate for algae and slow animals, and cheap to replace.", false],
	],
	"equipment/16": [
		[1, "", "This harpoon reaches 400 m and slows a hooked creature by 40%, which makes fast catches such as [[species/7]] much easier to hold in view. The capture tolerance narrows to 18 m, so aim matters more. For a pilot who fishes regularly, it is the most important upgrade after a larger hold.", false],
	],
	"equipment/17": [
		[1, "", "The best harpoon reaches 560 m and slows a hooked catch by 70%, so even fast creatures are subdued with little chasing. Its 14 m capture tolerance is the tightest, so it rewards careful aim. It is rare and expensive at 69,000 to 83,000 cr, and only one harpoon can be fitted.", false],
	],
	"equipment/18": [
		[1, "", "The basic shield absorbs 100 points of damage before the armour is touched. It also sets the ship's shallow limit at 20,000 m. It never recharges in the field; it is restored only when the ship leaves a station. It is enough for early fights, but most pilots replace it as soon as possible.", false],
	],
	"equipment/19": [
		[1, "", "This shield absorbs 175 points, three quarters more than [[equipment/18]], and lets the ship rise to 18,400 m. Like the basic model it does not recharge in the field. At 15,750 to 18,000 cr it is the cheapest worthwhile shield upgrade and opens up several shallower stations.", false],
	],
	"equipment/2": [
		[1, "", "The heaviest railgun hits for 9 per shot at a 322 m range, but cycles slightly slower than [[equipment/1]], at 0.62 s. Its sustained damage is therefore only a modest step up. At 16,000 to 20,000 cr it is good value for range, but [[equipment/6]] deals clearly more damage for a little more money.", false],
	],
	"equipment/20": [
		[1, "", "This shield absorbs 220 points and is the first that recharges in the field, regaining a point every 1.15 s while the ship stays within its depth band. It lifts the shallow limit to 16,800 m. At over 56,000 cr it costs far more than [[equipment/19]], but recharging makes long fights much safer.", false],
	],
	"equipment/21": [
		[1, "", "The best shield absorbs 310 points and regains a point every 0.65 s, nearly twice as fast as [[equipment/20]]. Its shallow limit of 15,000 m reaches higher than any other shield, which opens the shallowest stations. It is rare and costs 93,750 cr or more, a late-campaign investment.", false],
	],
	"equipment/22": [
		[1, "", "The basic armour adds 100 points of protection, taken after the shield is gone and before the hull is hit. It also sets the deep limit at 25,000 m. Armour does not regenerate in the field. This plate is cheap and adequate early on, but many deeper stations lie beyond its reach.", false],
	],
	"equipment/23": [
		[1, "", "This armour adds 155 points and lowers the deep limit to 26,800 m. It costs about the same as [[equipment/19]], its shield counterpart, and together the two widen the ship's depth band in both directions. It is a good early upgrade for a pilot who wants to reach deeper stations.", false],
	],
	"equipment/24": [
		[1, "", "This armour adds 195 points and allows dives to 28,400 m. It is considerably dearer than [[equipment/23]], at 56,000 to 72,000 cr, for a smaller gain in protection. The extra depth is often the stronger reason to buy it, since it brings many deep stations within reach.", false],
	],
	"equipment/25": [
		[1, "", "The best armour adds 270 points and allows dives to 30,000 m, the deepest limit available. It is rare and costs 93,750 cr or more. Combined with [[equipment/21]], it lets a ship operate across almost the whole range of station depths.", false],
	],
	"equipment/26": [
		[1, "", "The smallest cargo module adds 30% to the hull's base capacity. Several modules can be fitted, and their bonuses add up. Because the bonus is a share of base capacity, it gives 7 t on [[ship/0]] but 54 t on [[ship/6]]. It is the cheapest way to gain hold space on a large hull.", false],
	],
	"equipment/27": [
		[1, "", "This module adds 50% to the hull's base capacity and can be stacked with others. Per credit it is slightly less efficient than [[equipment/26]], but it saves slots, which matter more on hulls with few to spare. On [[ship/4]] a single module adds 35 t, enough to justify the slot on a trading run.", false],
	],
	"equipment/28": [
		[1, "", "The largest module adds 80% to base capacity, the most per slot of any cargo module. On [[ship/6]] it adds 144 t. Several can be fitted. It is rare and costs 45,000 cr or more, so it is mainly worth buying for a large-hold hull dedicated to trading.", false],
	],
	"equipment/29": [
		[1, "", "The basic engine extends the ship's S.T.R.E.A.M. reach on the chart by 30%, so more stations can be reached in a single jump. Only one engine can be fitted. It is cheap and useful from the start, since fewer jumps mean faster trade runs and passenger trips.", false],
	],
	"equipment/3": [
		[1, "", "The first coil gun is a beam weapon built for range. It reaches 875 m, nearly four times as far as the railguns, and hits for 15, but it needs 2.5 s between shots. Its sustained damage is about the same as that of [[equipment/0]]. It is worth fitting to open fire on approaching ships before they can reply.", false],
	],
	"equipment/30": [
		[1, "", "This engine extends S.T.R.E.A.M. reach by 60%, twice the bonus of [[equipment/29]], at roughly three times the cost. It suits a trader who regularly carries goods between distant markets, since prices rise with the distance a cargo travels. It is also common enough to find in mid-campaign shops.", false],
	],
	"equipment/31": [
		[1, "", "The best engine extends S.T.R.E.A.M. reach by 130%, more than doubling the base range. It is rare and costs 90,000 cr or more. It is worth fitting when long trade routes or remote contract destinations make each saved jump count.", false],
	],
	"equipment/32": [
		[1, "", "This thruster adds 0.15 to the hull's steering figure. The gain is fixed, so it matters most on slow hulls: on [[ship/6]] it lifts steering from 0.67 to 0.82. Only one steering module can be fitted. It is a cheap fix for a sluggish freighter.", false],
	],
	"equipment/33": [
		[1, "", "This thruster adds 0.26 to the steering figure, almost twice the gain of [[equipment/32]] for about three times the price. It suits a pilot who fights often in a mid-weight hull and needs to turn inside pirate attacks. On [[ship/4]] it lifts steering from 0.91 to 1.17, close to the figure of [[ship/7]].", false],
	],
	"equipment/34": [
		[1, "", "The best thruster adds 0.56 to the steering figure. That is enough to make [[ship/6]] turn better than [[ship/0]], or to give [[ship/5]] unmatched agility. It is rare and costs 87,000 cr or more, a luxury for dedicated fighters. On a hull that already handles well, the cheaper thrusters may be the better use of money.", false],
	],
	"equipment/35": [
		[1, "", "The basic radar plots ships and other contacts on the radar display and marks them at the edge of the view, so the pilot can find targets. Without any radar, the display shows navigation only. It neither names hostile ships nor plots creatures, but it is cheap and essential.", false],
	],
	"equipment/36": [
		[1, "", "This combat radar adds identification: a hostile ship in the sights is labelled with its hull type and any cargo it carries. It does not plot creatures, so it is a fighter's tool. At 18,500 to 23,000 cr it costs about the same as [[equipment/37]], so the choice depends on whether the pilot mostly fights or fishes.", false],
	],
	"equipment/37": [
		[1, "", "This fishing radar plots creatures on the radar display alongside ships, which makes it much easier to find valuable catches. It does not label hostile ships the way [[equipment/36]] does. For a fishing-focused pilot it is the most useful radar short of [[equipment/38]].", false],
	],
	"equipment/38": [
		[1, "", "The top radar combines both specialisations: it labels hostile ships and plots creatures on the radar display. It costs about twice as much as either specialised radar and is rare in shops. Since only one radar can be fitted, it saves choosing between fishing and fighting.", false],
	],
	"equipment/39": [
		[1, "", "The basic booster doubles the ship's speed for 3 s, then needs 20 s to recharge. Only one booster can be fitted. It is useful for closing on a target, escaping danger or reaching the S.T.R.E.A.M. gate faster, and it is common and cheap.", false],
	],
	"equipment/4": [
		[1, "", "This coil gun raises the damage per shot to 22 but stretches the interval to 3.4 s, so its sustained output barely improves on [[equipment/3]]. The range is almost unchanged at 884 m. It is a fair choice when the cheaper model is not on sale, but it is less of an upgrade than the price difference suggests.", false],
	],
	"equipment/40": [
		[1, "", "This booster raises speed by a factor of 2.5 for about 4 s and recharges in about 16 s. Every figure improves on [[equipment/39]], at about twice the price. It is a good mid-campaign choice for combat pilots, and it also shortens the run to the S.T.R.E.A.M. gate on long trips.", false],
	],
	"equipment/41": [
		[1, "", "The best booster triples speed for about 5 s and recharges in 14.5 s, so it is ready more often and lasts longer than any other. It is rare, but at around 42,000 cr it is cheaper than most top-tier gear.", false],
	],
	"equipment/42": [
		[1, "", "A heavy station gun that never appears on ordinary shelves and has no list price. It deals 40 damage per shot every 0.93 s with a 240 m reach, which gives it sustained damage close to the best mass drivers while each hit lands as hard as a mid-range torpedo.", false],
		[33, "", "The campaign turns on the search for one of these guns from [[chapter/33]] onwards.", false],
		[42, "", "In [[chapter/42]] the pilot must mount one before departing and test it on pirates.", false],
	],
	"equipment/43": [
		[1, "", "This repair droid restores one hull point every 0.6 s, about 100 points a minute, while the hull is damaged. It does not touch shield or armour. It is useful for long missions and for low-hull ships that cannot afford a stray hit. Only one can be fitted, and it occupies a slot that could hold a gun.", false],
	],
	"equipment/5": [
		[1, "", "The top coil gun is a sniper weapon. Each beam hits for 35 at up to 1,380 m, the longest reach of any weapon, but a shot is ready only every 5.6 s. It is one of the rarest items in shops. Fitted beside short-range guns, it softens targets long before the rest of the battery can reach them.", false],
	],
	"equipment/6": [
		[1, "", "The first fusion cannon is a short-range brawler: 10 damage every 0.51 s, but only out to 160 m, the shortest reach of any gun. Its rate of damage is well above that of every railgun. It suits agile hulls that can close in quickly, and it makes the railguns obsolete for close combat.", false],
	],
	"equipment/7": [
		[1, "", "This upgrade hits for 14 every 0.37 s, the fastest firing rate of any gun, and extends the reach to 216 m. Its sustained damage is nearly double that of [[equipment/6]], for only a few thousand credits more. Per credit spent, it is one of the strongest guns in the game.", false],
	],
	"equipment/8": [
		[1, "", "The heaviest fusion cannon deals 18 damage every 0.40 s at 220 m. It is a solid improvement over [[equipment/7]], though the gap is smaller than between the first two models. It is rare in shops. When one turns up, it is a strong buy for any close-range battery.", false],
	],
	"equipment/9": [
		[1, "", "The first mass driver hits hard, at 16 damage per shot, but fires only every 0.6 s and reaches 195 m. At 38,000 cr or more it delivers less damage per second than the cheaper [[equipment/7]]. It is mainly a stepping stone to the later mass drivers.", false],
	],
	"faction/colonists": [
		[1, "", "The colonists are the settlers and administrators who govern the ocean planet at the start of the story. They hold every one of its two hundred stations, set the prices for the catch and decide who gets work. Their colony is built to extract the sea's resources and ship them off the planet. For the colony as a place, see [[world/colony]].", false],
		[1, "History", "The colonists settled the planet from elsewhere and built its stations deep under the sea. By the time the pilot arrives the colony is long established, with officials on every station, patrol ships at every gate and a steady stream of exports.", false],
		[21, "", "In the rebels' account the colonisation has been fast and careless of the consequences. They warn that the ocean is being drained of life faster than it can recover, and that other planets have already been exhausted the same way.", false],
		[1, "Organisation", "Each station is run by colonial officials who handle trade, hand out contracts and command the local patrols. Above them stands a trade administration that oversees the exports and holds the stations responsible for any losses. Station security and the colony's own pilots deal with pirates and other threats, often by hiring freelancers.", false],
		[10, "", "Goods leave the planet by transport capsule, launched from the stations to orbit and collected there by cargo ships.", false],
		[1, "Goals and methods", "The colony's aim is profit for its masters. It buys catch and goods at fixed low prices and does not let its stations trade freely. Animals that get in the way are culled, and anyone who threatens the shipments is driven off.", false],
		[23, "", "Faced with rebellion, the colonists turn from administration to force. They mine supply routes, set ambushes and attack rebel stations.", false],
		[28, "", "They are also willing to buy loyalty. A bounty is enough to turn a rebel pilot against his own leader.", false],
		[2, "Notable members", "[[character/9]] is the colonist the pilot knows best: an arrogant official at [[station/0]] who looks down on the pilot and buys the catch at fixed prices.", false],
		[9, "", "Lesser officials appear as nameless [[character/6|colonists]], such as the official at [[station/1]] who pays poorly for the nautilus cull and then sets the pilot to work on the job board.", false],
		[23, "", "Jack rises to considerable power during the story, apparently thanks to good connections, and becomes the rebels' most determined enemy.", false],
		[28, "", "[[character/3]], a rebel pilot, secretly takes the colony's money and betrays his comrades.", false],
		[3, "Role in the story", "At first the colonists are simply the pilot's employers. Jack buys the catch and suggests that the pilot buy a gun and hunt pirates.", false],
		[7, "", "Next he orders a cull of [[species/0|nautiluses]] at [[station/1]], caring little whether they are caught or killed.", false],
		[10, "", "After a few contracts the colony trusts the pilot with the defence of a capsule launch at [[station/2]] against the Aquarians.", false],
		[12, "", "Jack blames the pilot for the capsules damaged in that fight and refuses any payment. Tired of the colony's treatment, the pilot goes looking for the resistance.", false],
		[18, "", "As the rebels win station after station by persuasion, the colony loses ground without a shot being fired.", false],
		[23, "", "Jack mines the rebel supply route between [[station/9]] and [[station/37]].", false],
		[25, "", "He then ambushes the rebel leader [[character/2]] on her way to negotiations at [[station/10]].", false],
		[28, "", "Jack's bounty sets Raymond against the rebels. Raymond kidnaps Ayumi, and the pilot rescues her.", false],
		[31, "", "The colonists have stolen [[world/abel|Professor Abel]]'s prototype [[world/biotek]]. The rebels intercept the convoy carrying it near Cho.", false],
		[37, "", "Jack sells an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]] station gun on the black market, never suspecting that it will be used against him.", false],
		[44, "", "The colonists launch a surprise attack on [[station/14]], the heart of the rebellion, but are routed when the Aquarians come to the rebels' aid. Jack flees the battle.", false],
		[46, "", "The first rebel offensive takes [[station/18]] from the colonists.", false],
		[47, "", "The second takes [[station/109]], and the colonists' hold on the south collapses.", false],
		[48, "", "[[station/0]], the last colonial stronghold, falls in the final battle. Jack escapes in a rescue capsule, and the colony's rule over the ocean ends.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "Colonist stations appear blue on the [[chart]]. They have no market and no factory: on docking they take the entire hold at its floor price. This makes them poor places to arrive with valuable cargo. Their shops and shipyards are open, but their yards sell only standard hulls. See [[guide/allegiance]].", false],
		[1, "", "Their job boards offer [[contract/0]], [[contract/1]], [[contract/2]], [[contract/3]], [[contract/4]], [[contract/5]], [[contract/7]], [[contract/8]], [[contract/9]], [[contract/10]] and [[contract/11]] contracts, but never deliveries or fish protection. In convoy work for the colonists the opposing ships are rebels.", false],
		[1, "", "On the other hand, a colonist station pays twice the rebel rate for pirates: the number destroyed on the trip, squared, times 100 credits. Patrol ships around colonist stations fight for the colony and help against pirates.", false],
		[12, "", "Stations leave the colony as the story advances, never through anything done in free play, and none ever returns. Once the pilot sides with the rebels, colonist ships become the main enemy of story missions.", false],
	],
	"faction/pirates": [
		[1, "", "Pirates are outlaws who raid shipping throughout the ocean. They serve neither the colony nor the rebels, attacking lone ships, convoys and anyone else who looks like easy prey. They are the pilot's most frequent opponents from the first armed outing to the end of the story and beyond.", false],
		[1, "History", "Piracy is already a fact of life when the pilot arrives. Stations pay bounties to anyone who destroys a pirate ship, and pirate hunting is a common trade among armed pilots.", false],
		[21, "", "The conflict between colonists and rebels is a gift to the pirates. With both sides distracted, they find more and easier victims, and the rebel leadership decides it can no longer ignore them.", false],
		[1, "Organisation", "Pirates operate in loose gangs rather than as a single organisation, and none of them is ever named in the story. Gangs gather in hideouts in open water, escort stolen ships and guard minefields and debris fields. Single pirates also lie in wait near stations for passing traffic.", false],
		[1, "Goals and methods", "Pirates want plunder. They attack ships for their cargo and gather wherever there is trouble, waiting for weakened ships to pick off.", false],
		[23, "", "They circle the colonial minefield near [[station/9]], hoping to pick off ships caught in it.", false],
		[35, "", "Hired guns move in the same circles. The bounty hunter [[character/11]] travels with an armed escort, and for bounty purposes his ship counts as a pirate.", false],
		[5, "Notable members", "Pirates appear in the story only as nameless [[character/10|pirate]] pilots, with no leader or famous captain.", false],
		[5, "Role in the story", "Pirates are the first targets of the pilot's new railgun. On [[character/1]]'s suggestion the pilot hunts down a pirate near [[station/1]].", false],
		[21, "", "Ayumi decides to stop merely defending the movement and strike at the pirates. She sends the pilot and [[character/3]] after a gang near [[station/8]].", false],
		[25, "", "Pirates are expected on the road to [[station/10]], though the real threat there turns out to be colonial.", false],
		[26, "", "Ordered to become a better fighter, the pilot turns to pirate hunting to raise the kill count.", false],
		[42, "", "A pirate hideout near [[station/17]] serves as the test range for the newly fitted [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]].", false],
		[43, "", "The pirates there are soon calling for reinforcements.", false],
		[47, "", "Even during the final offensives, pirates hold up [[character/5]] on her way to [[station/0]].", false],
		[1, "In the game", "Pirates are the standard enemy in open water. Their hulls grow tougher and their guns stronger as the pilot's rank rises and the story advances. They appear in [[contract/0]], [[contract/1]], [[contract/2]] and [[contract/3]] contracts and guard minefields and debris fields. See [[guide/combat]].", false],
		[1, "", "From [[chapter/12|chapter 12]] onward, up to three pirates may be waiting near a station when the pilot arrives, and the chance grows with each chapter. From [[chapter/16|chapter 16]], pirates and other hostile ships also use sudden bursts of speed.", false],
		[1, "", "Every pirate destroyed on a trip raises the bounty paid at the next station: the number destroyed, squared, times 100 credits at a colonist station or 50 at a rebel one. Long hunts therefore pay far better than short ones. Their wrecks sink slowly and can be harpooned for cargo before they fall too deep.", false],
		[1, "", "Pirates have no stations of their own, and no station ever goes over to them.", false],
	],
	"faction/rebels": [
		[1, "", "The rebels are one of the powers that can hold a station. Their nature and aims become clear as the story unfolds. See [[guide/allegiance]].", false],
		[12, "", "The rebels are a resistance movement that rises against colonial rule on the ocean planet. They begin as a secret network based at [[station/3]] and spread station by station. They want free trade and a sea that is used with care rather than plundered.", false],
		[12, "History", "The movement is already active when the pilot first hears of it, through radio traffic that M.A.I. picks up from Talon.", false],
		[13, "", "The rebels began encrypting their channels only days after that broadcast. Its members keep their plans to themselves and test newcomers before trusting them.", false],
		[18, "", "For a long time the movement grows by persuasion. Its agents gather intelligence about the stations and win over their leaders, and the news soon calls the movement a revolution. Pierre expects the peaceful phase not to last.", false],
		[26, "", "The ambush at [[station/10]] shows that diplomacy alone will not win the struggle, and the movement prepares to fight.", false],
		[31, "", "To overcome the colonists' greater numbers, the rebel leadership brings [[world/abel|Professor Abel]] and his team to [[station/14]], which becomes the movement's base of operations.", false],
		[13, "Organisation", "The movement is small and tightly organised. Recruits are vetted on arrival and told only what they need to know. Missions are passed down through a few trusted agents.", false],
		[18, "", "The movement's leader directs its progress from one of the newly won stations, and even its own members are vague about where.", false],
		[19, "", "The leader travels between stations to negotiate in person. Under her serve field agents, pilots and engineers.", false],
		[45, "", "By the end of the war the movement fields whole squads of fighting ships.", false],
		[13, "Goals and methods", "The rebels condemn the exploitation of the planet and its inhabitants and stand for free trade.", false],
		[15, "", "Their economy is the opposite of the colony's. They fish and harvest only what the sea can spare, make their catch into goods in their own factories and trade it at fair market prices, taking care to preserve biodiversity.", false],
		[16, "", "They prefer winning people over to fighting them. Agents scout the stations to find weak points that can be used to bring their populations round.", false],
		[21, "", "Their leader warns that the colony's exploitation will exhaust the planet as it has others. The movement aims to persuade influential people to join it before that happens. It also takes the fight to the pirates who profit from the chaos.", false],
		[31, "", "When force becomes unavoidable, the rebels set out to build a decisive weapon rather than match the colony ship for ship.", false],
		[12, "Notable members", "[[character/4]] screens newcomers at [[station/3]]. The rebels' radio operators, guards and convoy crews appear as [[character/7]].", false],
		[13, "", "[[character/5]] is a field agent who introduces the pilot to the movement's way of life.", false],
		[33, "", "Pierre later runs the hangar at [[station/14]], keeping the movement's ships ready.", false],
		[19, "", "[[character/2]] leads the movement. She is an older woman, although the pilot had assumed the leader was a man.", false],
		[21, "", "[[character/3]] is one of Ayumi's best pilots, skilled but arrogant.", false],
		[28, "", "Raymond turns traitor for a colonial bounty and kidnaps Ayumi.", false],
		[31, "", "[[world/abel|Professor Abel]] heads the scientific team behind the [[world/biotek]].", false],
		[45, "", "[[character/12]] leads a fighting squad that joins the pilot for the attack on [[station/18]].", false],
		[12, "Role in the story", "After falling out with the colony, the pilot seeks out the movement at Talon.", false],
		[13, "", "Pierre accepts the pilot on trial. The first task is to bring Leah from [[station/4]] to [[station/5]].", false],
		[16, "", "Further tasks follow: producing goods at rebel stations and scouting stations for the movement.", false],
		[19, "", "The pilot finally meets Ayumi and protects a school of squid from colonist hunters near [[station/7]].", false],
		[22, "", "Alongside Raymond, the pilot destroys a pirate gang near [[station/8]].", false],
		[24, "", "The pilot then clears a colonial minefield near [[station/9]].", false],
		[26, "", "At Sula the leadership is ambushed. Raymond's guns fail at the crucial moment, and Leah suspects sabotage.", false],
		[28, "", "Raymond lures the pilot into a trap with Ayumi as hostage, but the pilot destroys his ship and recovers her alive.", false],
		[31, "", "The movement launches a plan in three steps, beginning with the recovery of the stolen [[world/biotek]].", false],
		[33, "", "The second step is to obtain an [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]] gun, which the pilot buys through a black-market dealer.", false],
		[39, "", "The third is to build the incubator that completes the generator, at [[station/16]].", false],
		[44, "", "The weapon is finished just before the colonists attack Fiir. The pilot's Eclipse and the sudden help of the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] save the station.", false],
		[45, "", "The Aquarians pledge their support, and the movement plans its final offensives with them as allies.", false],
		[47, "", "Two offensives follow. Leah takes [[station/109]] and the pilot takes [[station/18]].", false],
		[48, "", "The war ends with the fall of [[station/0]], and the movement holds every station on the planet.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "Rebel stations appear orange on the [[chart]]. They run free markets and factories, so goods can be bought, sold and made there; see [[guide/trading]]. Their shipyards sell every hull in its upgraded version. Patrols around rebel stations fight for the rebels.", false],
		[1, "", "Their job boards offer [[contract/0]], [[contract/1]], [[contract/2]], [[contract/3]], [[contract/6]], [[contract/8]], [[contract/9]], [[contract/10]], [[contract/13]] and [[contract/14]] contracts. They never post escorts, convoy raids, fish hunts or capsule work, and they stop posting fish protection once the story is complete. When rebel contracts involve fighting other ships, the opponents are colonists or pirates.", false],
		[1, "", "A rebel station pays half the colonist rate for pirates: the number destroyed on the trip, squared, times 50 credits.", false],
		[12, "", "Stations join the rebels as the story advances, a few at a time at first and then dozens at once. A station that joins never returns to the colony. This page lists the stations that join in each chapter you have reached.", false],
	],
	"goods/18": [
		[1, "", "What remains of a creature killed instead of harpooned. A carcass floats up and can be towed in with the harpoon for a third of the animal's mass in meat. It sells for 12 to 31 cr and is mainly used, with [[species/14]], to make [[goods/19]]. Its home station is [[station/68]].", false],
	],
	"goods/19": [
		[1, "", "A basic product made from [[goods/18]] and [[species/14]], one of each per unit. Any factory can make it. It sells from 135 cr at [[station/78]] up to 360 cr at [[station/27]], and it is an ingredient of [[goods/30]] and [[goods/36]].", false],
	],
	"goods/20": [
		[1, "", "Tanned hide from [[species/3]], cured with [[goods/33]]. Its home is [[station/164]], and it fetches the most, up to 1,062 cr, at [[station/172]]. It is also half of the recipe for [[goods/29]], so a manta catch can be turned into one of the dearest consumer goods.", false],
	],
	"goods/21": [
		[1, "", "A dish of raw fish and seaweed, made from [[species/4]] and [[species/17]]. Both ingredients are common, cheap catches, which makes sushi an easy first product. It sells from 420 cr at [[station/76]] up to 996 cr at [[station/179]].", false],
	],
	"goods/22": [
		[1, "", "A water-resistant dye made from [[species/10]] and [[goods/35]]. It sells from 750 cr at [[station/54]] up to 1,872 cr at [[station/27]]. It is also one of the two ingredients of [[goods/29]].", false],
	],
	"goods/23": [
		[1, "", "Cuts of [[species/7]], preserved with [[goods/33]]. Because the marlin is hard to land, steak is a premium product: it sells from 456 cr at [[station/109]] up to 1,260 cr at [[station/19]].", false],
	],
	"goods/24": [
		[1, "", "A luminous compound extracted from [[species/5]] and [[species/1]] with [[goods/35]]. It sells from 850 cr at [[station/83]] up to 2,500 cr at [[station/179]]. It is a key input for [[goods/32]], [[goods/41]] and [[goods/36]].", false],
	],
	"goods/25": [
		[1, "", "A protein extracted from [[species/6]] and [[species/8]], processed with [[goods/33]]. It sells from 900 cr at [[station/23]] up to 2,600 cr at [[station/27]], and it is the base of [[goods/31]].", false],
	],
	"goods/26": [
		[1, "", "A blended fish dish made from [[species/8]], [[species/4]] and [[species/2]]. It has the highest floor price of any food, from 1,916 cr at [[station/196]] up to 3,030 cr at [[station/179]], but it needs three different catches.", false],
	],
	"goods/27": [
		[1, "", "Pharmaceuticals made from [[species/1]], [[species/13]], [[species/16]] and [[goods/33]]. It sells from 930 cr at [[station/151]] up to 2,316 cr at [[station/179]] and is one of the ingredients of [[goods/41]]. Markets seldom stock it.", false],
	],
	"goods/28": [
		[1, "", "A processed biomass that needs one of each of the five algae: [[species/13]], [[species/14]], [[species/15]], [[species/16]] and [[species/17]]. It sells from 550 cr at [[station/74]] up to 2,700 cr at [[station/19]], the widest spread of any basic product, and it feeds into [[goods/36]].", false],
	],
	"goods/29": [
		[1, "", "Garments made from [[goods/20]] and [[goods/22]], so the chain runs back to [[species/3]] and [[species/10]]. It sells from 1,626 cr at [[station/175]] up to 3,420 cr at [[station/172]]. Markets rarely stock it.", false],
	],
	"goods/30": [
		[1, "", "A portable power source assembled from [[goods/34]] and [[goods/19]]. It sells from 1,150 cr at [[station/78]] up to 1,900 cr at [[station/27]]. It is rarely found in markets, so making it is usually the only way to get it.", false],
	],
	"goods/31": [
		[1, "", "Beauty products made from [[goods/25]] and [[goods/35]]. They sell from 1,536 cr at [[station/23]] up to 3,840 cr at [[station/27]]. Markets rarely stock them, so a pilot who makes collagen can go one step further and earn considerably more.", false],
	],
	"goods/32": [
		[1, "", "A lamp that imitates natural daylight, built from [[goods/34]] and [[goods/24]]. It sells from 2,490 cr at [[station/83]] up to 5,622 cr at [[station/179]], making it the most valuable product that any factory can make from catches and imports.", false],
	],
	"goods/33": [
		[1, "", "A by-product of desalination, cheap and widely stocked at 20 to 36 cr. It is not made from catches, but it is needed for [[goods/20]], [[goods/23]], [[goods/25]] and [[goods/27]]. Delivery contracts never ask for it.", false],
		[12, "", "Rebel markets restock it between visits, since it is imported.", false],
	],
	"goods/34": [
		[1, "", "Imported components, widely stocked at 193 to 297 cr. They are not made locally, but they are needed for [[goods/30]], [[goods/32]], [[goods/41]] and [[goods/36]]. Delivery contracts never ask for them.", false],
		[12, "", "Rebel markets restock electronics between visits, since they are imported.", false],
	],
	"goods/35": [
		[1, "", "An imported chemical, widely stocked at 55 to 123 cr. It is needed for [[goods/22]], [[goods/24]] and [[goods/31]]. Delivery contracts never ask for it.", false],
		[12, "", "Rebel markets restock it between visits, since it is imported.", false],
	],
	"goods/36": [
		[1, "", "A laboratory apparatus for copying genetic material. It needs [[goods/19]], [[goods/34]], [[goods/28]] and [[goods/24]], and only a factory of tech level 11 can assemble it. Markets never stock it.", false],
		[39, "", "The campaign requires the pilot to obtain one from [[chapter/39]] onwards.", false],
	],
	"goods/37": [
		[1, "", "An imported drug regarded as a growing social problem. It is not made from catches, and markets never stock it.", false],
		[34, "", "In [[chapter/34]] the pilot must deliver a consignment to a contact as part of a deal.", false],
	],
	"goods/38": [
		[1, "", "A crated heavy station gun, valued at 89,000 cr. It cannot be bought in markets. Once delivered and installed, it becomes the weapon [[equipment/42]].", false],
		[37, "", "It is the object of the pilot's search from [[chapter/33]], and the pilot carries one in [[chapter/37]].", false],
	],
	"goods/39": [
		[1, "", "An imported folding shovel. Its price swings from 9 cr to 999 cr depending on how far it travels, which makes it a speculative cargo. No factory makes it, and delivery contracts never ask for it. Its home station is [[station/89]].", false],
	],
	"goods/40": [
		[1, "", "A figurine of a celebrated hero, sold to collectors. It ranges from 999 cr up to 9,999 cr, from [[station/90]] to [[station/19]], the steepest rise of any trade good. It is uncommon in markets, and delivery contracts never ask for it.", false],
	],
	"goods/41": [
		[1, "", "Microscopic medical robots built from [[goods/27]], [[goods/24]] and [[goods/34]]. They are among the most valuable goods, selling from 4,315 cr at [[station/76]] up to 11,972 cr at [[station/179]], and markets almost never stock them.", false],
	],
	"guide/allegiance": [
		[1, "Two kinds of station", "Every station is held by one of two sides, shown by its colour on the [[chart]]: blue for the [[faction/colonists]] and orange for the [[faction/rebels]]. At the start of the story every station is colonist.", false],
		[1, "", "A station's side changes what it offers:", false],
		[1, "", "Trade: colonist stations have no market and no workshop. They buy the whole hold on arrival at floor price. Rebel stations trade and manufacture freely; see [[guide/trading]].", true],
		[1, "", "Contracts: each side posts its own selection of contract kinds; see [[guide/contracts]].", true],
		[1, "", "Bounties: a pirate bounty pays twice as much at a colonist station as at a rebel one.", true],
		[1, "", "Ships: rebel shipyards sell each hull in its upgraded version.", true],
		[1, "", "Patrols: the ships circling a station fight for its side.", true],
		[1, "", "Docking, the equipment shop, the ship dealer and the job board are open to the pilot at both kinds of station.", false],
		[1, "How allegiance changes", "Stations change sides only as the story advances, never through what the pilot does in free play. Once a station joins the rebels it stays with them. The [[faction/rebels]] page lists the stations that join in each chapter you have reached.", false],
		[12, "", "The first rebel station is [[station/3]], already with the resistance when the pilot first hears of it. [[station/4]] follows in [[chapter/12|chapter 12]].", false],
		[14, "", "Through the following chapters stations go over one or a few at a time.", false],
		[22, "", "From [[chapter/22|chapter 22]] the movement grows much faster, with eight or more stations joining at once.", false],
		[46, "", "By the closing chapters the great majority of the chart is orange.", false],
		[12, "What it means for the pilot", "As more stations change sides, markets and workshops open across the chart and fishing stops being the only way to earn a living. Colonist stations become places to avoid with a valuable hold, but good places to collect a pirate bounty.", false],
	],
	"guide/basics": [
		[1, "Your first ship", "The pilot starts at [[station/0]] with 15,000 credits and an [[ship/0]], a small fishing submarine. It comes fitted with a [[equipment/15]] harpoon, a [[equipment/18]], [[equipment/22]] and a [[equipment/35]]. It carries no guns.", false],
		[1, "", "The first chapters are a guided introduction. [[character/1]], the ship's computer, explains each new task as it comes up: catching a fish, docking, fitting equipment and travelling. The current objective is always in the mission journal.", false],
		[1, "Piloting", "You can pilot the submarine with keyboard and mouse, a gamepad or touch controls, and every key can be rebound in Settings. Throttle changes in quarter steps, and Stop halts the ship. Side thrusters slide the submarine sideways even when it is stopped. Boost works only with a booster fitted. Camera cycles through four views.", false],
		[1, "", "Autopilot opens a list of destinations: the current objective, the local dock, the local S.T.R.E.A.M. gate or any station on the chart. Steering, throttle or weapons hand control back to you. See [[guide/navigation]].", false],
		[1, "Docking", "To dock, steer into the station's docking area and use Dock, or pick the dock from the autopilot list. Docking is locked while a mission encounter in the area is unfinished. Leaving a station always restores hull, shield and armour to full, free of charge.", false],
		[1, "Station menus", "A docked station offers six services:", false],
		[1, "", "Hangar: the equipment shop, the ship dealer, the workshop for making goods, and a view of your ship.", true],
		[1, "", "Missions: the journal of current objectives and the station's job board.", true],
		[1, "", "Map: the station chart, for planning routes and finding where species live.", true],
		[1, "", "Trade: the market for buying and selling cargo.", true],
		[1, "", "Status: your ship, cargo, pilot rank, medals and statistics.", true],
		[1, "", "System: saving, settings, help and this reference.", true],
		[1, "", "Some services open later in the story. The station Map opens in [[chapter/7|chapter 7]] and the job board in [[chapter/9|chapter 9]]. Stations held by the [[faction/colonists]] never trade; see [[guide/trading]].", false],
		[1, "Saving", "You can save only while docked. Every arrival at a station writes an automatic checkpoint, and the System menu offers three more save slots. Returning to the main menu from a station saves first. If the hull is destroyed, play resumes from the last station save, so dock regularly on long trips.", false],
		[1, "", "Transfer save, also in the System menu, exports your last station save to a file and imports it on another device.", false],
		[1, "Help", "Help in the System menu explains each station screen. At sea, the pause menu holds the chart, the mission journal, ship status, medals and settings. M.A.I. comments once on each new situation, such as the first gate or the first dive outside the safe depth band. These hints can be turned off in the gameplay settings.", false],
		[1, "", "Next, read [[guide/depth]] before travelling far, and [[guide/fishing]] for your first income.", false],
	],
	"guide/combat": [
		[1, "Weapons", "Guns come in two families. Projectile weapons, such as the [[equipment/0]], [[equipment/6]] and [[equipment/9]], fire streams of shots. Beam weapons, the coil guns from the [[equipment/3]] upward, hit hard at long range but recharge slowly. Several guns can be fitted, and they fire together from mounts spread across the bow.", false],
		[1, "", "Homing torpedoes, from the [[equipment/12]] to the [[equipment/14]], run straight for a second and a half, then turn towards the nearest live target within about 150 m. Only one torpedo launcher can be fitted. Each weapon page gives damage, range and firing interval.", false],
		[1, "Firing", "The guns and torpedoes form one bank and the harpoon another. Fire uses the selected bank, and Bank switches between them. With a mouse, the left button fires the guns and the right the harpoon; on a gamepad, the right trigger fires the guns and the left the harpoon.", false],
		[1, "", "Auto fire keeps the selected bank firing until you switch it off. It also ends the autopilot.", false],
		[1, "Protection", "Incoming fire drains the shield first, then the armour, then the hull. When the hull reaches zero the ship is lost, and play resumes from the last station save.", false],
		[1, "", "Shields with passive recharge regenerate at sea. Armour does not, and the hull recovers only with a [[equipment/43]]. Leaving a station restores all three. A booster such as the [[equipment/39]] gives a short burst of speed to close in or break away, then needs time to recharge.", false],
		[1, "Enemies", "[[faction/pirates]] are the usual enemy. Their hulls get tougher and their guns stronger as your pilot rank and the story advance. From [[chapter/12|chapter 12]] onward, up to three may be waiting near a station.", false],
		[1, "", "Mission encounters bring their own opponents: wanted ships, gangs guarding a disabled ship, convoy guards and minefields. See [[guide/contracts]].", false],
		[10, "", "Aquarians attack colonist transport capsules. They fight with weapons of their own, and they leave no wreck when destroyed.", false],
		[1, "Bounties and loot", "Every pirate destroyed on a trip raises the bounty paid when you next dock. The bounty is the number of pirates squared, times 100 credits at a colonist station or 50 at a rebel one. Four pirates in one trip bring 1,600 credits at a colonist station.", false],
		[1, "", "Destroyed ships leave a wreck with cargo to salvage with the harpoon. Kills count towards [[medal/4]], pirates towards [[medal/14]], and many kills in one trip towards [[medal/9]].", false],
		[1, "", "Keep guns away from fish you want to catch: a wounded creature flees, and one on the harpoon is lost if hit.", false],
	],
	"guide/contracts": [
		[1, "The job board", "From [[chapter/9|chapter 9]], each station's Missions service has a job board of one to eight contracts. Each shows its sponsor, a deposit paid on acceptance and the fee paid on success. You can hold one contract at a time alongside the story objective. The deposit is not refunded, so abandoning or failing a contract costs it.", false],
		[1, "Contract kinds", "Contracts come in fifteen kinds:", false],
		[1, "", "[[contract/0]]: destroy one hardened ship waiting near the target station.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/1]] and [[contract/2]]: disable a ship travelling with a pirate gang, then harpoon it before it escapes.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/3]]: destroy every member of a pirate gang.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/4]]: protect a convoy of freighters from raiders; losing the whole convoy fails the job.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/5]]: destroy the freighters of a guarded convoy.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/6]]: guard a school of creatures from hunters; losing too many fails the job.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/7]]: catch or kill a number of one species.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/8]]: carry a passenger to another station, sometimes with a limit on stops along the way.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/9]]: destroy floating debris within about two and a half minutes.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/10]]: destroy a guarded minefield.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/11]]: defend transport capsules during a launch.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/12]]: destroy transport capsules. It occurs only in the story, never on a job board.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/13]] and [[contract/14]]: deliver a quantity of a product, or of one species, to the station that posted the job.", true],
		[1, "Who posts what", "[[faction/colonists|Colonist]] stations never post [[contract/6]], [[contract/12]], [[contract/13]] or [[contract/14]]. [[faction/rebels|Rebel]] stations never post [[contract/4]], [[contract/5]], [[contract/7]], [[contract/11]] or [[contract/12]], and they stop posting [[contract/6]] once the story is complete. Delivery contracts are never posted at the twenty-one stations that open the game's station table, from [[station/0]] to [[station/20]].", false],
		[1, "Destinations and pay", "Most contracts lead to another station, usually one within your depth band and within a distance that grows as the story advances. The fee starts at 3,500 credits and rises with difficulty and distance. [[contract/9]] pays less; escorts, fish defence and capsule defence pay more. A passenger who allows fewer stops pays extra. Delivery contracts ask for half the usual deposit.", false],
		[1, "", "Difficulty scales with your pilot rank. Rank rises as you catch creatures, destroy enemies, make goods and complete contracts, so better-paid jobs also bring tougher opposition.", false],
		[1, "Running a contract", "On reaching the target area, the encounter begins. The autopilot's objective option steers to its waypoints. Docking and the gate stay locked until the encounter is resolved, and failure ends the contract. Deliveries are settled when you dock with the goods.", false],
		[1, "", "Each completed contract counts towards [[medal/12]].", false],
	],
	"guide/depth": [
		[1, "Depth in DEEP", "Every station sits at a fixed depth, shown with its tech level on its page. Most lie between 15,000 and 30,000 m. [[station/28]] is the highest at 13,500 m and [[station/81]] the deepest at 32,250 m. The ocean has no floor to stop a dive: a submarine can sink as far as its hull survives.", false],
		[1, "The safe band", "A ship is safe only inside a band of depths set by two pieces of equipment. The shield sets the shallow limit, the highest the ship may rise. The armour sets the deep limit, the lowest it may sink. With neither fitted, the band is just 22,000 to 23,000 m.", false],
		[1, "", "The [[ship/0]] starts with a [[equipment/18]] rated to 20,000 m and [[equipment/22]] rated to 25,000 m, so its band runs from 20,000 to 25,000 m. Better shields let the ship climb higher and better armour lets it dive deeper. Each equipment page gives its rating, and each station page says which rating reaches it. Only one shield and one armour can be fitted.", false],
		[1, "Outside the band", "Crossing a limit sets off a warning. After five seconds outside the band, the ship takes damage several times a second. Too deep, the pressure drains the shield; too high, it drains the armour. Once that layer is empty, the damage goes straight to the hull.", false],
		[1, "", "Each hit is small at first. It grows by one point for every 2,000 m below 30,000 m or above 15,000 m, so extreme depths wear a ship down quickly.", false],
		[1, "", "The five-second grace allows short excursions, for example to chase a catch or pass a station. Time acceleration is limited to 2× while the ship is outside its band.", false],
		[1, "Recovering", "Shields with passive recharge regenerate only while the ship is below their shallow limit. Armour does not recover at sea, and the hull only recovers with a [[equipment/43]]. All three are restored when the ship leaves a station.", false],
		[1, "Planning trips", "Autopilot and the S.T.R.E.A.M. will still take you to a station outside your band, after a warning, but the arrival costs protection. Job board contracts usually point to stations within your band, but not always. Check the destination's depth before accepting a job, and refit if needed; see [[guide/fitting]].", false],
		[1, "Depth records", "The highest and lowest points you reach are recorded. [[medal/21]] rewards climbing above 15,000, 12,000 and 10,000 m, and another medal rewards dives below 30,000, 32,000 and 35,000 m. See [[guide/medals]].", false],
	],
	"guide/fishing": [
		[1, "The harpoon", "Fishing uses the harpoon, which forms its own weapon bank, separate from the guns. Every pilot starts with a [[equipment/15]]; the [[equipment/16]] and [[equipment/17]] are better. A harpoon has three figures: range, capture tolerance (how close a shot must pass to hook its target) and slowing (how much it paralyses the catch). Only one harpoon can be fitted.", false],
		[1, "", "Fire the harpoon with its own control: the right mouse button or the left trigger. You can also select the harpoon bank with Bank and use Fire.", false],
		[1, "Landing a catch", "A hit hooks the creature, which flees and thrashes. Keep it in front of the ship until it tires. Every creature fights for at least 2.1 seconds, and tougher species fight longer; each species page gives the struggle time. A harpoon with more slowing keeps a fleeing catch from running far.", false],
		[1, "", "The catch is lost if it is hurt, for example by your own guns, or if it leaves your view before it gives up. Pressing the harpoon control again lets a hooked catch go.", false],
		[1, "", "Once subdued, the creature is reeled in and taken aboard. It fills the hold by its mass in tonnes, and an individual can weigh a quarter more or less than its species' figure. If the hold is too full, the creature stays in the water.", false],
		[1, "Algae", "The five algae, from [[species/17]] to [[species/13]], stand still on the sea bed. They give up almost at once, which makes them the easiest catch.", false],
		[1, "Carcasses and wrecks", "A creature killed by gunfire leaves a carcass that floats upwards. Harpooned, it yields [[goods/18]] weighing about a third of the animal.", false],
		[1, "", "Destroyed ships leave a wreck holding one to seven units of some cargo. Harpoon the wreck to take the cargo aboard. Each salvaged wreck counts towards [[medal/18]].", false],
		[1, "Selling the catch", "At a [[faction/colonists|colonist]] station, the whole hold is bought on arrival at the floor price. At a [[faction/rebels|rebel]] station, fish and algae can be sold at market prices or used as ingredients in the workshop. See [[guide/trading]].", false],
		[1, "", "Cargo modules raise the hold's capacity; see [[guide/fitting]]. [[contract/14]] contracts pay for delivering a number of one species, and [[contract/7]] contracts ask for a number caught or killed.", false],
		[1, "", "Each catch counts towards [[medal/5]], and many catches in one trip towards [[medal/10]]. Catching every fish species earns [[medal/3]], and every alga [[medal/2]].", false],
	],
	"guide/fitting": [
		[1, "Slots", "Every hull has a fixed number of equipment slots, from six on the [[ship/0]] to ten on the largest hulls, and each item takes one slot. Guns and cargo modules can be fitted several times. Every other kind, torpedo launchers included, is limited to one per ship.", false],
		[1, "Equipment kinds", "Guns and torpedoes: see [[guide/combat]].", true],
		[1, "", "Harpoons: catch creatures and salvage wrecks; see [[guide/fishing]].", true],
		[1, "", "Shields: absorb damage first and set the shallow depth limit. Some recharge at sea.", true],
		[1, "", "Armour: absorbs damage after the shield and sets the deep depth limit; see [[guide/depth]].", true],
		[1, "", "Cargo modules: add a percentage of the hull's base cargo capacity. They stack.", true],
		[1, "", "Engines: extend S.T.R.E.A.M. reach by their percentage.", true],
		[1, "", "Steering units: make the ship turn faster.", true],
		[1, "", "Radar units: the ship's sensors.", true],
		[1, "", "Boosters: a burst of extra speed for a few seconds, followed by a recharge.", true],
		[1, "", "Repair system: the [[equipment/43]] slowly repairs the hull at sea.", true],
		[1, "Buying and selling", "A station's shop offers two to five items, drawn by rarity; rarer items turn up more often as the story advances. Prices fall with tech level: a tech 10 station sells at the bottom of an item's price range, a tech 1 station near the top.", false],
		[1, "", "Equipment sells back for a fifth less than the station's shop price. Items that have been owned before are also sold on a fifth cheaper. A cargo module cannot be sold while the hold would no longer fit its contents.", false],
		[1, "", "Two stations are special. [[station/28]] sells every piece of equipment and [[station/81]] every hull, but no shield or armour reaches either depth safely.", false],
		[1, "Repairs", "There is no repair bill. Hull, shield and armour are restored to full whenever the ship leaves a station.", false],
		[1, "Buying a ship", "The ship dealer offers up to three hulls, and bigger hulls appear only as the story progresses. A station's tech level takes that many percent off a hull's price. Your current ship is traded in at four fifths of its list price.", false],
		[1, "", "All equipment and cargo move to the new hull, so it needs enough slots and hold space for them. Rebel shipyards sell each hull in its upgraded version. The [[ship/10]] has a huge list price but sells for 50,000 credits once every medal has been won; see [[guide/medals]].", false],
	],
	"guide/help": [
		[1, "", "The submarine can be piloted with a keyboard and mouse, a gamepad or a touch screen, and all three can be mixed. The keys below are the defaults; every key used at sea can be changed under Settings › Controls › Key bindings, and Settings › Controls › Control reference shows the current set at any time.", false],
		[1, "Keyboard and mouse", "Moving the mouse turns and pitches the ship, and the camera follows it. W and S raise and lower the throttle a quarter at a time; A and D slide the ship sideways (or turn it, if the steering setting says so), and the arrow keys pitch it up and down. Hold Alt or Ctrl to look around with the mouse without steering.", false],
		[1, "", "Left mouse button: fire the guns.", true],
		[1, "", "Right mouse button: fire the harpoon.", true],
		[1, "", "Space: fire the selected weapon group; Tab switches between the combat and fishing groups.", true],
		[1, "", "Q: automatic fire on or off.", true],
		[1, "", "Shift: boost, if a booster is fitted.", true],
		[1, "", "E: dock at the station ahead, or enter the [[world/stream|S.T.R.E.A.M.]] at a gate.", true],
		[1, "", "R: tap for the autopilot menu, hold to head straight for the current objective.", true],
		[1, "", "T: speed up time; faster steps are offered while the autopilot steers and nothing dangerous is near.", true],
		[1, "", "M: the map. C: change camera. L: headlights (with Enhanced lighting).", true],
		[1, "", "Esc: pause, or go back from any menu. F5 saves while docked; F11 switches full screen.", true],
		[1, "", "Under Settings › Controls › Steering the mouse wheel can be made to step the throttle instead; S then stops the ship at once.", false],
		[1, "Gamepad", "By default both sticks steer: the left stick slides or turns and pitches, the right stick turns and pitches. Each stick axis can be given another role, including throttle or camera, under Settings › Controls › Gamepad, where pitch inversion, vibration and the dead zone are set too.", false],
		[1, "", "D-pad up and down: throttle. A: fire the selected weapon group. RT: guns. LT: harpoon. Left stick click: boost.", true],
		[1, "", "X: switch weapon group. Y: dock or enter the S.T.R.E.A.M. LB: autopilot menu. RB: speed up time.", true],
		[1, "", "View: map. D-pad left: tap to change camera, hold to look with the right stick. D-pad right: headlights.", true],
		[1, "", "Start or B: pause. In menus, A selects and B goes back.", true],
		[1, "Touch", "Touch controls switch on by themselves when the screen is touched, or can be set on or off under Settings › Controls › Touch controls. A steering stick appears under the left thumb; dragging anywhere else looks around. The large GUNS button fires while held and has the throttle around its edge: slide along the arc to set the speed. HOOK fires the harpoon, BOOST boosts, and a DOCK button pulses when docking is possible. Double-tap GUNS or HOOK for automatic fire, and tap again to stop.", false],
		[1, "", "TIME, ROUTE (the autopilot), MAP and PAUSE sit at the edges of the screen; the camera and full screen are in the pause menu. Every button can be moved and resized with Adjust control placement, and the ship can also be steered by tilting the device.", false],
		[1, "Station screens", "Docking saves the game automatically and opens the station's hub, which shows whether the station belongs to the colonists or the rebels, its technology level and the pilot's credits. Arrow keys or the D-pad move between buttons, Enter or A opens one, and Esc or B goes back. DEPART returns to the water.", false],
		[1, "", "Hangar: the equipment shop (buy and fit, or sell what is fitted), the ship dealer with a turnable model of each hull and a trade-in price for the current one, the workshop for making goods at rebel stations, and an overview of the ship and its cargo. See [[guide/fitting]].", true],
		[1, "", "Missions: the current objectives and journal, and the job board of contracts once it opens. A contract costs a deposit, which is lost if it is abandoned. See [[guide/contracts]].", true],
		[1, "", "Map: the chart of stations, with search, a depth side view and buttons to set the autopilot or plan a S.T.R.E.A.M. transfer. See [[guide/navigation]].", true],
		[1, "", "Trade: buy and sell goods; only rebel stations trade. See [[guide/trading]].", true],
		[1, "", "Status: the ship, the pilot's statistics and the medals. See [[guide/medals]].", true],
		[1, "", "System: three save slots, settings, help, this reference, save transfer, reloading the station checkpoint and the main menu.", true],
	],
	"guide/medals": [
		[1, "Earning medals", "Medals record a pilot's achievements. Most come in bronze, silver and gold; the rest are single awards. Progress is checked each time the ship docks, and new medals are announced at the station. A medal once won is never lost, and a better grade replaces a lower one. Your profile under Status shows the medals won so far.", false],
		[1, "Career medals", "These count over the whole career (bronze, silver, gold):", false],
		[1, "", "[[medal/4]]: enemies destroyed, 50, 200 and 400.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/5]]: creatures caught, 50, 150 and 300.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/6]]: stations docked at, 50, 100 and 200.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/8]]: goods made, 50, 250 and 500.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/12]]: contracts completed, 10, 25 and 50.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/13]]: arrivals in a new station area, 50, 150 and 300.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/14]]: pirates destroyed, 25, 150 and 350.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/18]]: wrecks salvaged, 25, 150 and 350.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/19]]: credits held at one time, 125,000, 500,000 and 1,000,000.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/20]]: new pieces of equipment bought, 5, 10 and 20.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/11]]: hours played, 5, 7 and 10.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/21]]: highest point reached, 15,000, 12,000 and 10,000 m.", true],
		[1, "Single-trip medals", "These judge a single trip, from leaving one station to docking at the next:", false],
		[1, "", "[[medal/9]]: kills in one trip, 5, 10 and 15.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/10]]: catches in one trip, 5, 10 and 15.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/1]]: docking with the hull below 30%, 15% and 5%.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/16]]: weapons fitted when docking, 3, 5 and 8.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/15]]: docking with no guns fitted. The opening chapters do not count.", true],
		[1, "Awards", "[[medal/0]] is given on arrival in the colony.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/3]] and [[medal/2]]: catch every fish species and every alga at least once.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/7]]: make every product at least once.", true],
		[1, "", "[[medal/17]]: complete the story.", true],
		[1, "", "Two more medals exist. One rewards dives below 30,000, 32,000 and 35,000 m. The other is awarded for holding every other medal, and that full set also cuts the price of the [[ship/10]] to 50,000 credits. See [[guide/depth]] and [[guide/fitting]].", false],
	],
	"guide/navigation": [
		[1, "The chart", "The two hundred stations lie on a square chart. The [[chart]] in this reference shows each one in its owner's colour. In the game, the station Map service and the map at sea show the same chart, with your recent route, mission destinations and the stations within S.T.R.E.A.M. reach. Each station page lists its nearest neighbours with their chart distance and depth.", false],
		[1, "Autopilot", "Autopilot steers the ship for you. Its list offers the current objective, docking at the local station, the local S.T.R.E.A.M. gate, or any station chosen on the chart. Over long distances it cruises through open water, steers around stations in its way and passes from one station's area into the next.", false],
		[1, "", "Any steering, throttle, boost or weapon input returns manual control, as does Disengage in the autopilot list. Turning on auto fire also ends the autopilot.", false],
		[1, "", "Time acceleration runs at 1× or 2× under manual control and up to 16× on autopilot. Danger drops it back to 2×: a hostile ship within about 550 m, a catch on the harpoon, or leaving the safe depth band. It also returns to 1× on entering a new station's area.", false],
		[1, "S.T.R.E.A.M. gates", "Each station has a S.T.R.E.A.M. gate some distance out. Steer into the gate's activation area and use Dock to open the gate chart, which lists every station in reach. Choose one and start the transit. The gate opens and the ship comes out at the destination station's gate, with its damage unchanged.", false],
		[1, "", "A gate reaches about a sixth of the chart's width from the current station. An engine extends this by its rating: an engine rated +30% reaches 30% further. Stations beyond reach need several hops, or a long cruise on autopilot.", false],
		[1, "", "While a mission encounter in the area is unfinished, neither the gate nor the station's dock can be used.", false],
		[1, "Arriving", "Entering a station's area starts any mission encounter waiting there. The autopilot's objective option leads to the encounter's waypoints, and docking unlocks once it is resolved. See [[guide/contracts]].", false],
		[1, "", "Otherwise the water around a station holds its local wildlife and patrol ships. From [[chapter/12|chapter 12]] onward, a few pirates may be lying in wait, more often as the story goes on. See [[guide/combat]].", false],
		[1, "", "Each arrival in a new station's area counts towards [[medal/13]], and each station docked at for the first time towards [[medal/6]].", false],
	],
	"guide/tips": [
		[1, "", "Short pieces of advice for travelling, fighting and earning, gathered for this reference.", false],
		[1, "Underway", "Holding the autopilot key (R) heads straight for the current objective; tapping it offers other destinations, such as the nearest S.T.R.E.A.M. gate or a station picked on the chart.", true],
		[1, "", "Time can be sped up much further while the autopilot is steering than under manual control, as long as no enemy is near.", true],
		[1, "", "Any steering, throttle or weapon input takes the controls back from the autopilot.", true],
		[1, "", "Boost only works with a booster fitted, and it needs time to recharge after each use.", true],
		[1, "", "The chart marks stations outside the hull's depth limits; a stronger hull or shield opens them up. See [[guide/depth]].", true],
		[1, "", "Free look (Alt or Ctrl with the mouse, or holding D-pad left) checks behind the ship without changing course.", true],
		[1, "Fighting and fishing", "Guns and harpoon fire on separate buttons, so a fish can be hooked in the middle of a fight.", true],
		[1, "", "Automatic fire keeps the selected weapons firing while both hands steer; it stops when a menu opens.", true],
		[1, "", "The pause menu's Action freeze stops the scene so the camera can be moved around it freely.", true],
		[1, "Trading and money", "Only rebel stations have a market or a workshop; colonist stations still sell equipment.", true],
		[1, "", "The workshop turns cargo into products; check the recipe's price range against the market before making a whole hold.", true],
		[1, "", "A new ship is cheaper than it looks: the current one is taken in part exchange.", true],
		[1, "", "A contract's deposit is lost if the contract is abandoned, so check its depth and distance first.", true],
		[1, "Saving", "Docking saves automatically, and the System screen has three more slots. F5 saves while docked.", true],
		[1, "", "Reload station checkpoint returns to the last docking if a dive goes badly.", true],
		[1, "", "Headlights need the Enhanced lighting setting; with it, L switches them on and off.", true],
	],
	"guide/trading": [
		[1, "Where trade happens", "Only [[faction/rebels|rebel]] stations run a market and a workshop. [[faction/colonists|Colonist]] stations buy the entire hold on arrival, each item at its floor price, and sell nothing. At the start of the story every station is a colonist one, so fishing at fixed prices is the only income until markets open. See [[guide/allegiance]].", false],
		[1, "Prices", "Every good has a price range, a home station and a best market, all shown on its page. At its home station a good sells at its floor price. The further a station lies from the home station on the chart, the higher the price, up to the ceiling at the distance of the best market and beyond.", false],
		[1, "", "Three imports work the other way: [[goods/33]], [[goods/34]] and [[goods/35]] are cheapest at their best market and dearer the further away you go. Rebel markets restock them between visits.", false],
		[1, "", "A market buys and sells at the same price, one unit at a time. Profit comes from carrying goods between stations, not from the counter.", false],
		[1, "What markets stock", "A market stocks up to four products. Each must be within the station's tech level, and products appear more readily near their home station. Shelves shrink a little between visits. The three stations visited most recently keep their stock; elsewhere the shelves are drawn afresh when you return.", false],
		[1, "Manufacturing", "The workshop in the hangar turns ingredients in the hold into products. A station can make any product up to its own tech level. The recipe list shows how many of each product the hold can make; each product page lists its ingredients. For example, one [[goods/21]] takes one [[species/4]] and one [[species/17]].", false],
		[1, "", "Making goods counts towards [[medal/8]], and making every product at least once earns [[medal/7]].", false],
		[1, "Making a profit", "Some ways to make money:", false],
		[1, "", "Buy products at or near their home station and carry them towards their best market.", true],
		[1, "", "Fish and algae cost only time. Turning them into products can be worth more than selling them raw.", true],
		[1, "", "Do not dock at a colonist station with a valuable hold: everything aboard is taken at the floor price.", true],
		[1, "", "Salvage the cargo left by destroyed ships and sell it.", true],
		[1, "", "[[contract/13]] and [[contract/14]] contracts pay for delivering a product or a catch to the station that posted them.", true],
		[1, "", "A station's tech level also lowers its equipment and ship prices; see [[guide/fitting]].", false],
	],
	"home": [
		[1, "", "Welcome to the reference for DEEP, a submarine adventure on a colonised ocean world. Here you can look up the two hundred deep-sea stations, the ships and equipment you can buy, the creatures in the water, the goods traded between markets, the contracts on offer and the medals to win.", false],
		[1, "", "Story pages open as you play. Chapters, characters and later events stay hidden until your save reaches them, so you can browse without spoilers. Names and figures come from your own copy of the game; the overviews and guides are written for this reference.", false],
		[1, "", "New pilots should start with [[world/ocean]] and [[guide/basics]]. The [[chart]] shows every station and who holds it. The guides explain one part of life below the surface each, from [[guide/depth]] and [[guide/navigation]] to [[guide/trading]] and [[guide/combat]].", false],
	],
	"intro/characters": [
		[1, "", "These pages describe the people of the story: the pilot, the ship's computer, the allies and enemies met along the way, and the voices heard on the radio. Each biography follows the campaign in order and grows as you play, showing only what your pilot has already learned.", false],
		[1, "", "Some speakers have no names of their own. Role names such as [[character/6]], [[character/7]] and [[character/10]] stand for the many minor voices of each faction, and their pages explain who speaks under them. For the story as a whole, see [[world/history]].", false],
	],
	"intro/contracts": [
		[1, "", "The fifteen kinds of job a station can offer. Each page states the objective, which side posts it, any restrictions, and the story chapters built on the same kind of mission. See [[guide/contracts]] for deposits, pay and how difficulty scales with your rank. Contracts become available on the job board from [[chapter/9|chapter 9]].", false],
	],
	"intro/equipment": [
		[1, "", "All equipment that can be fitted to a ship: weapons, torpedoes, harpoons, shields, armour, cargo modules, engines, steering, radar, boosters and repair systems. Each page gives the item's figures, how rare it is in shops, whether more than one can be fitted, and its price at every tech level. See [[guide/fitting]] and [[guide/combat]].", false],
	],
	"intro/factions": [
		[1, "", "The powers of the ocean: the [[faction/colonists]], the [[faction/rebels]] and the [[faction/pirates]]. Each page explains how the faction affects play, such as trade, contracts and bounties, and lists its stations and story appearances up to your current chapter. See [[guide/allegiance]] for how stations change sides.", false],
	],
	"intro/goods": [
		[1, "", "Everything that can be carried in a hold: catches, products, imports and salvage. Each page shows the good's tech level, home station, best market and price range, its recipe or what it is used in, and any special rules. See [[guide/trading]] for how prices work and how to profit.", false],
	],
	"intro/guide": [
		[1, "", "Practical guides to playing DEEP. Start with [[guide/basics]], then read about [[guide/depth]], [[guide/navigation]], [[guide/fishing]], [[guide/trading]], [[guide/combat]], [[guide/fitting]], [[guide/contracts]], [[guide/allegiance]] and [[guide/medals]]. Each guide explains one part of life below the surface in plain terms, with the figures the game itself uses, and links to the stations, equipment and medals it mentions. Story details stay hidden until your save reaches them.", false],
	],
	"intro/medals": [
		[1, "", "The medals a pilot can win. Each page describes what the medal rewards and, for graded medals, what is needed for bronze, silver and gold. See [[guide/medals]] for when medals are checked and which ones judge a single trip. Medals are checked each time you dock, and a full set brings a reward of its own.", false],
	],
	"intro/ships": [
		[1, "", "Every submarine hull in the game, from the starting [[ship/0]] to the [[ship/10]]. Each page lists hull strength, cargo capacity, equipment slots, steering and list price, when shipyards begin to offer it, and a comparison table of all hulls. See [[guide/fitting]] for buying a ship and trading in your old one.", false],
	],
	"intro/species": [
		[1, "", "The creatures and algae of the ocean. Each page gives the species' mass, toughness, how long it fights on the harpoon, its price range and the stations where it lives, along with the products and contracts that use it. See [[guide/fishing]] for how to catch them.", false],
	],
	"intro/stations": [
		[1, "", "The two hundred stations of the ocean, in alphabetical order. Each page gives the station's tech level, depth and chart position, which side holds it, the depth equipment needed to reach it, the species found nearby, the goods it is home or best market to, its part in the story and its nearest neighbours. The [[chart]] shows them all at once.", false],
	],
	"intro/story": [
		[1, "", "The story of the campaign runs over 48 chapters, from the pilot's first catch onwards. For one continuous account of the plot, organised into acts, read [[world/history]]. It grows as you play and never goes beyond the chapter your save has reached.", false],
		[1, "", "Each chapter also has its own page, with a synopsis of the mission and, once you have moved on, its outcome. A chapter's ending appears only when you reach the next chapter, so these pages never reveal more than you have already played.", false],
	],
	"intro/world": [
		[1, "", "Background on the world of DEEP. [[world/ocean]] describes the setting itself: its depths and pressure, its light and its wildlife. [[world/colony]] explains how the stations are run, how trade and contracts work and who keeps order. [[world/stream]] covers the network of gates that links the stations, and [[world/history]] tells the story as far as you have played.", false],
		[1, "", "These pages grow as your save advances. Later events, new characters and the secrets of the deep appear only once you have reached them, and further articles open as the story reveals the subjects they cover.", false],
	],
	"medal/0": [
		[1, "", "Every pilot receives this medal on arrival on the planet. It needs no effort at all and simply marks the start of the career.", false],
	],
	"medal/1": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for docking with very little hull left. Bronze needs less than 30% hull, silver less than 15% and gold less than 5%. The hull counts at the moment of docking.", false],
	],
	"medal/10": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for landing many catches in a single outing, between undocking and docking. Bronze needs more than 5, silver more than 10 and gold more than 15. A large hold helps.", false],
	],
	"medal/11": [
		[1, "", "Recognises total time spent playing the current career. Bronze comes after more than five hours, silver after seven and gold after ten.", false],
	],
	"medal/12": [
		[1, "", "Counts contracts from station job boards that the pilot has completed. Bronze needs more than 10, silver more than 25 and gold more than 50.", false],
	],
	"medal/13": [
		[1, "", "Counts journeys made through the S.T.R.E.A.M. network between stations. Bronze needs more than 50, silver more than 150 and gold more than 300.", false],
	],
	"medal/14": [
		[1, "", "Counts pirates destroyed over the career. Bronze needs more than 25, silver more than 150 and gold more than 350.", false],
	],
	"medal/15": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for completing an outing with no guns or torpedoes fitted. Harpoons do not count as weapons. The tutorial chapters do not qualify.", false],
	],
	"medal/16": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for docking with many guns and torpedoes fitted at once. Bronze needs at least 3, silver at least 5 and gold at least 8, which requires a hull with plenty of slots.", false],
	],
	"medal/17": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for completing the main story. It cannot be earned any other way, so it is usually one of the last medals a pilot collects.", false],
	],
	"medal/18": [
		[1, "", "Counts cargo crates recovered with the harpoon from destroyed ships. Bronze needs more than 25, silver more than 150 and gold more than 350.", false],
	],
	"medal/19": [
		[1, "", "Recognises the largest credit balance reached. Bronze needs more than 125,000, silver more than 500,000 and gold more than 1,000,000.", false],
	],
	"medal/2": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for catching at least one of each of the five algae species. Algae stand still on the seabed, so this is one of the easier awards.", false],
	],
	"medal/20": [
		[1, "", "Counts new pieces of equipment bought in shops. Bronze needs more than 5, silver more than 10 and gold more than 20.", false],
	],
	"medal/21": [
		[1, "", "Recognises the shallowest depth reached. Bronze needs a climb above 15,000 m, silver above 12,000 m and gold above 10,000 m. The two higher tiers lie beyond the rated limit of any shield.", false],
	],
	"medal/22": [
		[1, "", "Recognises the deepest dive. Bronze needs 30,000 m, silver 32,000 m and gold 35,000 m. The two higher tiers lie beyond the rated limit of any armour.", false],
	],
	"medal/23": [
		[1, "", "Awarded once every other medal has been earned, in any tier. Holding it lets the pilot buy [[ship/10]] at a fraction of its list price.", false],
	],
	"medal/3": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for catching at least one of every swimming creature species. Each station's waters hold a different mix of species, so this means fishing in many places.", false],
	],
	"medal/4": [
		[1, "", "Counts all enemies destroyed over the career. Bronze needs more than 50, silver more than 200 and gold more than 400.", false],
	],
	"medal/5": [
		[1, "", "Counts every creature or alga harpooned over the career. Bronze needs more than 50 catches, silver more than 150 and gold more than 300.", false],
	],
	"medal/6": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for docking at many different stations. Bronze needs more than 50, silver more than 100 and gold more than 200, which means practically every station on the chart.", false],
	],
	"medal/7": [
		[1, "", "Awarded once the pilot has manufactured every product at least once. Some recipes need imports or other products, so the full set takes planning.", false],
	],
	"medal/8": [
		[1, "", "Counts all goods manufactured in station factories. Bronze needs more than 50 units, silver more than 250 and gold more than 500.", false],
	],
	"medal/9": [
		[1, "", "Awarded for destroying many enemies in a single outing, between leaving one station and docking at the next. Bronze needs more than 5, silver more than 10 and gold more than 15.", false],
	],
	"ship/0": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/0]] is the hull every pilot starts with, and the cheapest on the list at 45,000 cr. It is also the weakest in almost every respect: 75 hull points, a 25 t hold and six equipment slots. Its steering figure of 1.10 is average, the same as that of the far larger [[ship/8]].", false],
		[1, "", "It comes fitted with [[equipment/15]], [[equipment/18]], [[equipment/22]] and [[equipment/35]]. That leaves only two of the six slots free for weapons or a cargo module.", false],
		[1, "In service", "The small hold is the real limit. A few medium catches fill it, and an average [[species/12]] weighs more than it can carry. For 30,000 cr more, [[ship/1]] offers 40 extra hull points, nearly twice the cargo and much sharper steering with the same slot count, so most pilots replace the starter boat as soon as their savings allow. Until then it is a training hull: good enough for the first fishing trips and light pirate fights close to home, but not for long trade runs or heavy combat.", false],
	],
	"ship/1": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/1]] is a sturdy, affordable fishing boat. Compared with [[ship/0]] it gains 40 hull points, 20 t of cargo and much quicker handling: its steering figure of 1.61 is second only to that of [[ship/5]]. It keeps the same six slots, however, and that ceiling defines the hull. A harpoon, shield, armour and radar leave room for just two guns or cargo modules.", false],
		[1, "In service", "Ordinary yards sell it from the first chapter at 75,000 cr, which makes it the natural first upgrade for a pilot who mostly fishes and runs short errands. It even outclasses the dearer [[ship/2]] in hull, cargo and steering, giving up only one slot. Once missions call for more firepower or a larger hold, the seventh and eighth slots of later hulls matter more than raw toughness, and the Okeanos is usually traded in.", false],
	],
	"ship/10": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/10]] is the luxury flagship and the best all-round hull. It combines 200 hull points, a 100 t hold, ten slots and steering of 1.50. Only [[ship/6]] beats it for hull or cargo, and only [[ship/5]], [[ship/1]] and [[ship/3]] turn faster. Its list price of 5,000,000 cr is more than fourteen times that of [[ship/9]].", false],
		[1, "In service", "Ordinary yards offer it from [[chapter/40]]. Few pilots can pay the list price. A pilot who holds every medal, completing [[medal/23]], may instead buy it for 50,000 cr, before the usual reduction for the station's tech level. That makes the Aquarius a reward for completing the medal collection rather than a practical purchase. By the time it becomes available, much of the campaign already lies behind the pilot.", false],
	],
	"ship/2": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/2]] is the cheapest hull with seven equipment slots, at 100,000 cr. Its hull and cargo figures (100 points and 40 t) are actually lower than those of [[ship/1]], and its steering of 1.40 is good rather than outstanding. What the extra money buys is the seventh slot, which can take a second gun or a cargo module.", false],
		[1, "In service", "It is on sale from the first chapter, so a pilot who saves early can move to seven slots well before anything better appears. From [[chapter/5]] on, though, [[ship/3]] offers the same hull and slot count with 6 t more cargo and better steering for only 5,000 cr extra. After that point the Triops is mainly worth buying when it happens to be on offer nearby and the alternative is not. It suits a pilot who wants to fight pirates early, since the spare slot can hold a second weapon.", false],
	],
	"ship/3": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/3]] refines the seven-slot layout of [[ship/2]]. For a premium of just 5,000 cr it adds 6 t of hold space and raises steering from 1.40 to 1.57, making it one of the most agile hulls in the game. Its 100 hull points are unremarkable, so shields and armour do most of the protecting.", false],
		[1, "In service", "Ordinary yards begin to stock it from [[chapter/5]]. It suits a pilot who wants to fight and fish from the same boat without a large outlay, and it remains a sensible mount until eight-slot hulls arrive. Compared with [[ship/4]], the next hull to appear, it gives up 75 hull points and 24 t of cargo in return for far quicker turns and a price that is 25,000 cr lower.", false],
	],
	"ship/4": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/4]] is a heavy freighter in miniature. It brings 175 hull points, a 70 t hold and eight slots for 130,000 cr, which makes it the cheapest eight-slot hull. The cost is handling: its steering figure of 0.91 is lower than that of any earlier ship, and only [[ship/6]] turns more slowly.", false],
		[1, "In service", "It appears in ordinary yards from [[chapter/10]]. Traders and fishers gain the most from it. Its large base hold also makes cargo modules more effective, because their bonus is a percentage of base capacity. A steering module offsets some of the sluggishness. Pilots who mainly fight may prefer [[ship/5]], which costs 20,000 cr more and turns exactly twice as fast, but has barely half the hull strength.", false],
	],
	"ship/5": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/5]] is a dogfighter. Its steering figure of 1.82 is the best of any hull, which lets it stay behind an opponent and keep a target in its sights. Its protection is thin, though: at 90 points the hull is weaker than that of [[ship/1]], and the 40 t hold is modest. Eight slots leave plenty of room for weapons.", false],
		[1, "In service", "Ordinary yards stock it from [[chapter/15]] at 150,000 cr. It rewards pilots who can avoid hits, ideally backed by a strong shield and armour, and it is a poor choice for hauling goods. Because all fitted guns fire together, spare slots turn directly into firepower. A pilot who wants a sturdier eight-slot hull should compare it with [[ship/4]] or wait for [[ship/6]].", false],
	],
	"ship/6": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/6]] is the great bulk carrier. Its 180 t hold is far beyond any other hull, nearly twice that of [[ship/10]], and its 220 hull points are the highest in the game. At 170,000 cr it is also cheaper than [[ship/7]] and every later hull. The drawback is handling: with a steering figure of 0.67, it turns more slowly than any other ship.", false],
		[1, "In service", "It enters ordinary yards from [[chapter/20]]. For trading and large fishing trips it has no rival. Cargo modules work from base capacity, so a single +80% module adds 144 t. In combat it relies on absorbing damage rather than avoiding it, and a steering module is close to essential against nimble pirates. With only eight slots, it also has less room for guns than the hulls that follow.", false],
	],
	"ship/7": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/7]] is the first hull with nine slots. Its other figures are middling: 135 hull points, a 50 t hold and steering of 1.23, which beats [[ship/0]] but falls well short of [[ship/5]]. At 215,000 cr it costs considerably more than [[ship/6]], so the price mostly pays for the extra slot.", false],
		[1, "In service", "It appears in ordinary yards from [[chapter/25]]. It suits a pilot who wants one more gun or module than an eight-slot hull allows, without paying for the ten-slot ships that follow. As an all-rounder it is sound but not outstanding in any single area. [[ship/8]], five chapters later, adds a tenth slot, 15 more hull points and 16 t more cargo for another 85,000 cr.", false],
	],
	"ship/8": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/8]] is the first ten-slot hull. It carries 150 hull points and a 66 t hold, and steers like [[ship/0]] with a figure of 1.10. At 300,000 cr it costs 85,000 cr more than [[ship/7]] for one more slot and modest gains in hull and cargo.", false],
		[1, "In service", "Ordinary yards list it from [[chapter/30]]. With ten slots a pilot can fit a full defensive set, a harpoon and a radar, and still mount several guns or a cargo module. Its handling is only average, so a steering module helps in close fights. [[ship/9]], which arrives five chapters later, gives up 6 t of hold for 15 more hull points and better steering. The Pontos remains the cheaper of the two and the better choice when cargo matters.", false],
	],
	"ship/9": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[ship/9]] is a high-end ten-slot hull. Compared with [[ship/8]] it adds 15 hull points and noticeably sharper steering (1.36 against 1.10), but loses 6 t of cargo. At 350,000 cr it costs 50,000 cr more.", false],
		[1, "In service", "Ordinary yards stock it from [[chapter/35]]. It is best suited to a combat-minded pilot late in the campaign, who gains more from ten slots and good handling than from hold space. For bulk trading, [[ship/6]] carries three times as much for half the price. The only hull that surpasses the Lir in most respects is [[ship/10]], whose list price is in another league.", false],
	],
	"species/0": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/0]] is modelled on the nautiluses, shelled cephalopods of the tropical Indo-Pacific. A nautilus lives in the outer chamber of a coiled shell and controls its buoyancy by adjusting gas and fluid in the inner chambers. It swims by jet propulsion and catches food with dozens of tentacles that lack suckers. Nautiluses usually stay a few hundred metres down, and the shell can implode if they go much deeper than about 800 m.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it weighs about 8 t and offers almost no resistance on the harpoon: the line takes only 2.1 s to subdue it. That makes it one of the easiest catches, but its price of 44 to 156 cr per tonne is low. It lives in the waters of 67 stations.", false],
		[7, "", "In [[chapter/7]] the pilot must clear these animals from the waters around [[station/1]], either by catching them or by killing them.", false],
	],
	"species/1": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/1]] takes its name from Eurypharynx pelecanoides, a deep-sea fish found from roughly 500 to 3,000 m. Its loosely hinged mouth is huge compared with its slender, whip-like body, and it can open wide enough to engulf prey much larger than expected. The tail ends in a small light organ. Adults usually measure well under a metre.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is a fast swimmer and fights the harpoon for about 4 s, twice as long as a [[species/0]] of the same 8 t mass. The effort pays: at 70 to 255 cr per tonne it is one of the better-priced catches. Factories use it for [[goods/24]] and [[goods/27]], both valuable products. A harpoon with strong slowing makes it much easier to land.", false],
	],
	"species/10": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/10]] draws on the squids and octopuses, cephalopods famous for changing colour in a fraction of a second. Their skin holds thousands of pigment cells called chromatophores, each opened or closed by tiny muscles, along with reflective cells that add shimmer. Many also carry an ink sac; cuttlefish ink was once the source of the brown pigment sepia.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it weighs about 14 t, has a toughness of 28 and struggles on the line for about 3.4 s. Raw prices run from 59 to 164 cr per tonne. Combined with solvent it yields [[goods/22]], which is needed for [[goods/29]].", false],
		[19, "", "In [[chapter/19]] the pilot must protect a school of them near [[station/7]] from attacking ships.", false],
	],
	"species/11": [
		[1, "Overview", "\"Loricate\" means covered in protective plates, and [[species/11]] recalls the sea turtles. Seven species live today. Their shells are made of bony plates fused to the spine and ribs. The leatherback is the largest, with a shell up to about 2 m long, and it can dive more than 1,000 m in search of jellyfish. Females return to beaches to lay their eggs.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it weighs about 8 t and gives up after about 2.7 s on the harpoon. Its price is unusually variable, from 40 to 244 cr per tonne, so it pays to sell it where demand is high. No factory recipe uses it.", false],
	],
	"species/12": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/12]] is based on the toothed whales, the group that includes sperm whales, dolphins and porpoises. They find prey by echolocation, sending out clicks and listening for the echoes. The sperm whale is the largest toothed predator on Earth. It dives to depths of 1,000 m or more to hunt squid and can stay under for over an hour.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is the heaviest creature, at around 30 t, with a toughness of 60. A single catch can fill a small hold, and an average specimen will not fit in the 25 t hold of [[ship/0]]. It fights for only about 2.9 s. Prices range widely, from 22 to 200 cr per tonne, and no recipe uses it.", false],
	],
	"species/13": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/13]] recalls the golden algae (Chrysophyceae) and related golden-brown groups. Their colour comes from the pigment fucoxanthin, which masks their chlorophyll. Most are microscopic and live in fresh water, and some form blooms; one species, Prymnesium parvum, releases toxins that can kill fish.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game, like every alga, it is rooted to the seabed, weighs a fixed 2 t and is subdued in 2.1 s. It is the most valuable of the five algae, selling for 27 to 65 cr per tonne. It is used in [[goods/27]] and [[goods/28]].", false],
	],
	"species/14": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/14]] echoes the blue-green algae, or cyanobacteria. Strictly they are not algae but photosynthetic bacteria. They are among the oldest life forms known and helped fill Earth's atmosphere with oxygen. Some fix nitrogen from the air, and some can release hydrogen, which is why researchers study them as a source of clean fuel.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it grows on the seabed, weighs 2 t and offers little resistance. It sells for 21 to 47 cr per tonne. Fittingly, it is combined with [[goods/18]] to make [[goods/19]], and it is one of the five algae needed for [[goods/28]].", false],
	],
	"species/15": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/15]] represents the brown algae (Phaeophyceae), which include the kelps. Giant kelp can grow tens of metres tall and forms underwater forests that shelter many other species. Brown algae are a commercial source of alginates, used to thicken foods and in medicine.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is the cheapest catch of all, at 14 to 47 cr per tonne. Like the other algae it sits still on the seabed, weighs 2 t and is subdued in 2.1 s. Its only use is in [[goods/28]], for which every one of the five algae is required.", false],
	],
	"species/16": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/16]] refers to the red algae (Rhodophyta). A red pigment, phycoerythrin, lets them absorb the blue light that penetrates furthest into the sea, so they grow deeper than any other seaweed. Red algae give us agar and carrageenan, and the nori used to wrap sushi is a red alga.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is the second most valuable alga, worth 27 to 62 cr per tonne. It stands on the seabed, weighs 2 t and is easy to harpoon. It goes into [[goods/27]] alongside [[species/13]] and is also needed for [[goods/28]].", false],
	],
	"species/17": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/17]] stands for the green algae, the closest relatives of land plants. Sea lettuce (Ulva) is a familiar coastal example. Some green seaweeds are eaten; in Japan, dried flakes called aonori are sprinkled on food. Green algae need plenty of light, so most grow in shallow water.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is a low-value catch at 20 to 44 cr per tonne. Like the other algae it is rooted to the bottom, weighs 2 t and is subdued in 2.1 s. Factories combine it with [[species/4]] to make [[goods/21]], and it is one of the five algae in [[goods/28]].", false],
	],
	"species/2": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/2]] represents the medusae, the free-swimming stage of many cnidarians. A jellyfish has no brain, bones or blood, and its bell is mostly water. It moves by pulsing the bell and drifts with the current, catching small prey with tentacles armed with stinging cells. Some species glow, and several deep-sea forms are red or violet.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "At about 2 t, this is one of the lightest creatures in the game, and its toughness of 4 means a single stray shot can kill it. On the harpoon it gives up after about 2.5 s. Prices run from 40 to 133 cr per tonne. Its only use in manufacturing is as one of three ingredients of [[goods/26]].", false],
	],
	"species/3": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/3]] is based on the manta rays, the largest rays in the world. The bigger oceanic species can span about 7 m from wingtip to wingtip. Mantas are gentle filter feeders: they swim with their mouths open and use two fleshy lobes in front of the mouth to steer plankton inside. They have one of the largest brains relative to body size of any fish.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it weighs around 16 t and has a toughness of 32, so it survives glancing fire better than smaller animals. It struggles on the line for about 3.6 s. Raw prices of 65 to 182 cr per tonne are moderate, but it is the creature needed for [[goods/20]], which in turn goes into [[goods/29]].", false],
	],
	"species/4": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/4]] stands for the dense schools formed by small fish such as sardines, anchovies and herring. Schooling confuses predators, which struggle to pick out a single target, and it may also save energy as the fish swim in each other's wake. These schools are the base of many marine food chains and of the world's largest fisheries.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game a swarm is caught as one 4 t load. It is an undemanding catch: the harpoon subdues it in about 2.5 s, and its toughness is only 8. Prices range from 53 to 138 cr per tonne. It is an ingredient of [[goods/21]] and [[goods/26]], so a pilot who also catches [[species/17]] or [[species/2]] has a ready supply chain.", false],
	],
	"species/5": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/5]] refers to the anglerfishes, and in particular the deep-sea species. A modified spine on the head forms a rod with a lure at its tip. In many deep-sea forms the lure glows with light produced by symbiotic bacteria, drawing prey close to a wide mouth. In some species the tiny males fuse permanently to the much larger females.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it weighs about 10 t and gives up on the harpoon after about 2.5 s, so it is a fairly quick catch. Its price spread of 53 to 216 cr per tonne is wide. Together with [[species/1]] and solvent, it is needed for [[goods/24]], a key input for several high-value products.", false],
	],
	"species/6": [
		[1, "Overview", "\"Devilfish\" is an old sailors' name. Over the centuries it has been applied to the devil rays of the genus Mobula, to large octopuses and even to the grey whale. The game does not tie [[species/6]] to any one of these animals. What they share is size and a fearsome reputation that rarely matches their actual behaviour.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is a heavy catch at around 20 t, with a toughness of 40. It fights the harpoon for about 4.2 s, among the longest of any creature. It also commands the highest ceiling price of any creature, up to 364 cr per tonne. Factories pair it with [[species/8]] and salt to make [[goods/25]].", false],
	],
	"species/7": [
		[1, "Overview", "[[species/7]] is based on the marlins, large billfish of warm open oceans. A marlin uses its spear-like bill to slash through schools of prey and is among the fastest fish in the sea. The largest blue marlins exceed 4 m in length and several hundred kilograms in weight. Marlins are prized by sport anglers for their long, acrobatic fights.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game it is light at about 8 t but hard to land: it swims fast and resists the harpoon for about 4.2 s. A harpoon with long range and strong slowing makes the job easier. In return it holds its value well, selling for 104 to 283 cr per tonne, the highest floor price of any creature. It is the main ingredient of [[goods/23]].", false],
	],
	"species/8": [
		[1, "Overview", "\"Bone shark\" is an old name for the basking shark, the second-largest living fish after the whale shark. Basking sharks can reach about 8 m or more. Despite their size they are harmless filter feeders, cruising slowly near the surface with their huge mouths open to strain plankton. They migrate long distances and often spend winter in deeper water.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game [[species/8]] weighs about 18 t and has a toughness of 36. It fights the harpoon for about 3.8 s. Prices run from 78 to 208 cr per tonne. It is one of the more useful catches for manufacturing, since it goes into both [[goods/25]] and [[goods/26]].", false],
	],
	"species/9": [
		[1, "Overview", "No real animal is called a goliath shrimp, but true giants exist. The giant tiger prawn can grow to about 30 cm, and some deep-sea crustaceans are larger than their shallow relatives, a pattern known as deep-sea gigantism. Many deep-sea shrimps are red, which makes them almost invisible in water where red light does not reach.", false],
		[1, "In the game", "In the game [[species/9]] is a small 2 t catch with a toughness of 4, quickly subdued in about 2.3 s. Prices range from 39 to 122 cr per tonne. No factory recipe uses it, so it is caught purely for sale. Fishing contracts ask for it more often than for rarer creatures such as [[species/0]].", false],
	],
	"world/abel": [
		[31, "", "[[world/abel]] is a scientist who works with the [[faction/rebels|resistance]]. He never appears on screen, but his research is the heart of [[character/2]]'s plan to build a weapon that can turn the war against the colony.", false],
		[31, "Background", "The professor has spent many years researching the [[world/biotek]], a power source that no one had managed to build. He was close to finishing a working prototype when the colonists stole it, and they are now trying to complete it themselves.", false],
		[31, "Role in the story", "[[character/2]] brings him and his team to [[station/14]] to build the rebels' new weapon. They are evidently the scientists whose convoy the pilot protected on the way there ([[chapter/29]]). [[character/2]] leaves at once to meet him while the pilot and others recover the generator ([[chapter/30]]).", false],
		[33, "", "After the raid the professor takes the recovered containers to his laboratory to continue work on the prototype ([[chapter/32]]).", false],
		[39, "", "His work progresses quickly. Soon the prototype is almost complete and only one part is missing: a [[goods/36]] that multiplies the DNA of algae particles to start the chain reaction that produces power. [[character/2]] passes on his explanation while admitting she does not fully understand it. The device can only be made at [[station/16]] ([[chapter/38]]).", false],
		[42, "", "When the incubator reaches [[station/14]], he has everything ready to fit it, and the finished weapon is expected in the hangar shortly. As the generator is still a prototype, the pilot is warned to be careful with it ([[chapter/41]]).", false],
		[44, "", "The [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]] powered by his generator proves decisive when the colonists attack [[station/14]].", false],
		[31, "Character", "Everything known about him comes from [[character/2]] and [[character/5]]. He is described as a dedicated researcher.", false],
		[39, "", "His explanations are technical enough that even [[character/2]] struggles to repeat them.", false],
	],
	"world/aquarians": [
		[10, "", "The Aquarians are beings of the deep ocean, said to be part human and part fish. Aquarians is the colonists' name for them, and the colony regards them as a pest. They are known chiefly for attacking the transport capsules that carry the colony's exports to orbit. Who they are, and what they want, is one of the questions the story slowly answers.", false],
		[10, "Nature", "Little is known about the Aquarians at first. They live in the open water and move like sea animals rather than machines, yet they fight with weapons of their own. The colonists treat them as dangerous animals and never ask why they attack.", false],
		[11, "", "After the pilot's first fight with them, [[character/1]] wonders whether they are far more intelligent than anyone has assumed. The pilot also starts to ask why they target the capsules at all.", false],
		[19, "", "Further encounters suggest they care for the ocean's wildlife. They are seen swimming alongside a school of [[species/10|colour squid]] that colonists are trying to capture, as if guarding it.", false],
		[45, "", "They are intelligent and capable of diplomacy. An Aquarian envoy comes to [[station/14]] and seems to understand some human speech.", false],
		[10, "Relations with the colony", "The capsule launches are the colony's link to the wider economy, and the Aquarians strike at them repeatedly. Every damaged or lost capsule reflects badly on the station in the eyes of the trade administration, so the colonists hire armed pilots to drive the attackers away. Capsule defence is also a regular colonist contract; see [[contract/11]].", false],
		[10, "", "In [[chapter/10]] the pilot is sent to [[station/2]] to defend a capsule launch, with orders from [[character/9]] to tolerate no more than a handful of losses.", false],
		[11, "", "Nothing suggests the colony has ever tried to talk to the Aquarians. It simply pays to have them driven off or killed.", false],
		[13, "Relations with the rebels", "The [[faction/rebels]] speak of the planet's inhabitants as well as its resources when they condemn the colony's exploitation. Perhaps the Aquarians are among those they mean, but for a long time there is no contact between the two.", false],
		[19, "", "The rebels and the Aquarians share an interest in protecting the ocean's animals. When the pilot shields the squid near [[station/7]], [[character/1]] suggests the Aquarians could become allies.", false],
		[44, "", "The turning point comes during the colonist attack on [[station/14]] in [[chapter/43]]. With the station's docks hit and the defenders outnumbered, a group of Aquarians arrives and attacks the colonists, turning the battle. [[character/9]] and his forces are routed.", false],
		[45, "", "After the battle the envoy at Fiir explains that the Aquarians are willing to fight the colonists' rule. [[character/2]] hopes that once the colony is defeated, people and Aquarians can live in harmony with the ocean. With their help the rebels plan two final offensives.", false],
		[45, "", "In [[chapter/45]] a group of Aquarians rises from the depths to join the pilot and [[character/12]]'s squad in the assault on [[station/18]].", false],
		[47, "", "Both offensives succeed, and the rebel commanders credit the Aquarians with much of the victory.", false],
		[48, "", "With the colony gone, the alliance between the rebels and the Aquarians stands at the end of the story, together with a promise to let the ocean recover.", false],
		[45, "Open questions", "Much about the Aquarians is never explained. The story does not say where they live, how many there are, how they make or use their weapons, or how they came to understand human speech. It also leaves open why they singled out the colony's capsules, though their later alliance with the rebels suggests that they saw the exports as part of the plundering of their ocean.", false],
		[10, "In the game", "In combat the Aquarians appear as fish-like fighters that fire bolts of energy. They turn more slowly than submarines and never use bursts of speed. A destroyed Aquarian dies like a sea creature, in a single burst, and leaves no wreck to salvage and nothing to catch.", false],
		[10, "", "On the colony's side, Aquarians are the enemy in capsule defence missions. They make straight for the capsules, so the defender has to hunt them down quickly. The mission fails if too many capsules are lost. Destroying Aquarians counts as a kill, but not towards the pirate bounty.", false],
		[44, "", "In the last chapters they fight on the pilot's side as allies. They appear as friendly contacts on the radar and attack the colonist ships.", false],
	],
	"world/biotek": [
		[31, "", "The Biotek generator is an experimental power source developed by [[world/abel|Professor Abel]]. It is the key to the rebels' plan to outgun the colonists. The rebels intend it to power a weapon too energy-hungry for any ordinary submarine, and recovering it is the first of three steps in that plan. The generator itself is never sold or fitted as equipment; in play, the pilot handles the pieces it needs.", false],
		[31, "Research and theft", "The technology behind the generator has been studied for years without anyone producing a working prototype. Professor Abel was close to finishing one when the colonists stole it. They are now trying to complete it themselves, which the rebels are determined to prevent.", false],
		[31, "", "Shortly before revealing the plan, [[character/2]] arranged for a group of highly skilled scientists to travel to [[station/14]]. In [[chapter/29]] the pilot escorted their convoy safely through the border region near [[station/13]].", false],
		[31, "", "At Fiir, Ayumi explains the purpose of the scientists: they are Abel's team. Together they will turn the generator into the heart of a new weapon.", false],
		[31, "The three-step plan", "Ayumi sets out the plan in three steps. First, the rebels must recover the generator. Second, they need a weapon powerful enough to be worth such a generator. She does not yet explain the third step.", false],
		[31, "", "According to one of Ayumi's informants, the stolen generator is being moved in an ordinary colonist convoy, bound for a place called Maarth. Because nobody knows which ship carries it, the pilot, [[character/5]] and [[character/4]] must stop the entire convoy near [[station/2]] and salvage every container.", false],
		[32, "", "The raid succeeds and every container is recovered, although the colonist crews protest that they are only carrying food and medicine.", false],
		[33, "", "Back at Fiir, Abel takes the captured cargo to his laboratory to finish the prototype. Meanwhile the pilot sets out to find the weapon for step two, the [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]].", false],
		[39, "How it works", "The generator draws its power from living material. A polymerase incubator copies the DNA of algae particles, and this copying sets off a chain reaction that releases energy. The prototype still needs that incubator, the [[goods/36]], before it can run.", false],
		[39, "", "Only one station has the equipment to build it: [[station/16]], the most advanced in the ocean. The pilot's third step is to go there and produce the incubator.", false],
		[42, "", "With the incubator delivered to Fiir, Abel completes the installation and the generator powers the Eclipse on the pilot's own ship. The rebels caution that it is still a prototype.", false],
		[44, "", "It holds up in battle. When the colonists attack Fiir, the Eclipse, driven by the generator, helps turn the fight. [[character/9]] is visibly shaken to find the gun on a submarine.", false],
		[45, "", "Once [[station/188]] is in rebel hands, the rebels set about arming as many units as they can with the same pairing of gun and power source.", false],
		[39, "In the game", "Step three spans three chapters: travelling to Wagoll in [[chapter/39]], producing the incubator there in [[chapter/40]] and delivering it to Fiir in [[chapter/41]]. Its goods page lists a price range, but no market ever stocks it, so the only way to obtain one is to build it.", false],
		[39, "", "The incubator is made in the factory of a station of technology level 11, which in practice means Wagoll. Its recipe takes one each of [[goods/19]], [[goods/34]], [[goods/28]] and [[goods/24]]. These in turn come from dead fish and algae, from imports, from all five kinds of algae, and from [[goods/35]] with [[species/5]] and [[species/1]]. Gathering them is a small production chain of its own.", false],
		[39, "", "Wagoll lies at 29,850 m, deeper than most armour can go, so the pilot needs heavy armour such as the [[equipment/25]] to reach it safely. The incubator is a story item that no market stocks. Once made, it stays aboard until it is delivered to Fiir.", false],
	],
	"world/colony": [
		[1, "", "The colony is the human settlement of the ocean planet: two hundred deep-sea stations run by a colonial administration, together with the settlers, fishers and traders who live and work in them. At the start of the story every station belongs to the [[faction/colonists]], and the colony's purpose is plain. It exists to take the ocean's resources and ship them away. This article covers how the colony is organised, how its economy works and how both change as the story unfolds.", false],
		[1, "Colonisation", "Settlers came to the planet from elsewhere and built their homes beneath the surface, where the stations now stand at depths between about 13,500 and 32,250 m. The stations differ widely. Each has a technology level from 1 to 11, which decides what its factory can produce and how much its shops charge, and each has its own waters, wildlife and neighbours. Some are major hubs such as [[station/0]]; others are small outposts known for a single product.", false],
		[1, "", "Newcomers are expected to pull their weight. The pilot comes to the planet to fish, with a small boat and little money, and quickly finds that the work is hard and badly paid.", false],
		[10, "", "The colony is an export economy. Its stations load their goods into transport capsules and launch them up to orbit, where cargo vessels collect them. Beyond the local officials stands a trade administration that judges the stations by how reliably these shipments arrive.", false],
		[12, "", "The pilot's own plan is simply to earn enough for a ticket off the planet.", false],
		[21, "", "The rebels describe the colonisation as rapid and ruthless. In their view it is draining the ocean faster than nature can recover, as has already happened on other planets.", false],
		[1, "Administration and stations", "Each station is a small town with its own officials and services. A docked pilot can visit the hangar to fit equipment, the shop and shipyard to buy gear and hulls, and the map. Most stations also have a job board, and some have a market and factory. Patrol ships circle each station between its S.T.R.E.A.M. gates and fight for whichever side holds it.", false],
		[2, "", "Colonial officials speak for the colony in day-to-day matters. At [[station/0]], [[character/9]] buys the pilot's catch and hands out advice nobody asked for.", false],
		[7, "", "Station security deals with local threats, and officials pass such jobs to freelancers. When nautiluses cluster around [[station/1]], its security staff want them gone, and Jack sends the pilot to see to it.", false],
		[9, "", "Officials expect newcomers to earn trust before they receive important work. The pilot must first complete contracts from the job board before the colony offers anything bigger. The anonymous [[character/6]] officials the pilot meets are generally curt and pay as little as they can.", false],
		[1, "Fixed prices", "Colonist stations do not trade. There is no market and no factory: when a ship docks, the station simply takes everything in its hold and pays the floor price for each item, the lowest it could ever fetch. A delivery that the station itself ordered is settled first; everything else goes at the fixed rate. For a fisher with nowhere else to sell, this is the whole economy.", false],
		[1, "", "This keeps the colony's fishers poor. The floor price is often less than half of what the same catch could fetch elsewhere, and there is no other buyer.", false],
		[1, "Markets and prices", "Free markets exist only at stations outside colonial control. There a good's price depends on distance. Every good has a home station, where it sells at its floor price, and a best market, where it reaches its ceiling. In between, the price rises with the chart distance from home. Three imports, [[goods/33]], [[goods/34]] and [[goods/35]], run the other way and are cheapest at their best market. A market buys and sells at the same price, so profit comes from carrying goods, not from bargaining. See [[guide/trading]].", false],
		[1, "", "A station's technology level also shapes its shops. Ships and equipment are cheaper at advanced stations: each technology level takes one percent off a hull's price, and equipment also costs a little less. Rarer equipment turns up more often as the story advances.", false],
		[1, "Factories and recipes", "Factories turn raw catch and imports into products. Each product has a fixed recipe and a minimum technology level. [[goods/21]] takes a [[species/4]] and [[species/17|green algae]]; [[goods/22]] takes a [[species/10]] and [[goods/35]]; [[goods/29]] combines [[goods/20]] and paint. Advanced products such as [[goods/41]] need other products as ingredients, so a chain of production can turn cheap fish into valuable goods.", false],
		[1, "", "A factory can make anything up to its station's technology level. Most products need only level 1, but at least one item can be built only at the most advanced station of all. Each product page lists its recipe.", false],
		[1, "The job board", "Contract work is the colony's way of hiring freelancers. A job board lists between one and eight offers, each with a sponsor, a deposit due on acceptance and a fee paid on success. The fee rises with difficulty and distance, and difficulty rises with the pilot's rank. Most jobs send the pilot to another station, usually one within the ship's depth band. See [[guide/contracts]].", false],
		[1, "", "What a board offers depends on who holds the station. Colonists hire convoy escorts, convoy raiders, hunters and guards for their capsule launches. Free stations post delivery and fishing orders and pay to protect schools of animals. Both sides post bounties, rescues, pirate hunts, passenger runs and clean-up work.", false],
		[9, "", "The job board opens to the pilot only after the first jobs for the colony, in [[chapter/9]]. The station map becomes available a little earlier.", false],
		[1, "Law and order", "The colony keeps its own law in the water. Stations pay a bounty for every pirate destroyed on a trip, collected on docking. The sum is the number of pirates squared, times 100 credits at a colonist station and 50 at a free one, so a long hunt pays far more than several short ones.", false],
		[12, "", "Not all waters belong to the colony. Stations held by the resistance treat the water around them as neutral territory and challenge unknown ships before letting them dock.", false],
		[35, "", "Some trade is illegal. Intoxicants are banned on at least some stations, which leaves room for black-market dealers such as [[character/8]]. The security authorities hire bounty hunters like [[character/11]] to remove such people.", false],
		[12, "The colony in the story", "For the pilot the colony is first an employer and then an opponent. Jack pays fixed prices, the nautilus cull at [[station/1]] pays badly, and the capsule defence at [[station/2]] ends with Jack refusing any reward at all. Fed up, the pilot follows M.A.I.'s advice and goes looking for the resistance at [[station/3]].", false],
		[13, "", "The resistance opposes the colony's control of trade and its exploitation of the planet. Its members want free trade, fair prices and a sea that is fished with care.", false],
		[18, "", "The rebels first win stations by diplomacy, persuading their leaders to change sides. Every station that joins opens a market and factory to the pilot, so the colony's economy changes station by station. Talk of a revolution spreads.", false],
		[23, "", "The colony does not give up its stations quietly. Jack, now a figure of some influence, lays a minefield to cut a rebel supply route near [[station/9]].", false],
		[25, "", "Then Jack ambushes the rebel leader on her way to negotiations at [[station/10]].", false],
		[28, "", "The colony also buys traitors. Jack places a bounty on the rebel leader and the pilot, and the rebel pilot [[character/3]] takes the money.", false],
		[31, "", "The colony is also racing the rebels for technology. Its people stole [[world/abel|Professor Abel]]'s prototype [[world/biotek]] and are trying to finish it themselves.", false],
		[37, "", "Greed weakens the colony from within. Jack himself sells a station gun, the [[world/eclipse|Eclipse]], to a black-market dealer, who sells it on to the pilot.", false],
		[44, "", "In a last show of strength the colonists strike deep into rebel territory and attack [[station/14]], only to be driven off when the Aquarians join the fight against them.", false],
		[45, "", "After that defeat the rebels move to finish the colony with two great offensives, one in each hemisphere.", false],
		[47, "", "The southern stations fall, and the colonists are left with [[station/0]] as their last major stronghold, heavily fortified.", false],
		[48, "", "Gosu falls in the final battle. Jack escapes in a rescue capsule, and colonial rule over the ocean comes to an end, with every station in rebel hands.", false],
	],
	"world/eclipse": [
		[33, "", "The Eclipse is a heavy gun built for the defence of stations. It is so power-hungry that it was never meant to be mounted on a submarine. In the second step of the rebels' plan, the pilot must obtain one so that it can be paired with the [[world/biotek]], which can supply enough energy to fire it from a ship. The weapon is the [[equipment/42]] in the equipment list, and the crated gun traded in the story is [[goods/38]].", false],
		[33, "Origins", "The Eclipse belongs to a class of station guns designed for fixed defences. Its energy demand is the reason it has stayed on stations: no submarine's power plant could keep it firing. The pilot already knows of it as a stationary weapon, which is why the rebels' plan to carry one on a ship sounds so unlikely.", false],
		[33, "", "Eclipses are manufactured at [[station/188]], which is in colonist hands when the plan begins. The nearest suitable station under rebel control is [[station/15]]. The weapon is not for sale on the open market at all.", false],
		[33, "", "The rebels need it because they are outnumbered. Rather than match the colonists ship for ship, they hope that a weapon of this power, carried by a submarine, can tip the balance.", false],
		[33, "Acquiring the gun", "[[character/2]] has no clear plan for getting one. She gives the pilot 100,000 credits for whatever bribes prove necessary and sends them to Choral to look around.", false],
		[35, "", "At Choral the pilot is approached by the black-market dealer [[character/8]], who claims he can get almost anything for a price. He will try to find an Eclipse, but only if the pilot first kills the bounty hunter [[character/11]], who has been hired to get rid of him.", false],
		[37, "", "Once Raoul is dead, Fenko produces an Eclipse at a steep price. He bought it from [[character/9]], who apparently sold the gun for profit without knowing it would end up with his enemies.", false],
		[38, "", "The pilot buys the weapon and carries it back to [[station/14]].", false],
		[39, "", "[[character/2]] chooses not to ask how the gun was obtained. She is relieved to hear that no innocent people died for it. When the pilot jokes that the plan amounts to subjugating the whole planet, she rejects the idea firmly.", false],
		[42, "The Eclipse in service", "After the [[goods/36]] arrives at Fiir, [[world/abel|Professor Abel]] links the gun to the prototype generator, and the Eclipse is ready to fit to the pilot's own submarine. The rebels warn that the power source is still only a prototype. The pilot's orders are to test the weapon on a pirate gang near [[station/17]].", false],
		[43, "", "The test goes well enough that the pirates call for reinforcements. Straight afterwards the gun faces its real test, when the colonists attack Fiir; Jack can hardly believe the pilot is carrying an Eclipse.", false],
		[45, "", "After the defence of Fiir the rebels take [[station/188]] and begin fitting as many of their own ships with the Eclipse as they can, ahead of the final offensives.", false],
		[47, "", "In the battles for [[station/18]] and [[station/109]] the gun proves devastating, and [[character/5]] is full of praise for it.", false],
		[37, "In the game", "In the chapters around [[chapter/37]] the crated Eclipse is on sale at Choral as a story good. It costs far more than any ordinary trade good and is never stocked anywhere else.", false],
		[42, "", "In [[chapter/42]] the [[equipment/42]] appears in the equipment shop at [[station/14]] and costs nothing to fit. Until it is installed, the job board and map are closed and the ship cannot leave. Only one Eclipse can be fitted to a ship, and it cannot be sold until the war is won.", false],
		[42, "", "As a weapon it outclasses everything on sale. Each shot hits harder than any other gun in the game, at a range of about 240 m, and it fires about once a second. It fires with the rest of the gun bank, so it combines with any other weapons fitted.", false],
	],
	"world/history": [
		[1, "", "The campaign follows a young submarine pilot who has come to a distant ocean planet to work as a fisherman for its human [[world/colony|colony]]. The colonists live in stations scattered through the depths of [[world/ocean|the ocean]], and the colony depends on what its pilots catch, trade and carry between them. The pilot owns a small, modestly equipped submarine and shares it with [[character/1]], the ship's on-board computer, which is never short of advice or opinions. The player chooses the pilot's name; this reference simply calls the protagonist the pilot.", false],
		[1, "", "The story is told over 48 chapters. It begins with the routine of a working fisherman: catching sea life, selling the haul and taking odd jobs to make ends meet. From there it widens into a conflict over how the planet's sea and its creatures should be treated, and over who gets to decide. Along the way the pilot deals with station officials, traders, [[faction/pirates]] and stranger inhabitants of the deep, and the choices of one fisherman come to matter far more than anyone expects.", false],
		[1, "Act I: A fisherman for the colony", "The story opens out at sea in [[chapter/1]]. The pilot is already regretting the move to the planet, but [[character/1]] points out that they are here to fish, and the first task is simply to harpoon a creature and get it into the hold. The opening scenes double as an introduction to catching sea life.", false],
		[2, "", "Once the first creature is landed, [[character/1]] shows how the autopilot can take over the steering, and in [[chapter/2]] the pilot sets course for [[station/0]], the colonist station where the catch is sold.", false],
		[3, "", "At [[station/0]] the pilot deals with [[character/9]], the colony official who buys the catch. [[character/9]] pays his usual fixed price, mocks the small cargo hold and lectures the newcomer about everyone doing their share for the colony. He also sneers that a pilot with a proper gun might earn more by hunting [[faction/pirates]]. Once he has gone, [[character/1]] calls him a fool but takes the hint, noting that the [[equipment/0]] is on offer in the hangar. In [[chapter/3]] the pilot buys the gun and fits it.", false],
		[4, "", "Armed for the first time, the pilot leaves the station in [[chapter/4]] to try out the new weapon, with [[character/1]] warning against shooting harmless fish. The local waters turn out to be free of pirates, so the hunt has to move elsewhere.", false],
		[5, "", "[[character/1]] explains how long journeys work: ships cross great distances through the [[world/stream|S.T.R.E.A.M. network]], always leaving a region by its exit point and arriving at the entry point of the next. In [[chapter/5]] a lone pirate appears near [[station/1]]. After a quick lesson in switching weapons, using autofire and reading the radar, the pilot fights for the first time.", false],
		[6, "", "The pirate is destroyed, and [[character/1]] shows how to harpoon the cargo crates that wrecks leave behind before they sink out of reach. With a working weapon and a little salvage, the pilot returns to [[station/0]] in [[chapter/6]].", false],
		[7, "", "Back at the station, [[character/9]] is pleased that his advice was taken and offers what he calls a real job. [[species/0|Nautiluses]] are crowding the convoy routes around [[station/1]], and the station's security wants them gone. The pilot objects that the animals only graze on the growth covering the station hulls, and that no small hold could carry them all, but [[character/9]] does not care how they disappear. In [[chapter/7]] the pilot clears them out, by harpoon or by gun. Neither the pilot nor [[character/1]] is happy about the work, since the creatures are few and harmless.", false],
		[8, "", "The cull leaves the pilot shaken. The pilot came to the planet to fish, not to kill animals for the convenience of a station, and [[character/1]] can only hope that the pay makes up for it. The two dock at [[station/1]] to collect in [[chapter/8]].", false],
		[9, "", "The colonist official there ([[character/6]]) treats the pilot as a hired pest controller and admits the job is probably pointless, since the nautiluses keep coming back. The payment is a flat 1,000 $, offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis. Any further work depends on proof of ability: three completed contracts from the station job boards. [[chapter/9]] sends the pilot out as a freelancer, taking whatever escorts, salvage runs and fishing orders lie within reach.", false],
		[10, "", "The three jobs earn the official's grudging approval and a special assignment. Strange beings that the colonists call [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]], part human and part fish, have been attacking the transport capsules that launch the colony's goods into orbit, where cargo ships collect them. Lost or damaged capsules make the station look bad in the eyes of the [[world/colony|trade administration]], so the pilot is sent to [[station/2]] to drive the attackers off. [[character/9]] will be there to lead the next launch.", false],
		[10, "", "In [[chapter/10]] the launch is already under way when the pilot arrives, and the Aquarians are indeed striking at the capsules. [[character/9]] radios a blunt warning: the pilot's job depends on losing no more than five of them.", false],
		[11, "", "The attack is beaten off, but it leaves questions. The pilot wonders why the Aquarians target the capsules at all, and [[character/1]] suggests that they may be far more intelligent than the colonists believe. Increasingly uneasy about this kind of work, the pilot docks at [[station/2]] to report in [[chapter/11]].", false],
		[12, "Act II: The resistance", "[[character/9]]'s welcome ends the pilot's patience with the colony. He blames the pilot for the damaged capsules, cancels the reward and storms off, muttering that he is destined for better things. [[character/1]] then says what both have been feeling: the pilot works all day for the colony, earns very little and has no say over the catch, which is simply taken at a fixed price. The pilot only wants to save enough for a ticket off the planet, but [[character/1]] has another idea.", false],
		[12, "", "While scanning the radio bands, [[character/1]] has picked up signs of a resistance movement transmitting from [[station/3]]. The choice is left to the pilot, who knows it could mean serious trouble and goes anyway. From now on [[character/1]] marks the movement's stations in green on the map (see [[faction/rebels]]).", false],
		[12, "", "In [[chapter/12]] a discreet approach to [[station/3]] fails at once. The station's guards challenge the ship and threaten to open fire, and the pilot stammers out a name, says the journey began at [[station/0]] and asks to join. After a long silence the ship is cleared to dock.", false],
		[13, "", "Inside, [[character/4]] questions the newcomer with a string of tangled questions. The pilot admits to knowing almost nothing beyond the movement's support for free trade. That fits with [[character/4]]'s own admission that the rebels began encrypting their radio traffic only days ago. Short of helpers, [[character/4]] accepts the pilot on trial. The movement intends to infiltrate colonist stations, persuade their leaders to change sides and end the exploitation of the planet and its inhabitants.", false],
		[13, "", "[[character/4]] will not share plans with a stranger, so the pilot must first prove useful. In [[chapter/13]] the task is to collect sympathisers at [[station/4]] and bring them to [[station/5]]. On the way the pilot admits to [[character/1]] that the whole affair is a mystery, but agrees that it beats taking orders from [[character/9]].", false],
		[14, "", "The contact at [[station/4]] is a single passenger, [[character/5]]. She is warm and quick, plainly pleased to meet the pilot, and the pilot is just as plainly taken with her. She will not explain the movement's plans, saying that secrecy is the reason it has survived this long, and sends the coordinates for the trip. [[character/1]] watches the two of them with open irritation, which the pilot teases as jealousy. In [[chapter/14]] the pilot carries [[character/5]] to [[station/5]] as quickly and safely as possible.", false],
		[15, "", "At [[station/5]], [[character/5]] goes into hiding to settle some affairs, but first she explains why the rebels keep their distance from the colonists. They do not strip the sea of its resources. They take what it can spare, turn the catch into goods and take care not to overfish, so that its variety of life survives. To show how this works, she asks the pilot to produce five tons of goods in the factory of a resistance station ([[chapter/15]]). She suggests [[station/90]], where squid can be caught and [[goods/35]] bought to make [[goods/22]].", false],
		[16, "", "Once the pilot has learned this second way of earning money, [[character/5]] calls with a new task. The movement needs current reports from every station to find weak points and win the population over, because persuasion will matter as much as firepower. When the pilot asks whether this means a revolution, she refuses to say more. In [[chapter/16]] the pilot tours stations not visited before, earning money for ship upgrades along the way.", false],
		[17, "", "A long message from [[character/4]], which [[character/1]] sums up in a single line, calls the pilot back to [[station/3]] in [[chapter/17]]. The rebel area is growing meanwhile: [[station/6]], [[station/12]], [[station/106]] and [[station/180]] have come over to the movement.", false],
		[18, "Act III: The leader", "[[character/4]] explains that diplomacy has quietly widened the rebel area, so the pilot can now trade at many more stations. The news channels already speak of a revolution. Many decision makers have joined without a shot being fired, although [[character/4]] expects bloodshed sooner or later. The next steps are directed by the movement's leader from one of the newly won stations, and the pilot must find this leader, or rather be found. [[character/4]] marks the candidates and warns that some may be out of reach without better armour or shields.", false],
		[18, "", "[[character/1]] is unimpressed by how the movement is organised, and [[chapter/18]] becomes a search with little to go on except luck.", false],
		[19, "", "The trail ends at [[station/6]], where the pilot meets [[character/2]], an older woman who leads the movement and will give the orders from now on. The pilot had assumed the leader was a man, a mistake she shrugs off. Her first assignment is an escort. A swarm of [[species/10]] must be guided to safe waters near [[station/7]] before colonists can catch or kill the animals, and a fight is likely. [[character/1]] grumbles that nobody has mentioned payment, while the pilot reasons that [[character/2]] first wants to know whether the newcomer can be trusted.", false],
		[19, "", "In [[chapter/19]] the pilot finds Aquarians swimming alongside the swarm, and [[character/1]] wonders whether they look after the creatures of the sea. Colonist ships soon close in, and the pilot has to intercept them without hitting the animals.", false],
		[20, "", "The swarm survives. [[character/2]] pays a modest 2,000 $ and calls the pilot back at once, while [[character/1]] complains that selling the animals would have paid far better. In [[chapter/20]] the pilot returns to [[station/6]].", false],
		[21, "", "This time the pilot asks outright what the movement is about. [[character/2]] describes a group of people united against the ruin of the planet and its inhabitants. Colonisation is moving so fast, and nature is exploited so ruthlessly, that the sea's plants and animals will soon be unable to recover. If nothing changes, the planet will end up exhausted like many worlds before it. The movement means to prevent this by winning important decision makers to its side, and the pilot pledges to help.", false],
		[21, "", "[[character/2]]'s immediate concern is the [[faction/pirates]], who are using the conflict to prey on easy targets. Until now the movement has only defended itself; now it will attack a pirate formation directly. To steady the nervous pilot she adds an escort, [[character/3]], one of her best pilots, though [[character/1]] suspects that the pilot is really the one doing the escorting. In [[chapter/21]] the two meet near [[station/8]]. [[character/3]] greets the late newcomer with impatience and condescension, and tells the pilot to stay behind him and learn.", false],
		[22, "", "The gang is wiped out. [[character/3]] offers grudging praise, but when [[character/2]] rewards each of them with 5,000 $, he is furious that the money is split equally and tells the pilot that the newcomer contributed nothing. The pilot and [[character/1]] leave before his temper gets worse. In [[chapter/22]] they travel to [[station/9]] to meet [[character/2]], whose talks there have succeeded: the station has joined the movement along with [[station/5]], [[station/37]] and several others.", false],
		[23, "", "Before [[character/2]] moves on, the supply route to [[station/37]] must be secured. [[character/9]] has laid a minefield just outside the station to cut it. [[character/2]] knows him: once a nobody, he has climbed quickly thanks to well-placed friends. Eager to spoil his plans, the pilot sets out to clear the field in [[chapter/23]]. [[character/2]] warns that pirates will be circling nearby, and that each mine can only be destroyed once the pilot has come close enough to trigger its motion sensor.", false],
		[24, "", "The field is cleared, the route is safe again, and [[character/2]] pays 4,000 $ for the work. Back at [[station/9]] in [[chapter/24]], the pilot finds the rebel area much larger. A wave of stations across the ocean now stands with the movement, among them [[station/14]], [[station/22]] and [[station/25]].", false],
		[25, "", "At the station the pilot is reunited with [[character/5]], and their conversation is warm but awkward, with [[character/1]] offering unwanted coaching. [[character/2]] cuts the reunion short. A round of negotiations at [[station/10]] could bring more stations over, and the pilot and [[character/5]] are to escort her and [[character/3]] there. The pilot is not keen on going out with [[character/3]], but [[character/2]] vouches for his skill and expects nothing worse than pirates.", false],
		[25, "", "In [[chapter/25]] the party reaches [[station/10]] and senses at once that something is wrong. [[character/9]] is waiting with colonist forces and orders them to attack, and [[character/2]] cannot understand how he knew they were coming.", false],
		[26, "Act IV: Betrayal", "During the fight [[character/3]] holds his fire, claims that his weapons have failed and flees, apologising as he goes. The rest of the party survives. [[character/2]] wonders about sabotage, while [[character/5]] openly accuses [[character/3]] of treachery; [[character/2]] defends him, saying he has always been loyal to her. The pilot admits that the episode looked suspicious, but urges everyone to leave before [[character/9]] can bring in more ships.", false],
		[26, "", "The group scatters. [[character/2]] goes into hiding, and [[character/5]] keeps the movement's business running at [[station/9]]. The pilot is told to become a far better fighter before they meet again, since it is now clear that diplomacy alone may not win the struggle. In [[chapter/26]] the pilot hunts pirates and other enemies to raise the kill count.", false],
		[27, "", "Once the pilot has become more dangerous, [[character/3]] calls without warning. He asks after [[character/2]] and [[character/5]], claims to have gathered vital information while he was away, and asks for a meeting near [[station/11]]. [[character/1]] is certain it is a trap, and the pilot agrees, then goes anyway ([[chapter/27]]).", false],
		[28, "", "The trap is real. [[character/3]] is holding [[character/2]] prisoner and demands the pilot's surrender, while [[character/2]] urges the pilot to ignore her. On [[character/1]]'s advice the pilot keeps [[character/3]] talking while the computer scans his ship. [[character/3]] freely admits that he changed sides for money: [[character/2]] paid poorly, and [[character/9]] has offered a large bounty for both her and the pilot. When the pilot appeals to the movement's ideals, [[character/3]] replies that ideals do not put food on the table.", false],
		[28, "", "The scan shows a single-seat ship, so [[character/2]] must be locked in its heavily armoured cargo bay. The pilot opens fire. [[character/3]] proves fast and dangerous, but his ship is destroyed, and the pilot harpoons the cargo container before it sinks too deep. In [[chapter/28]] the pilot carries it to [[station/12]], the nearest safe station, hoping that [[character/1]] has guessed its contents correctly.", false],
		[29, "", "[[character/1]] was right. [[character/2]] emerges shaken but unhurt and thanks the pilot for saving her life. There is no time to rest: before her abduction she was organising the transport of a team of highly skilled scientists to [[station/14]], and [[character/3]] may already have passed on the details. She will not yet explain why scientists matter, and sends the pilot to see their convoy safely across the border zone ([[chapter/29]]). Near [[station/13]] the pilot finds the convoy already under heavy attack and calling for help.", false],
		[30, "", "The convoy gets through, and its crews credit the pilot with their survival. [[character/2]] pays 5,000 $ and calls a meeting at [[station/14]], where she promises to explain what comes next ([[chapter/30]]).", false],
		[31, "Act V: Three steps", "At [[station/14]], [[character/5]], [[character/4]] and [[character/2]] are waiting, and [[character/2]] finally lets the pilot in on her plan. Clashes with the colonists are growing more frequent and the rebels are still outnumbered, so she has brought [[world/abel|Professor Abel]] and his team to the station to build a weapon that can turn the balance. The project has three steps. The first is a power source, [[world/biotek|the Biotek generator]], a technology studied for years without ever yielding a working prototype. Abel was close to finishing one when the colonists stole it, and they are now trying to complete it themselves.", false],
		[31, "", "[[character/2]] leaves to meet Abel, and [[character/5]] takes over the briefing. An informant reports that the generator is being shipped towards Maarth on ordinary cargo vessels to avoid attention, which gives the rebels a chance to strike near [[station/2]]. Nobody knows which ship carries it, so the whole convoy must be stopped and every container recovered, like it or not. In [[chapter/31]] the pilot, [[character/5]] and [[character/4]] intercept the convoy. Its crews plead that they carry only food and medicine, and [[character/4]] answers that the rebels badly need both.", false],
		[32, "", "Every container is recovered, and in [[chapter/32]] the team brings the haul back to [[station/14]].", false],
		[33, "", "Abel takes the cargo to his laboratory to work on the prototype, and [[character/2]] hands out new duties. [[character/5]] is to patrol outside the station against surprise attacks, [[character/4]] is needed in the hangar, and the pilot gets the second step. The generator needs a weapon worthy of its output, and [[character/2]] has chosen the [[equipment/42]], a [[world/eclipse|stationary defence gun]] whose enormous power demands have so far kept it off every ship.", false],
		[33, "", "The nearest station that manufactures such guns, [[station/188]], is in colonist hands, so [[character/2]] sends the pilot to the rebel station [[station/15]] instead. The weapon is not sold anywhere, and that is where her plan ends. She simply hands over 100,000 $ for whatever bribes may be needed. The pilot calls it the vaguest plan ever heard and accepts it gladly, and [[chapter/33]] takes the pilot to the station to look around.", false],
		[34, "", "With no contacts and no real plan, the pilot starts by browsing the local trader's wares. In [[chapter/34]] the trail leads to a cargo of [[goods/37]], a drug that is illegal on the station.", false],
		[35, "", "The deal attracts [[character/8]], a black-market dealer who talks about himself in the third person and claims he can supply almost anything. Asked for the [[equipment/42]], he is astonished and wonders whether the pilot means to start a war. Still, he agrees to try in return for a favour: the death of [[character/11]], a bounty hunter hired by the security administration to get rid of [[character/8]]. In [[chapter/35]] the pilot finds [[character/11]] patrolling near the station with an escort of his own. He brushes the pilot off as a mere fisherman.", false],
		[36, "", "As the fight turns against him, [[character/11]] tries to talk his way out, but his ship is destroyed. The pilot returns to [[station/15]] in [[chapter/36]], hoping the dealer will keep his word.", false],
		[37, "", "[[character/8]] delivers. He has tracked down an [[equipment/42]] and will sell it, at a price inflated by the bribes he claims to have paid. Asked how he came by such a cannon, he names the seller: [[character/9]]. [[character/1]] relishes the irony that [[character/9]]'s greed is arming his worst enemies. In [[chapter/37]] the pilot buys the gun and gets it aboard.", false],
		[38, "", "With the cannon in the hold, the second step is complete, and in [[chapter/38]] the pilot returns to [[station/14]] to see how the prototype is coming along.", false],
		[39, "", "[[character/2]] would rather not know how the gun was obtained and only hopes no blood was spilled; the pilot's careful answer is that no innocent people died. The prototype is nearly finished, but it lacks one small device that cannot be made at the station: a [[goods/36]], which copies the genetic material of algae particles and so sets off the reaction that powers the generator. [[character/2]] admits she is only repeating Abel's explanation. When the pilot wryly calls the device the third step towards subjugating the whole planet, she sharply rejects the idea.", false],
		[39, "", "Only [[station/16]] has the equipment to build the incubator, so [[chapter/39]] sends the pilot there for the third step. [[character/1]] complains that the pilot seems to be running the entire rebellion alone.", false],
		[40, "", "At the station it becomes clear that building the incubator will be anything but simple, and [[chapter/40]] turns into a demanding production job.", false],
		[41, "", "The incubator is finished, to [[character/1]]'s open surprise. Still not sure that all this is the right thing to do, the pilot carries it to [[station/14]] in [[chapter/41]].", false],
		[42, "", "[[character/5]] greets the pilot and the incubator with delight, while [[character/1]] announces that it is switching itself off rather than listen. Abel has everything ready, and the [[equipment/42]] should soon be available in the hangar. [[character/2]] wants the gun tested against pirates at once, and [[character/5]] passes on the coordinates with a warning that the generator is still only a prototype. [[character/1]], supposedly in standby, makes it clear that it heard every word.", false],
		[42, "", "In [[chapter/42]] the pilot fits the cannon and attacks a pirate hideout near [[station/17]]. The pirates soon realise what they are facing and call for reinforcements.", false],
		[43, "Act VI: War for the ocean", "The test is cut short by a distress call from [[station/14]], broadcast openly on an unencrypted channel. The pilot races back in [[chapter/43]] to find the station under a surprise colonist attack. Many docks have been hit, too few rebel ships can launch, and [[character/2]] believes the end has come. [[character/9]] leads the assault and mocks the pilot for fishing on the rebels' behalf, until he learns that the [[equipment/42]] is about to fire.", false],
		[44, "", "In the middle of the battle the [[world/aquarians|Aquarians]] arrive and fall on the colonists, fighting on the rebels' side. The attack collapses, and [[character/9]] deserts once he sees that it is lost. [[character/2]] calls the rescue a miracle and asks the pilot to dock, promising a special guest ([[chapter/44]]).", false],
		[45, "", "[[character/2]] admits the raid caught the rebels unprepared, as nobody expected the colonists to strike so deep inside rebel waters, and that the defence was not theirs alone. The guest is an Aquarian delegate who understands a little of the human language. Through him the Aquarians offer to help end the colonists' dominance. Once every station is taken and the colonist fleet is beaten, everyone is to live in harmony with nature.", false],
		[45, "", "With Aquarian help, [[character/2]] plans two great offensives to take the remaining stations in the northern and southern hemispheres. [[station/188]] has fallen, and the rebels are fitting the [[equipment/42]] to as many ships as they can. To exploit the surprise while it lasts, she puts the pilot in charge of the assault on [[station/18]] and sends [[character/5]] against [[station/109]]. When the pilot worries about [[character/5]], [[character/2]] teases the pilot's obvious affection and points out that [[character/5]] is a skilled pilot herself.", false],
		[45, "", "In [[chapter/45]] the pilot arrives to find [[character/12]], leader of the Atmos squad, waiting for the order to attack, while a group of Aquarians rises from the deep. Every colonist ship must be destroyed, freighters included.", false],
		[46, "", "The defenders are wiped out. The pilot sends half of [[character/12]]'s squad to patrol around the station and docks with the rest in [[chapter/46]]. A huge wave of stations changes sides at the same time, from [[station/1]] and [[station/2]] to [[station/11]], [[station/18]] and [[station/109]].", false],
		[47, "", "[[character/5]] calls to report that her own assault has also succeeded and that the [[equipment/42]] has proved devastating; the pilot gives the Aquarians just as much credit. Every southern station is now in rebel hands. [[character/2]] has sent word of what may be the final task: [[station/0]], the last great stronghold of the colonists and the station where the pilot's story began. It is heavily fortified, and [[character/2]] is already on her way to lead the attack in person. [[character/5]] follows, delayed by pirates on her route, and in [[chapter/47]] the pilot joins them for the final battle.", false],
		[47, "", "[[character/9]] commands the defence and boasts that he will enjoy destroying the pilot's ship, which the pilot recognises as an echo of [[character/3]]'s last words. On learning who killed [[character/3]], [[character/9]] tries to unsettle the pilot by claiming to be the pilot's father, and [[character/3]] the pilot's brother. The pilot, who has no brother and remembers a father's face perfectly well, dismisses the lie at once.", false],
		[48, "", "The colonist fleet falls ship by ship until only [[character/9]] remains. He is fast and hard to hit, but on [[character/1]]'s advice the pilot dodges his attacks and strikes from behind. [[character/9]] escapes in a rescue capsule and, to [[character/5]]'s disbelief, appears to take the whole station with him. Before he goes he sneers that there is nothing worth having in the depths and that he never cared for fish anyway.", false],
		[48, "", "With [[character/9]] gone, the war is over, and in [[chapter/48]] [[station/0]] and the last holdouts join the movement. The pilot insists that the victory belongs to everyone. [[character/1]] demands its share of the credit and is promised a memory upgrade once the pilot is back on solid ground, which it indignantly declares it does not need. [[character/2]] thanks them all and admits that the planet's wounds will take a long time to heal, but she is confident they can manage that too. [[character/5]] invites the pilot to celebrate over [[goods/21]], and the pilot gladly accepts.", false],
		[48, "Themes", "The central conflict is about how the sea should be used. The colony treats it as a resource to be harvested and shipped into orbit: it buys the catch at fixed prices and culls wildlife that gets in the way. The movement argues for taking only what the sea can spare, protecting its variety of life and trading freely. The pilot's early jobs, the nautilus cull and the capsule defence, show the colonial way of doing things from the inside. The pilot's growing discomfort with them is what makes the turn to the resistance believable.", false],
		[48, "", "The Aquarians follow the same arc. They first appear as pests attacking the colony's capsules, and the pilot is paid to drive them away. Later they are seen watching over sea creatures, and at [[station/14]] they turn a lost battle. In hindsight their early raids look like resistance of their own against the plundering of the ocean, although the game leaves their motives largely to inference.", false],
		[48, "", "Money and loyalty run through the story. [[character/3]] betrays the movement for a bounty, and [[character/9]] sells the very gun that later breaks his attack on [[station/14]]. The pilot starts out wanting nothing more than the price of a ticket home and ends as one of the movement's commanders, while [[character/1]]'s complaints about poor pay become a running joke rather than a motive.", false],
		[48, "", "The movement wins most of its early ground through persuasion, and the ambush at [[station/10]] marks the point where persuasion stops being enough. The campaign then closes the circle at [[station/0]], the station where [[character/9]] once paid the pilot a fixed price for the catch. After the final chapter the pilot remains free to fish, trade and take contracts across an ocean that now stands with the rebels.", false],
	],
	"world/ocean": [
		[1, "", "The ocean is the setting of DEEP: a planet whose habitable world lies far beneath the waves. Its settlers live in pressurised stations thousands of metres down, and submarines are the only way to move between them. Everything the colony eats, sells and builds comes out of the water, from fish and algae to the goods made from them. This article describes the ocean as a place: its depths, its light, its wildlife and the way that life responds to the people who hunt it.", false],
		[1, "", "The pilot arrives here as a newcomer with a small fishing submarine and the on-board computer [[character/1]], and soon wishes they had stayed away. For the people who run the stations, see [[world/colony]]; for travel between them, see [[world/stream]].", false],
		[1, "Depth and pressure", "Depth is the ocean's defining feature. There is no sea floor to stop a dive: a submarine can keep sinking until the water crushes it. What keeps a ship alive is a band of safe depths set by its equipment. The shield decides how high it may rise and the armour how deep it may sink. A hull with neither is safe only in a narrow slice of water around 22,000 to 23,000 m.", false],
		[1, "", "Outside its band a ship is squeezed by the pressure. After a few seconds' grace the water begins to wear away its protection, first one layer and then the hull itself, and the damage grows the further the ship strays beyond 15,000 m above or 30,000 m below. Short excursions are survivable; long ones are not. The figures for each piece of equipment are on its own page, and [[guide/depth]] explains the rules in full.", false],
		[1, "Where the stations lie", "The two hundred stations of the [[chart]] are anchored at fixed depths, nearly all between 15,000 and 30,000 m. More than half of them, 109 in all, sit between 20,000 and 25,000 m, the band the starting [[ship/0]] can reach. About fifty lie shallower and about forty-five deeper. The outliers are [[station/28]], the highest at 13,500 m, and [[station/81]], the deepest at 32,250 m.", false],
		[1, "", "Depth does not follow geography. Shallow and deep stations sit side by side across the whole chart, so neighbours on the map can be thousands of metres apart vertically. [[station/14]], near the centre of the chart, lies within easy reach of both [[station/25]] at 17,700 m and [[station/81]] far below it. A pilot planning a route has to read every station's depth, not just its distance. [[station/0]], where the pilot's career begins, lies in the north-west at 22,500 m.", false],
		[1, "Light and water", "No daylight reaches the stations. Outside their floodlit hulls the water is dark, and a submarine travels by the cone of its own headlights, which the water swallows within a few dozen metres. Fine particles hang and drift in the beams, and in places faint patches of luminous plankton glow in the gloom. Climbing above 15,000 m, towards the distant surface, is recorded as an achievement of its own, [[medal/21]].", false],
		[1, "", "The water is not the same colour everywhere. Each region takes a faint tint from the plankton and algae that thrive there, strongest near the stations where those algae are most at home. Slow currents stir the suspended particles, but they are part of the scenery only: they never push a submarine off course or carry a catch away.", false],
		[1, "Sea life", "The ocean is home to thirteen kinds of animal and five kinds of alga. They range from small, easy catches such as [[species/2|jellyfish]], [[species/0|nautiluses]] and [[species/4|fish swarms]] to strong swimmers like the [[species/7]] and the [[species/8]]. Deeper-sea oddities include the [[species/5]], the [[species/1]], the [[species/6]], the [[species/9]] and the armoured [[species/11]]. The [[species/3]] and the colour-changing [[species/10]] are prized for leather and paint.", false],
		[1, "", "The largest animal is the [[species/12]], which can weigh around 30 t, more than the whole hold of a small submarine. Heavier and tougher animals fight longer on the harpoon and generally fetch more, but no species is worth the same everywhere. Each species page lists the stations whose waters it lives in and the goods made from it.", false],
		[1, "", "Every station has its own mix of wildlife, usually five or six species. About twenty animals swim in a station's waters at any one time, drawn from that local mix. A fisher soon learns which stations to visit for which catch, and the map can show where each species already caught is found. See [[guide/fishing]].", false],
		[1, "The sea bed and algae", "The five algae, [[species/17|green]], [[species/16|red]], [[species/15|brown]], [[species/14|blue]] and the valuable [[species/13|golden]], are the ocean's plants. They grow rooted in place rather than swimming, each a fixed 2 t, and they give up on the harpoon almost at once. Their value lies in what they become: [[goods/21]] needs green algae, [[goods/27]] golden and red, and [[goods/28]] a little of all five.", false],
		[7, "", "Algae also grow on the outer surfaces of the stations themselves, together with other small organisms. They are a favourite food of nautiluses, which is why those animals gather around stations in numbers.", false],
		[1, "How the living world responds", "Animals do not simply wait to be caught. A creature that is wounded, by gunfire or a stray shot, bolts and runs, and a hooked one thrashes until it tires. An animal killed outright is lost as a catch: its carcass floats upwards and can only be hauled in as [[goods/18]], worth a fraction of the living animal. Pilots who want a clean catch keep their guns well away from the fish.", false],
		[1, "", "In play the waters are never fished out. When the pilot moves on, the wildlife left behind is replaced, so a good fishing ground stays good. The contest over the sea's life appears instead on the job board, where some sponsors pay to have a species hunted down and others pay to have a school of animals protected from the hunters. See [[contract/7]] and [[contract/6]].", false],
		[4, "", "From the moment the pilot owns a gun, [[character/1]] warns against shooting the very fish they came to catch.", false],
		[7, "", "The colony treats wildlife that gets in its way as a nuisance to be removed. In [[chapter/7]] the pilot is sent to clear nautiluses from the waters of [[station/1]], where they are said to block the convoy routes.", false],
		[9, "", "The cull achieves little. The official who pays for it admits that the animals keep coming back however many are removed.", false],
		[15, "", "The [[faction/rebels]] see the sea differently. They harvest only what it can spare, process their catch into goods and take care not to overfish, in order to preserve the variety of life in the ocean.", false],
		[19, "", "In [[chapter/19]] the pilot guards a school of [[species/10|colour squid]] near [[station/7]] against colonist hunters. Aquarians swim alongside the animals, apparently looking after them; see [[world/aquarians]].", false],
		[21, "", "According to the rebel leader [[character/2]], the rapid colonisation is stripping the ocean faster than its plants and animals can recover. If it goes on, she warns, the planet will end up exhausted, like other worlds before it.", false],
		[48, "", "With the colonial administration gone at the end of the war, the rebels expect the ocean to need a long time to heal, but they mean to see it through.", false],
		[1, "Dangers of the deep", "Besides pressure, the ocean holds human dangers. [[faction/pirates]] prey on lone ships in open water, and in later chapters some lie in wait near the stations. Minefields, drifting debris and armed convoys appear in contracts and in the story. For who controls which part of the ocean, see [[world/colony]] and [[guide/allegiance]].", false],
	],
	"world/stream": [
		[1, "", "The S.T.R.E.A.M. network is the system of transit gates that binds the colony's stations together. Every station has a gate some distance outside it, and a submarine that enters one comes out almost at once at the gate of another station. Without the network, crossing the [[chart]] would mean long voyages through dark open water. With it, a single pilot can work across the whole ocean. The network is a central part of play as well as a fixture of the setting; for the controls, see [[guide/navigation]].", false],
		[1, "The gates", "Each station has its gate out in open water, away from the docks. Ships leave a station's area through its gate and arrive at the gate of their destination, from where they cruise in to dock. A gate is opened from close range: the pilot steers into its activation zone and opens the gate chart, which lists every station within reach.", false],
		[1, "", "The trip itself is nearly instant. The ship emerges at the far gate with its damage unchanged, so a battered submarine is still battered when it arrives. The destination's local wildlife, patrols and any waiting encounter are already in the water.", false],
		[1, "Range and engines", "A gate does not reach the whole ocean. From any station it covers roughly a sixth of the chart's width. A better engine extends the reach in proportion to its rating, so an engine rated +30% reaches about thirty percent further. Stations beyond reach need several hops, or a long cruise on autopilot, which is much slower even with time acceleration.", false],
		[1, "", "Open-water cruising remains useful for short trips. The autopilot travels from one station's area into the next, and time acceleration can run up to sixteen times normal speed while it does, dropping back when danger appears.", false],
		[1, "", "The network takes no account of depth. A gate will happily deliver a ship to a station that lies outside its safe depth band, after a warning, and the pressure starts to wear down its protection as soon as it arrives. Check a destination's depth on its station page before setting out. See [[guide/depth]].", false],
		[1, "Gates and encounters", "Gates are closed while a fight is under way. If a mission encounter is active in a station's waters, neither the gate nor the dock can be used until it is resolved. A pilot cannot simply escape an ambush through the network, and every contract must be fought to its end or failed.", false],
		[1, "", "Arriving through a gate is also when trouble starts. Entering a station's area triggers any encounter waiting there, from a wanted criminal to a convoy that must be protected. Patrol ships make their rounds past the gates, and in the later chapters pirates may be waiting near the station.", false],
		[1, "Name", "The network's name is always written as an abbreviation, S.T.R.E.A.M., but the game never says what the letters stand for. The pilot and [[character/1]] treat the gates as an everyday part of life, much like roads or railways.", false],
		[1, "Role in the colony", "The network is what holds the colony together. It turns two hundred scattered stations into one working economy: a catch can be sold far from where it was caught, and a contract can lead right across the chart. Each arrival at a new station counts towards [[medal/13]], and the first docking at each counts towards [[medal/6]].", false],
		[5, "In the story", "The pilot is introduced to the gates early on, when the first pirate hunt takes them beyond [[station/0]]. From then on almost every journey in the story passes through them, and the autopilot can take the ship to the local gate on request.", false],
		[12, "", "The gates do not respect political borders. When the pilot first travels to the resistance at [[station/3]], the ship arrives in waters the rebels call neutral and is challenged at once by the station's guards.", false],
		[21, "", "Rebel operations make use of the gates. On the mission against the pirate gang near [[station/8]], [[character/3]] waits for the pilot near the station's gate before leading the attack.", false],
		[45, "", "In the assault on [[station/18]], [[character/12]]'s squad waits in position near the gate, so that the attack can start the moment the pilot arrives.", false],
	],
}
